\begin{abstract}
This paper establishes a finite-aperture model for pattern-reconfigurable antennas (PRAs) and exploits its aperture-constrained Fourier representation to develop an efficient multiuser beamforming framework. First, we develop an ideal model by representing the complex radiation pattern using a Fourier series. By applying the Jacobi--Anger expansion, we relate the antenna aperture to the effective Fourier order and derive aperture-dependent power bounds for individual Fourier coefficients. Second, we employ a practical multiport network model and design three representative reconfigurable pixel antennas to verify the predicted effective Fourier orders and per-order power constraints. We then formulate weighted sum-rate maximization for multiuser downlink transmission under both models. Using bounded Fourier coefficients as auxiliary variables, we develop a coefficient-bound penalty-based weighted minimum mean-square error algorithm that separates continuous beamforming from discrete switch-state optimization. We further propose a one-bit neighborhood search that exploits the Sherman--Morrison identity to accelerate the discrete optimization. Simulation results validate the physical relevance of the ideal model and demonstrate multiuser sum-rate gains and reduced optimization runtime for densely switched antennas. The proposed model provides a physically grounded basis for PRA-enabled communication analysis and design, while the framework accommodates different beamforming objectives and PRA implementations.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
	Pattern-reconfigurable antennas, reconfigurable pixel antennas, antenna modeling,  multiuser communications, beamforming design.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Multi-antenna technology has long been a cornerstone of modern wireless communications~\cite{Zhu2026MovableReconfigurable}. From first-generation to fifth-generation wireless systems, antenna architectures have evolved from single-antenna systems to multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) and massive MIMO. More recently, this evolution has continued toward extremely large-scale MIMO~\cite{Chen2024XLMIMO}. By exploiting array, diversity, and spatial-multiplexing gains, these technologies have substantially improved transmission reliability and spectral efficiency. Nevertheless, these advances have been achieved primarily by increasing the number of antenna elements, while antenna configurations, including array geometries and antenna radiation characteristics, have remained largely fixed. In future wireless networks,  increasingly complex propagation environments and more demanding communication requirements will make it difficult for such fixed antenna configurations to fully meet system needs. Therefore, in addition to further increasing the array scale, it is also important to enhance antenna flexibility~\cite{Ying2024RmMIMOOverview,Wang2026FlexibleMIMO,Ning2025MAArchitectures}.
Reconfigurable antennas (RAs) have emerged as a promising approach to improving antenna flexibility by introducing controllable changes in antenna configurations, such as spatial position, orientation, and intrinsic radiation characteristics~\cite{Ma2026RASurvey,TWCLMZ,TWC25YSJ}. By dynamically controlling these configurations, RAs extend system adaptation from the signal domain to the antenna domain, allowing the antenna configuration to adapt to varying propagation conditions and communication requirements. Their considerable potential has attracted increasing attention from the wireless communications community, leading to extensive research on various forms of RAs~\cite{Zheng2026RotatableAntenna,Shao2025SixDMA,chen2025dbraa,TWCLMZ}. Among these, position reconfiguration and pattern reconfiguration represent two important reconfiguration mechanisms.
Position reconfiguration changes the positions at which the electromagnetic field is excited. By adjusting the antenna positions, position-dependent phase variations are introduced across the propagation paths, thereby changing their coherent superposition and reshaping the effective channel. When position reconfiguration is jointly optimized with baseband and radio frequency (RF) signal processing, the resulting effective channel variations provide additional degrees of freedom to improve transmission performance, suppress interference, and enhance estimation accuracy. Representative antenna technologies based on position reconfiguration include movable antennas (MA)~\cite{Zhu2024MAMultiuser}, fluid antenna systems (FAS)~\cite{Wong2021FAS}, and pinching-antenna systems (PASS)~\cite{Ding2025PinchingAntenna,chen2026hybrid}. MA physically repositions antennas within a prescribed region to obtain favorable channel conditions~\cite{Zhu2026MATutorial,chen2025remaa}. FAS realizes position flexibility by selecting antennas among densely distributed candidate ports within a compact region~\cite{New2025FASTutorial,Zhang2026FASISACURA,Zhang2026FASJADCE}. PASS creates radiating points at configurable locations along a dielectric waveguide~\cite{Liu2026PASSOverview}. Existing studies have investigated these technologies from multiple perspectives, including performance analysis, channel estimation, and joint position and beamforming optimization.
Pattern reconfiguration modifies the intrinsic complex angular response of an antenna by redistributing the surface currents over its radiating structure. Such redistribution is realized using tunable RF components, such as positive-intrinsic-negative (PIN) diodes and varactors~\cite{Christodoulou2012Reconfigurable,Liu2026RAArrays,TWCLMZ}. Research in this direction includes conventional pattern-reconfigurable antennas (PRAs) and implementations of the same pattern-reconfiguration principle developed within the FAS framework~\cite{Zhang2025PRAFAS}. Although these antenna technologies may employ different physical structures, their channel-adaptation capability is primarily derived from their controllable angular responses. An appropriate model of pattern reconfiguration is essential for communication-system analysis and design. Existing communication-oriented PRA models can be broadly divided into hardware-independent ideal models and hardware-aware practical models. Ideal models represent the radiation pattern using continuous functions or orthogonal basis expansions, such as spherical harmonics~\cite{Ying2025Reconfigurable,Zheng2026TriHybrid}. Practical models instead represent the radiation pattern using precharacterized pattern codebooks or physical models, such as multiport networks~\cite{Shen2026AntennaCoding}. Based on these models, existing studies have mainly investigated pattern selection or joint pattern and beamforming optimization.
In this paper, we focus on PRAs because their electronically controlled electromagnetic reconfigurability makes them well suited to rapid channel adaptation and large-scale integration. Despite the growing research on PRAs, three important issues remain unresolved. First, existing ideal models are often constructed from mathematical considerations and represent continuous pattern functions using orthogonal basis expansions. However, the physical constraints imposed by a finite antenna size  are not explicitly incorporated into these models. This disconnect obscures the physical interpretation of the optimized patterns and, more importantly, may admit patterns that cannot be physically realized, thereby greatly overestimating the performance gains offered by PRAs. Second, ideal and practical PRA models have largely been developed along separate research lines. Consequently, the physical validity of ideal models has not been systematically verified using realizable hardware, while the size- and hardware-induced limitations observed in practical models remain insufficiently explained from a theoretical perspective. Third, practical PRAs often employ numerous discrete RF switches, giving rise to a large set of antenna states. These discrete states are further coupled with the continuous digital beamformer, resulting in substantial design complexity. This difficulty motivates the development of low-complexity optimization methods.
To address these issues, this paper develops a finite-aperture ideal model for PRAs, establishes and validates the relationship between the ideal and practical PRA models, and develops a low-complexity hardware-aware algorithm for PRA-enabled multiuser communication systems. The main contributions are summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}
	\item We develop a finite-aperture ideal model for PRAs. First, we represent the continuous complex radiation pattern using a Fourier series. By applying the Jacobi-Anger expansion, we then establish an explicit relationship between the antenna aperture and the effective Fourier order and derive aperture-dependent power bounds for the individual Fourier coefficients. The resulting finite-dimensional model provides a physically grounded, implementation-independent capability envelope for PRAs confined to a prescribed aperture.

	\item We establish and validate the relationship between the ideal and practical PRA models. Specifically, we design three reconfigurable pixel antennas (RPAs) with different aperture sizes and pixel layouts, and construct multiport network models that map their discrete switch states to radiation patterns. After validating the multiport network models against full-wave electromagnetic simulations, we represent the practically realizable patterns in the same Fourier domain as the ideal model. The results show that the practical patterns exhibit the aperture-dependent effective Fourier orders and satisfy the per-order power constraints in the ideal model, while the specific pixel and switch configurations determine the realizable subset within the ideal capability envelope. These results validate the physical relevance of the ideal model and provide a theoretical interpretation of the practical PRA characteristics.

	\item We formulate the weighted sum-rate (WSR) maximization problem for PRA-enabled multiuser communications and develop a coefficient-bound penalty-based weighted minimum mean-square error (WMMSE) algorithm for the resulting mixed-binary problem. By introducing  auxiliary continuous Fourier-coefficient variables, the algorithm decomposes the mixed-binary design into continuous Fourier coefficient optimization and discrete switch-state optimization. For the discrete optimization, we propose a one-bit neighborhood search (OBNS) that evaluates all single-bit neighbors of the current state and exploits the Sherman-Morrison identity to update the inverse of the loaded multiport impedance matrix.
\end{itemize}

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{SysModel} develops the ideal and practical PRA models and validates their relationship. Section~\ref{sec:mb-system} presents the system model and problem formulations. Section~\ref{sec:mb-algorithm} develops the proposed optimization algorithm and analyzes its convergence as  well as computational complexity. Section~\ref{SR} presents the simulation results, and Section~\ref{CON} concludes the paper.
