\section{Modeling of PRAs}\label{SysModel}
In this section, we investigate the modeling of PRAs. We first introduce the operating principle of PRAs and discuss their relationship with position reconfiguration, as commonly considered in MA and FAS. Then, we develop a  finite-aperture ideal model to characterize the reconfigurability of PRAs based on a Fourier series and the Jacobi-Anger expansion. Moreover, we present a multiport network model of RPAs as a representative practical realization of PRAs. Finally, we validate the developed ideal model using radiation patterns generated by practical pixel-antenna designs and establish a link between the ideal and practical models.

\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=90mm]{Fig1/ReconAntennaMovableFluidAntenna.pdf}
	\caption{Multipath reweighting through pattern and position reconfiguration.
		\label{fig_image1}}
\end{figure}
\subsection{Introduction to PRAs}
A PRA is an antenna whose radiation pattern can be dynamically altered by controlling tunable components embedded in its radiating structure. In a two-dimensional propagation environment, its complex far-field response can be represented as a continuous function of the spatial angle $\theta \in [-\pi,\pi)$, denoted by $f(\theta)\in\mathbb{C}$.
To demonstrate how the radiation pattern affects multipath reception, we consider a radiation source whose signal reaches a PRA through $L$ propagation paths, as illustrated on the left of Fig.~\ref{fig_image1}. The channel gain and angle of the $l$th path are denoted by $\alpha_l$ and $\theta_l$, respectively. The resulting effective channel can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{CRPRA}
	h = \sum_{l=1}^{L}f(\theta_l)\alpha_l.
\end{align}
According to \eqref{CRPRA}, the effective channel is a coherent superposition of the multipath components, where $f(\theta_l)$ determines the complex weight applied to the $l$th path. Reconfiguring the radiation pattern therefore changes the relative amplitudes and phases of these components and consequently reshapes the effective channel.
We next compare pattern reconfiguration with the position-reconfiguration mechanism commonly considered in MA and FAS, as illustrated on the right of Fig.~\ref{fig_image1}. Suppose that an antenna is displaced from $\boldsymbol{p}$ to $\widetilde{\boldsymbol{p}}$. Neglecting the variation in large-scale path loss caused by the displacement, the effective channel becomes
\begin{align}\label{CRMF}
	\overline{h}
	= \sum_{l=1}^{L} e^{j2\pi (d_l(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{p}})-d_l({\boldsymbol{p}}))/\lambda}\alpha_l
	= \sum_{l=1}^{L}e^{j\psi_l(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{p}})}\alpha_l,
\end{align}
where $d_l({\boldsymbol{p}})$ denotes the propagation distance associated with the $l$th path at position ${\boldsymbol{p}}$, $\psi_l(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{p}})\triangleq 2\pi (d_l(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{p}})-d_l({\boldsymbol{p}}))/\lambda$, and $\lambda$ denotes the carrier wavelength. \eqref{CRMF} shows that position reconfiguration also changes the coherent superposition of the propagation paths by modifying their complex weights. 
From \eqref{CRPRA} and \eqref{CRMF}, both reconfiguration mechanisms modify the coherent superposition of the propagation paths by changing their complex weights. However, the underlying mechanisms and the resulting constraints on these weights are different. Position reconfiguration modifies the path phases through physical displacement, resulting in unit-modulus weights $e^{j\psi_l(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{p}})}$ that are jointly determined by a common position vector. In contrast, pattern reconfiguration directly modifies the angular response $f(\theta_l)$ and can therefore adjust both the amplitudes and phases of the path weights, subject to the set of physically realizable radiation patterns. Consequently, a PRA can reproduce the same effective channel as position reconfiguration if the corresponding position-induced path weights are realizable by its radiation pattern. Moreover, the two mechanisms induce different feasible sets of path weights. Specifically, position reconfiguration is constrained by the unit-modulus weights jointly determined by the antenna position, whereas pattern reconfiguration is constrained by the physically realizable radiation patterns.
Note that the equivalence between  position reconfiguration and pattern reconfiguration has been investigated in previous works~\cite{zhang2026pixel,chen2025remaa,Zhang2025PRAFAS}. These studies primarily establish the equivalence by matching spatial correlation characteristics and focus on emulating physical antenna movement. In contrast, this work interprets the relationship from a path-wise channel-transformation perspective. As discussed above, pattern reconfiguration is not restricted to position-induced unit-modulus path weighting, but has its own feasible set of complex path weights determined by the physically realizable radiation patterns. Therefore, PRAs should not be viewed merely as a means of emulating antenna movement. Their distinct reconfigurability may provide additional degrees of freedom for manipulating multipath superposition. Motivated by this observation, we next characterize the intrinsic reconfigurability of PRAs through ideal and practical radiation-pattern models.
\subsection{Ideal Model for PRAs}~\label{IdealModel}
We next develop an implementation-independent ideal model to characterize the intrinsic pattern reconfigurability of a PRA. The radiation pattern $f(\theta)$ is a continuous complex-valued function of the spatial angle, and directly treating $f(\theta)$ as a design variable leads to an infinite-dimensional formulation. To obtain a tractable parametric representation, we expand it using a Fourier series as
\begin{align}\label{FourierTransform}
	f(\theta)=\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}\gamma_n e^{jn\theta},
\end{align}
where $\gamma_n\in\mathbb C$ denotes the $n$th Fourier coefficient.
\subsubsection{Order Constraint}\label{OC}
Although \eqref{FourierTransform} converts the functional design into the design of Fourier coefficients, it still contains infinitely many variables. To obtain a finite-dimensional representation, we next exploit the physical constraint imposed by the finite antenna size.
\begin{proposition}
	Let $R_{\rm A}$ denote the radius of the smallest circle enclosing the antenna aperture.
	The Fourier coefficients of $f(\theta)$ admit a rapidly decaying upper envelope for
	$|n|>e\pi R_{\rm A}/\lambda$. Accordingly, an effective Fourier truncation order is
	given by
	\begin{align}
		N=\left\lceil \frac{e\pi R_{\rm A}}{\lambda}\right\rceil .
	\end{align}
\end{proposition}
\textit{Proof: See Appendix A.}
Proposition 1 indicates that the high-order Fourier coefficients are increasingly
suppressed beyond an aperture-dependent effective order. Accordingly, retaining
the $2N+1$ Fourier orders with $|n|\leq N$, where $N$ is determined by the
electrical size of the antenna aperture, provides the finite-dimensional approximation
\begin{align} \label{FourierTransform2}
	f(\theta) \approx \sum_{n=-N}^{N}\gamma_n e^{jn\theta}.
\end{align}
\subsubsection{Total Power Constraint}\label{TPC}
To eliminate arbitrary scaling of the radiation pattern, we normalize its angular power to unity. Using \eqref{FourierTransform2}, we have
\begin{align}\label{PatternPower}
	\frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} |f(\theta)|^2 {\rm d}\theta&\approx\frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \bigg| \sum_{n=-N}^{N}\gamma_n e^{jn\theta}\bigg|^2 {\rm d}\theta\nonumber
	&\overset{\rm (a)}{=}\sum_{n=-N}^{N} |\gamma_n|^2,
\end{align}
where (a) follows from the orthogonality of the Fourier basis functions. Therefore, the normalized Fourier coefficients satisfy
\begin{align}\label{CoefficientPowerConstraint}
	\sum_{n=-N}^{N} |\gamma_n|^2 =1. 
\end{align}
\subsubsection{Per-Order Power Constraint}\label{PCE}
The total power constraint alone does not capture the order-dependent limitations imposed by the finite antenna size. In particular, sufficiently high-order angular modes are strongly suppressed for an antenna with a finite electrical size~\cite{Bucci1987Spatial}. Therefore, an additional per-order power constraint is imposed on the Fourier coefficients. As derived in Appendix~B, the power of the $n$th-order coefficient satisfies
\begin{align}\label{TB}
	|\gamma_n|^2\leq\min\{1,C B_n\},
\end{align}
where $B_n$ is an aperture-dependent factor and $C$ characterizes the admissible aperture-current intensity. For convenience, define
\begin{align}
	\rho_n\triangleq\sqrt{\min\{1,C B_n\}}
\end{align}
as the amplitude bound of the $n$th Fourier coefficient.
Combining the order, total-power, and per-order power constraints, the proposed ideal PRA model is given by
\begin{align}
	&f(\theta)\approx
	\sum_{n=-N}^{N}
	\gamma_n e^{jn\theta},\nonumber
	&\sum_{n=-N}^{N}
	|\gamma_n|^2=1,\quad|\gamma_n|\leq\rho_n,\quad
	n=-N,\ldots,N.
\end{align}
Note that ideal models for PRAs have also been considered in existing works~\cite{Ying2025Reconfigurable,Zheng2026TriHybrid}. The developed model extends these formulations in three aspects. First, it adopts a complex-valued Fourier representation that explicitly captures both the amplitude and phase of the angular response. Such joint amplitude-phase characterization is consistent with practical PRA designs~\cite{WangPhaseAmp2024}, and is particularly suitable for manipulating the coherent superposition of multipath components. Second, the truncation order is explicitly related to the finite electrical size of the antenna aperture. Third, an order-dependent power bound is imposed on each angular mode to capture the aperture-induced suppression of high-order modes. Taken together, the truncation-order relation and the per-order power bounds specify two fundamental physical conditions that an ideal PRA model should satisfy.
In summary, the proposed ideal model provides an antenna aperture-dependent theoretical capability envelope on the radiation patterns achievable by a PRA confined to a prescribed finite aperture. 
\subsection{Practical Model  for PRAs}~\label{PracticalModel}
The radiation pattern is governed by the surface-current distribution over the antenna radiating structure. Therefore, reconfiguring the radiation pattern requires the surface current to be redistributed. An RPA provides a practical architecture for this purpose. As shown on the left side of Fig.~\ref{fig_image2}, an RPA consists of metallic pixels interconnected by RF switches, such as PIN diodes. Switching each PIN diode between the ``on'' and ``off'' states changes the current paths over the antenna surface and reconfigures the resulting radiation pattern.
\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=90mm]{Fig2/ReconfigurableAntenna.pdf}
	\caption{Equivalent multiport network model of an RPA.
		\label{fig_image2}}
\end{figure}
A multiport network model is widely used to characterize the effect of the PIN diode states on the radiation pattern~\cite{Shen2026AntennaCoding}. In this model, each feed point and each PIN-diode connection is represented as a port, while each PIN diode is represented by a switchable load. Let $Q$ denote the number of PIN diodes. The resulting network contains $Q+1$ ports, comprising one feed port and $Q$ pixel ports. The current and voltage of the $q$th pixel port, $q=1,2,\cdots,Q$, are denoted by $i_q$ and $v_q$, respectively. For notational convenience, define $\boldsymbol{i}_{\rm P} \triangleq [i_1,i_2,\cdots,i_Q]^{\rm T}$ and $\boldsymbol{v}_{\rm P} \triangleq [v_1,v_2,\cdots,v_Q]^{\rm T}$. The feed-port current and voltage are denoted by $i_{\rm A}$ and $v_{\rm A}$, respectively. The port voltages $[v_{\rm A},\boldsymbol{v}_{\rm P}^{\rm T}]^{\rm T}$ and currents $[i_{\rm A},\boldsymbol{i}_{\rm P}^{\rm T}]^{\rm T}$ are related through the network impedance matrix
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol{Z}_{\rm I} = \left[ \begin{matrix}
		z_{\mathrm{A}\mathrm{A}}&		\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{AP}
		\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{PA}&		\boldsymbol{Z}_\mathrm{PP}
	\end{matrix} \right],
\end{align}
where $z_{\mathrm{A}\mathrm{A}}$, $\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{AP}$, $\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{PA}$, and $\boldsymbol{Z}_\mathrm{PP}$ denote the feed-port self-impedance, the feed-pixel mutual-impedance row and column vectors, and the pixel-port impedance matrix, respectively. The port voltage-current relationship is
\begin{align}\label{portrelationship}
	\left[\begin{matrix}
		v_\mathrm{A}
		\boldsymbol{v}_\mathrm{P}
	\end{matrix} \right] = \left[\begin{matrix}
		z_{\mathrm{A}\mathrm{A}}&		\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{AP}
		\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{PA}&		\boldsymbol{Z}_\mathrm{PP}
	\end{matrix} \right]\left[\begin{matrix}
		i_\mathrm{A}
		\boldsymbol{i}_\mathrm{P}
	\end{matrix} \right].
\end{align}
We next model the PIN diodes. Each PIN diode operates in either the ``on'' or ``off'' state, and the collective switch state is represented by the binary vector $\boldsymbol{t}\in\mathbb{Z}^{Q}$. Specifically, $[\boldsymbol{t}]_q=0$ and $[\boldsymbol{t}]_q=1$ represent the ``on'' and ``off'' states of the $q$th PIN diode, respectively. Under the ideal-switch approximation, the impedance of a PIN diode is nearly zero in the ``on'' state and approaches infinity in the ``off'' state. Accordingly, the PIN diodes are represented by the load-impedance matrix
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol{Z}_{\rm L}(\boldsymbol{t}) = \mathrm{diag}\{z_{{\rm L},1},z_{{\rm L},2},\cdots,z_{{\rm L},Q}\},
\end{align}
where the $q$th diagonal entry of $\boldsymbol{Z}_{\rm L}(\boldsymbol{t})$ is
\begin{align}
	z_{{\rm L},q} = \begin{cases}
		0	,~~~~~[\boldsymbol{t}]_q = 0,
		\infty	,~~~~[\boldsymbol{t}]_q = 1.
	\end{cases}
\end{align}
In numerical evaluations, the infinite off-state impedance is approximated by a sufficiently large value, e.g., $10^{6}$. The load impedance, pixel-port current, and pixel-port voltage satisfy
\begin{align}\label{vP1}
	\boldsymbol{v}_\mathrm{P} = -\boldsymbol{Z}_{\rm L}(\boldsymbol{t})\boldsymbol{i}_\mathrm{P}.
\end{align}
From \eqref{portrelationship}, the pixel-port voltage also satisfies
\begin{align}\label{vP2}
	\boldsymbol{v}_\mathrm{P} = \boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{PA}i_\mathrm{A} + \boldsymbol{Z}_\mathrm{PP}\boldsymbol{i}_\mathrm{P}.
\end{align}
Combining \eqref{vP1} and \eqref{vP2} yields
\begin{align}\label{Ip}
	\boldsymbol{i}_\mathrm{P} = -(\boldsymbol{Z}_{\rm L}(\boldsymbol{t})+\boldsymbol{Z}_\mathrm{PP})^{-1}\boldsymbol{z}_\mathrm{PA}i_\mathrm{A}.
\end{align}
By electromagnetic linearity, the radiation pattern of the RPA is the superposition of the open-circuit embedded patterns of the feed and pixel ports, weighted by their respective port currents. Hence, the resulting radiation pattern is
\begin{align}\label{matcal}
	g(\theta) = \sum_{q=1}^{Q+1} i_{q} b_q(\theta),
\end{align}
where $b_q(\theta)$, $q=1,2,\cdots,Q$, denotes the open-circuit embedded radiation pattern associated with the $q$th pixel port. We stipulate that $i_{Q+1} = i_{\rm A}$ for ease of notation and $b_{Q+1}(\theta)$ is the open-circuit embedded radiation pattern associated with the feed port. Note that the impedance parameters and embedded radiation patterns required by this practical model can be obtained from full-wave electromagnetic simulations.
