\subsection{Consistency Between the Ideal and Practical Models}
\label{ModelVerification}
The ideal and practical PRA models characterize pattern reconfigurability from complementary perspectives. The ideal model in Section~\ref{IdealModel} characterizes the implementation-independent capability envelope imposed by a prescribed antenna size, whereas the practical model in Section~\ref{PracticalModel} describes the radiation patterns realizable by a specific RPA architecture. Their consistency can therefore be examined by comparing the Fourier-domain characteristics of practically realizable radiation patterns with the constraints determined by the ideal model. Specifically, practical RPA designs are used to verify the aperture-dependent Fourier order and per-order coefficient bounds of the ideal model, while the ideal model provides a theoretical reference for interpreting the Fourier-domain behavior and realizable coefficient regions of practical RPAs.
To examine this consistency, we consider three RPA designs, denoted by RPA~1, RPA~2, and RPA~3, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig_image3}. RPA~1 employs a $6\times6$ pixel layout within a $0.25\lambda\times0.25\lambda$ aperture, RPA~2 employs a $6\times6$ pixel layout within a $0.5\lambda\times0.5\lambda$ aperture, and RPA~3 employs a $3\times3$ pixel layout within the same $0.5\lambda\times0.5\lambda$ aperture. These configurations allow the effects of the aperture size and hardware configuration to be distinguished. Specifically, RPA~1 and RPA~2 have the same pixel layout but different aperture sizes, whereas RPA~2 and RPA~3 have the same aperture size but different pixel layouts. Consistent with the two-dimensional angular model adopted in this work, the following validation is performed using the complex far-field responses over a fixed angular cut. 
We first verify the practical multiport network model by comparing the radiation patterns calculated using \eqref{matcal} with those obtained from full-wave electromagnetic simulations. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_image4}, the radiation patterns obtained using the two approaches are in close agreement. This agreement confirms that the multiport network model accurately captures the dependence of the practical radiation pattern on the switch configuration and can therefore be used to investigate the Fourier-domain characteristics of the three RPAs.
