We further examine the per-order power constraint in Section~\ref{PCE}. Fig.~\ref{fig_image6} compares the maximum normalized Fourier-coefficient power realized by the three RPAs with the corresponding theoretical bounds in \eqref{TB}. For all three designs, the realized Fourier-coefficient powers remain below the theoretical bounds. Moreover, the coefficient powers become negligible at sufficiently high Fourier orders, consistent with the aperture-induced limitation characterized by the ideal model. These observations further validate the per-order Fourier-coefficient power constraints derived for the ideal model. Meanwhile, a noticeable gap remains between the realized maxima and the theoretical bounds. This gap is expected because the theoretical bounds characterize the implementation-independent capability envelope associated with a prescribed aperture, whereas a practical RPA is additionally constrained by its pixel geometry, switch topology, and realizable current distributions.
We finally examine the realizable Fourier-coefficient regions of the three RPAs in Fig.~\ref{fig_image7}. Each sample is obtained from a switch configuration by calculating the corresponding radiation pattern and extracting its Fourier coefficients. The samples of RPA~1 and RPA~2 form denser and more diverse realizable sets than those of RPA~3, showing that the hardware configuration affects the range of achievable Fourier coefficients. In particular, although RPA~2 and RPA~3 have the same aperture size and thus the same effective Fourier order, their different pixel layouts lead to substantially different realizable coefficient regions.
The above results demonstrate the consistency between the ideal and practical PRA models. The practical RPA designs exhibit the aperture-dependent effective Fourier order and per-order Fourier-coefficient power limitations predicted by the ideal model. In turn, the ideal model provides an implementation-independent theoretical reference for interpreting the Fourier-domain characteristics of practical RPAs: the antenna aperture mainly determines the effective Fourier order and the corresponding capability envelope, whereas the pixel layout and switch configuration determine the realizable coefficient region within this envelope. Therefore, the ideal and practical models provide complementary descriptions of PRA reconfigurability from theoretical and implementation perspectives, respectively.
\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:mb-system}
Based on the PRA models developed in Section~\ref{SysModel}, we establish a Fourier-domain downlink model for PRA-enabled multiuser MIMO and formulate the WSR maximization problems under the ideal and practical PRA models.
