\subsection{System Model}
\label{subsec:mb-downlink}
We consider a narrowband downlink system in which a base station (BS) equipped with $M$ PRAs simultaneously serves $K$ single-antenna users. Each PRA is driven by a dedicated RF chain, and the BS employs the fully digital beamformer $\boldsymbol W=[\boldsymbol w_1,\ldots,\boldsymbol w_K]\in\mathbb C^{M\times K}$. Let $\boldsymbol s=[s_1,\ldots,s_K]^{\mathrm T}\in\mathbb C^K$ denote the data
symbol vector, where $\mathbb E\{\boldsymbol s\boldsymbol s^{\mathrm H}\}=\boldsymbol I_K$. The transmit-power constraint is
\begin{align}
	\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\mathrm F}^2\leq P_0.
	\label{eq:mb-transmit-power}
\end{align}
Under the ideal PRA model in Section~\ref{IdealModel}, the radiation pattern of
the $m$th PRA is represented as
\begin{align}
	f_m(\theta)=\boldsymbol f(\theta)^{\mathrm T}\boldsymbol\gamma_m,
	\label{IModel}
\end{align}
where $\boldsymbol f(\theta)=[e^{-jN\theta},\ldots,1,\ldots,e^{jN\theta}]^{\mathrm T} \in\mathbb C^T$ contains the retained angular basis functions and $\boldsymbol\gamma_m=[\gamma_{m,-N},\ldots,\gamma_{m,N}]^{\mathrm T} \in\mathbb C^T$ contains the corresponding Fourier coefficients, with $T\triangleq2N+1$.
For the practical PRA model, let $\boldsymbol t_m\in\{0,1\}^{Q}$ denote the PIN diode state vector of the $m$th PRA. According to the multiport network model, its radiation pattern is
\begin{align}
	g_m(\theta)
	=\boldsymbol b(\theta)^{\mathrm T}\boldsymbol i_m(\boldsymbol t_m),
	\label{PModel}
\end{align}
where $\boldsymbol b(\theta)=[b_1(\theta),\ldots,b_{Q+1}(\theta)]^{\mathrm T} \in\mathbb C^{Q+1}$ collects the embedded port patterns and
$\boldsymbol i_m(\boldsymbol t_m)=[i_{m,1}(\boldsymbol t_m),\ldots,i_{m,Q+1}(\boldsymbol t_m)]^{\mathrm T} \in\mathbb C^{Q+1}$ collects the corresponding port currents. Here, $Q$ is the number of PIN diodes, and the $(Q+1)$ ports comprise $Q$ pixel ports and one feed port.
The consistency analysis in Section~\ref{ModelVerification} shows that practical patterns can be represented in the same Fourier domain as ideal patterns. Accordingly, the truncated representation of \eqref{PModel} is
\begin{align}\label{PModel2}
	g_m(\theta) \approx\boldsymbol f(\theta)^{\mathrm T} \boldsymbol\xi_m(\boldsymbol t_m),
\end{align}
where
$\boldsymbol\xi_m(\boldsymbol t_m) =[\xi_{m,-N}(\boldsymbol t_m),\ldots,\xi_{m,N}(\boldsymbol t_m)]^{\mathrm T} \in\mathbb C^T$ contains the retained Fourier coefficients of the practical pattern. Projecting \eqref{PModel} onto the Fourier basis gives
\begin{align}
	\xi_{m,n}(\boldsymbol t_m)
	&=\sum_{q=1}^{Q+1}c_{n,q}i_{m,q}(\boldsymbol t_m),~ n=-N,\ldots,N,
	\label{eq:practical-modal-coefficient}
	c_{n,q}
	&\triangleq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
	b_q(\theta)e^{-jn\theta}\,\mathrm d\theta.
	\label{eq:embedded-pattern-coefficient}
\end{align}
Define $\boldsymbol C\in\mathbb C^{T\times(Q+1)}$ by $[\boldsymbol C]_{n+N+1,q}=c_{n,q}$. Then, the practical multiport model and the common Fourier-domain model are linked by the mapping
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol\xi_m(\boldsymbol t_m)
	=\boldsymbol C\boldsymbol i_m(\boldsymbol t_m).
	\label{eq:hardware-to-modal-map}
\end{align}

In this paper, we focus on the channel-shaping gain provided by pattern reconfiguration. To keep the same total-pattern-power scale as the ideal models, we normalize $\boldsymbol{\xi}_m(\boldsymbol{t}_m)$ as 
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol{\zeta}_m(\boldsymbol{t}_m) = \boldsymbol{\xi}_m(\boldsymbol{t}_m)/\|\boldsymbol{\xi}_m(\boldsymbol{t}_m)\|_2.
\end{align}
From \eqref{IModel} and \eqref{PModel2}, the radiation patterns of PRAs are composed of the channel angle-dependent responses and the electromagnetic design parameters. By incorporating the channel-angle-dependent responses into the Saleh--Valenzuela channel model, we express the Fourier-domain channel between the $m$th antenna and the $k$th user as
\begin{align}\label{modalchannel}
	\boldsymbol{h}_{m,k} = \sum_{l=1}^{L_k}\alpha_{k,l}^*a_m^*(\theta_{k,l})\boldsymbol f^*(\theta_{k,l}),
\end{align}
where $L_k$ is the number of channel paths, and $\alpha_{k,l}$ and $\theta_{k,l}$ are the channel gain and  angle of the $l$th path, respectively. $a_m(\theta_{k,l})$ is the array response of the $m$th antenna. Denote the position of the $m$th antenna by $x_m$. Then, $a_m(\theta_{k,l}) = e^{j2\pi x_m\sin\theta_{k,l}/\lambda}$. Stacking $\boldsymbol{h}_{m,k}$ for $M$ PRAs yields
\begin{align}
	\overline{\boldsymbol{h}}_k=\left[\boldsymbol{h}_{1,k}^{\rm T},\ldots,\boldsymbol{h}_{M,k}^{\rm T}\right]^{\rm T}\in\mathbb{C}^{MT}.
\end{align}
Stacking the Fourier coefficients for the $M$ antennas, we obtain the electromagnetic beamformers for the ideal and practical models as
\begin{align}\label{EMBeamformer}
\boldsymbol{\Gamma} &= \mathrm{diag}\{\boldsymbol{\gamma}_1,\cdots,\boldsymbol{\gamma}_M\},\nonumber
\boldsymbol{\varLambda}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}}) &= \mathrm{diag}\{\boldsymbol{\zeta}_1(\boldsymbol{t}_1),\cdots,\boldsymbol{\zeta}_M(\boldsymbol{t}_M)\},
\end{align}
 where $\boldsymbol{\overline{t}}\triangleq[\boldsymbol{t}_1^{\rm T},\boldsymbol{t}_2^{\rm T},\cdots,\boldsymbol{t}_M^{\rm T}]^{\rm T}$.
With the Fourier-domain channel in \eqref{modalchannel} and the ideal electromagnetic beamformer in \eqref{EMBeamformer}, the received signal for the $k$th user can be expressed as 
\begin{align}\label{eq:received}
	y_k=\boldsymbol{\overline{h}}_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{w}_k s_k+\sum_{i\neq k}\boldsymbol{\overline{h}}_k^{\rm H}
 \boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{w}_i s_i+n_k,
\end{align}
where $n_k\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^2)$ denotes the additive white Gaussian noise with power $\sigma^2$. By replacing $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ with $\boldsymbol{\varLambda}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}})$, we can obtain the received signal under the practical electromagnetic beamformer.

\subsection{Problem Formulation} \label{subsec:mb-common-modal}
\subsubsection{Ideal PRA Model} From \eqref{eq:received}, the signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) of the $k$th user under the ideal PRA model is expressed as
\begin{align}\label{SINR}
\varUpsilon_k(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\Gamma}) =\frac{|\boldsymbol{\overline{h}}_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{w}_k|^2}
	 {\sum_{i\neq k}|\boldsymbol{\overline{h}}_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\boldsymbol{w}_i|^2+\sigma^2}.
\end{align}
Based on \eqref{SINR}, the WSR of $K$ users can be expressed as 
\begin{align}\label{sumrate}
	S(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\Gamma}) = \sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_k\log_2(1+\varUpsilon_k(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\Gamma})),
\end{align}
where $\chi_k$ is the weighting coefficient of the $k$th user. 
According to the ideal PRA model in Section~\ref{IdealModel}, the feasible set of $\boldsymbol{\gamma}_m$ is
\begin{align}\label{feasibleset}
	\mathcal{T}\!=\!\left\{\boldsymbol\gamma\in\mathbb C^T\big|\|\boldsymbol\gamma\|_2^2\!=\!1, |\gamma_n|\leq  \rho_{n},~n\!=\!-N,\ldots,N\right\}.
\end{align}
We aim to maximize the WSR of $K$ users subject to the feasible constraint of $\boldsymbol{\gamma}_m$ in \eqref{feasibleset} and the power constraint of $\boldsymbol{W}$ in \eqref{eq:mb-transmit-power}, which can be formulated as 
\begin{subequations}\label{prob:mb-ideal}
	\begin{align}
		(\mathcal P_{\rm I})~~\max_{\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{\gamma}_m}\quad&S(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\Gamma})\label{prob:mb-ideal-a}
		\mathrm{s.t.~}\quad~&\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\mathrm F}^2\leq P_0,\label{prob:mb-ideal-b}
		&~\boldsymbol\gamma_m\in\mathcal{T},\quad m=1,\ldots,M.\label{prob:mb-ideal-c}
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
\subsubsection{Practical PRA Model} By replacing $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$ with $\boldsymbol{\varLambda}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}})$, we obtain the SINR of the $k$th user and the WSR of the $K$ users under the practical PRA model as $\varUpsilon_k(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varLambda}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}}))$ and $S(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varLambda}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}}))$, respectively. Unlike the ideal model, which uses continuous Fourier coefficients, the practical model determines each radiation pattern through the PIN diode state vector $\boldsymbol{t}_m$, where $[\boldsymbol{t}_m]_q\in\{0,1\}$. Replacing the corresponding parts in $(\mathcal P_{\rm I})$, we can obtain the practical-model formulation as
\begin{subequations}\label{mbpra}
	\begin{align}
		(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-1)~~\max_{\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{t}_m}\quad&S(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varLambda}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}}))\label{mbpraa}
		\mathrm{s.t.}\quad~&\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\mathrm F}^2\leq P_0,\label{mbprab}
		&[\boldsymbol{t}_m]_q\in\{0,1\}, \quad q=1,\ldots,Q.\label{mbprac}
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
In the next section, we develop a coefficient-bound penalty-based WMMSE (CBP-WMMSE) algorithm for $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-1)$. The ideal problem $(\mathcal P_{\rm I})$ is the continuous counterpart of $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-1)$ with the discrete hardware mapping removed and can therefore be solved using the continuous optimization component of the proposed algorithm.

\section{CBP-WMMSE Algorithm}
\label{sec:mb-algorithm}



In this section, we develop a CBP-WMMSE algorithm for solving $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-1)$. By introducing  auxiliary continuous Fourier-coefficient variables, the algorithm decomposes the mixed-binary design into continuous Fourier coefficient optimization and discrete switch-state optimization. For the discrete optimization, we propose an OBNS that evaluates all single-bit neighbors of the current state and exploits the Sherman-Morrison identity to update the inverse of the loaded multiport impedance matrix.
\subsection{Problem Reformulation}
\label{subsec:mb-rwmmse}
In $(\mathcal{P}_{\rm P}-1)$, the binary variables of PIN diodes are involved in the WSR objective. Therefore, the optimization involves the coupling of discrete and continuous variables. To relax this coupling, we introduce  auxiliary continuous Fourier-coefficient variables
\begin{align}\label{etaCons}
\boldsymbol{\eta}_m = \boldsymbol{\zeta}_m(\boldsymbol{t}_m)\in\mathbb{C}^{T}.
\end{align}
$\boldsymbol{\eta}_m\triangleq [\eta_{m,-N},\cdots,\eta_{m,N}]^{\rm T}$ collects the Fourier coefficients of the $m$th PRA. Since $\boldsymbol\eta_m$ is a continuous surrogate for a practical Fourier-coefficient vector, its component amplitudes are bounded by those supported by the hardware. Let $\overline\rho_n$ denote the maximum achievable amplitude of order $n$, which can be obtained through offline characterization of the RPA, i.e., $\overline\rho_n\triangleq\max_{\boldsymbol t_m\in\{0,1\}^{Q}}|\zeta_{m,n}(\boldsymbol t_m)|$. The feasible set of $\boldsymbol\eta_m$ is then
\begin{align}
	\overline{\mathcal T}\!\triangleq\!\left\{\boldsymbol\eta\in\mathbb C^T\middle|\|\boldsymbol\eta\|_2^2\!=\!1,~|\eta_n|\!\leq\!\overline\rho_n,~n\!=\!-N,\ldots,N\right\},
\end{align}
where $\eta_n\triangleq [\boldsymbol\eta]_{n+N+1}$. Define $\boldsymbol{\varXi}\triangleq\mathrm{diag}\{\boldsymbol{\eta}_1,\boldsymbol{\eta}_2,\cdots,\boldsymbol{\eta}_M\}$.  Then, $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-1)$ can be equivalently written as
\begin{subequations}\label{mbpra2}
	\begin{align}
		(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-2)~~\max_{\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{\varXi},\boldsymbol{t}_m}\quad&S(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varXi})\label{mbpraa2}
		\mathrm{s.t.~}\quad~~&\eqref{mbprab},~\eqref{mbprac},~\eqref{etaCons},~\boldsymbol{\eta}_m\in\overline{\mathcal T}.
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}

According to the full-power property established in~\cite{TSP23ZXT}, any nontrivial stationary solution uses the entire power budget. Then, we can discard the power constraint  and convert $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-2)$ to
\begin{subequations}\label{mbpra3}
	\begin{align}
		(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-3)~~\max_{\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{\varXi},\boldsymbol{t}_m}\quad&\overline{S}(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varXi})\label{mbpraa3}
		\mathrm{s.t.~}\quad~~&~\eqref{mbprac},~\eqref{etaCons},~\boldsymbol{\eta}_m\in\overline{\mathcal T},
	\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where 
\begin{align}\label{SINR2}
	&\overline{S}(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varXi}) = \sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_k\log_2(1+\overline{\varUpsilon}_k(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varXi})),\nonumber
	&\overline{\varUpsilon}_k(\boldsymbol{W},\boldsymbol{\varXi}) =\frac{|\boldsymbol{\overline{h}}_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol{\varXi}\boldsymbol{w}_k|^2}
	{\sum_{i\neq k}|\boldsymbol{\overline{h}}_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol{\varXi}\boldsymbol{w}_i|^2+\frac{\sigma^2}{P_0}\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\rm F}^2}.
\end{align}
Note that the solutions of $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-2)$ and $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-3)$ are connected with a scalar. After solving $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-3)$, we scale its beamforming solution to satisfy the power constraint $P_0$, thereby obtaining a solution to $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-2)$.

 $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}\text{-}3)$ remains nonconvex because of the WSR objective in \eqref{mbpraa3} and the hardware equality in \eqref{etaCons}. To handle the WSR objective, define the effective channel $\boldsymbol q_k\triangleq\boldsymbol{\varXi}^{\rm H}\overline{\boldsymbol h}_k\in\mathbb C^M$, and let $u_k\in\mathbb C$ and $v_k>0$ denote the receive equalizer and mean-square error (MSE) weight for the $k$th user, respectively. The MSE is
\begin{align}\label{ek}
	e_k\!=\!|u_k|^2\left(\sum_{i=1}^{K}|\boldsymbol q_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol w_i|^2+\frac{\sigma^2}{P_0}\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\rm F}^2\right)\!-\!2\Re\!\left\{u_k^*\boldsymbol q_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol w_k\right\}+1.
\end{align}
Let $\boldsymbol u\triangleq[u_1,\ldots,u_K]^{\rm T}$ and $\boldsymbol v\triangleq[v_1,\ldots,v_K]^{\rm T}$. Maximizing \eqref{mbpraa3} is equivalent to minimizing
\begin{align}\label{equivalence}
	\Phi(\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{\varXi},\boldsymbol u,\boldsymbol v)\triangleq\sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_k\left(v_ke_k-\ln v_k\right).
\end{align}
To address the nonconvex hardware equality constraint, we incorporate it into the objective through the penalty term
\begin{align}\label{Mini}
	\mu\sum_{m=1}^{M}\left\|\delta_m\boldsymbol\eta_m-\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)\right\|_2^2,
\end{align}
where $\mu>0$ is the penalty coefficient and $|\delta_m|=1$. The phase rotation $\delta_m$ permits phase-invariant alignment between the continuous and practical Fourier-coefficient vectors. Because this per-antenna phase can be absorbed into the corresponding row of the digital beamformer, it does not change the achievable system performance. The penalized objective is
\begin{align}\label{Lrho}
	\mathcal L_{\mu}\triangleq\Phi(\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{\varXi},\boldsymbol u,\boldsymbol v)+\mu\sum_{m=1}^{M}\left\|\delta_m\boldsymbol\eta_m-\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)\right\|_2^2.
\end{align}
Consequently, the penalized problem is
\begin{align}
	(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-4)~&\min_{\boldsymbol W,\boldsymbol{\varXi},\overline{\boldsymbol t},\boldsymbol u,\boldsymbol v,\boldsymbol\delta}\quad\mathcal L_{\mu}\nonumber
	&\mathrm{~~~~~s.t.}~\eqref{mbprac},~\boldsymbol\eta_m\in\overline{\mathcal T},~v_k>0,~|\delta_m|=1,
\end{align}
where $\boldsymbol\delta\triangleq[\delta_1,\ldots,\delta_M]^{\rm T}$.

Note that for a fixed $\mu$, $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-4)$ has a tractable block structure when the other variables are fixed. We therefore cyclically update $\boldsymbol u$, $\boldsymbol v$, $\boldsymbol W$, $\boldsymbol{\varXi}$, $\overline{\boldsymbol t}$, and $\boldsymbol\delta$.  Moreover, a finite penalty coefficient may not sufficiently enforce $\boldsymbol{\eta}_m=\boldsymbol{\zeta}_m(\boldsymbol t_m)$, whereas an excessively large initial penalty coefficient may cause the hardware-consistency term to dominate the WMMSE objective and thereby hinder convergence to a high-quality solution. Following the penalty-decomposition principle~\cite{Shi2020PDD}, we therefore initialize $\mu$ with a small positive value and progressively increase it. Accordingly, $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-4)$ is solved using a two-loop procedure, in which the inner loop alternately updates the variable blocks for a fixed $\mu$, while the outer loop increases $\mu$ until the hardware-consistency residual satisfies a prescribed tolerance.
\subsection{Inner-Loop Variable Updates}
\subsubsection{Optimization of $\boldsymbol{u}$} With all other variables fixed, $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-4)$ reduces to
\begin{align}\label{uk}
	\min_{u_k}~\sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_k\left(v_ke_k-\log v_k\right).
\end{align}
From \eqref{uk}, the optimal $u_k$ is
\begin{align}\label{eq:u}
	\widehat u_k=\frac{\boldsymbol q_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol w_k}{\sum_{i=1}^{K}|\boldsymbol q_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol w_i|^2+\frac{\sigma^2}{P_0}\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\rm F}^2}.
\end{align}
\subsubsection{Optimization of $v_k$}
With the remaining variables fixed and $u_k=\widehat u_k$, the optimal MSE weight is
\begin{align}\label{eq:v}
	\widehat v_k=1/\widehat e_k,
\end{align}
where $\widehat e_k$ denotes \eqref{ek} evaluated at $u_k=\widehat u_k$.

\subsubsection{Optimization of $\boldsymbol W$}
We next fix the other variables and optimize $\boldsymbol W$. Define
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol Q&\triangleq[\boldsymbol q_1,\ldots,\boldsymbol q_K],\qquad \boldsymbol G\triangleq\boldsymbol Q^{\rm H}\boldsymbol Q\in\mathbb C^{K\times K}.
\end{align}
Moreover, define
\begin{align}\label{eq:rwmmse-definitions}
	d_k&\triangleq\chi_kv_k|u_k|^2,\qquad c_k\triangleq\chi_kv_ku_k,\qquad \tau\triangleq\frac{\sigma^2}{P_0}\sum_{k=1}^{K}d_k,\nonumber
	\boldsymbol D&\triangleq\operatorname{diag}\{d_1,\ldots,d_K\},\qquad \boldsymbol \Omega\triangleq\operatorname{diag}\{c_1,\ldots,c_K\}.
\end{align}
The terms in \eqref{ek} that depend on $\boldsymbol W$ satisfy
\begin{align}\label{eq:W-quadratic-identities}
	\sum_{k=1}^{K}d_k\sum_{i=1}^{K}|\boldsymbol q_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol w_i|^2&=\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\boldsymbol W^{\rm H}\boldsymbol Q\boldsymbol D\boldsymbol Q^{\rm H}\boldsymbol W\right),\nonumber
	\frac{\sigma^2}{P_0}\sum_{k=1}^{K}d_k\|\boldsymbol W\|_{\rm F}^2&=\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\boldsymbol W^{\rm H}\tau\boldsymbol I_M\boldsymbol W\right),\nonumber
	-2\Re\!\left\{\sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_kv_ku_k^*\boldsymbol q_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol w_k\right\}&=-2\Re\!\left\{\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{\rm H}\boldsymbol Q^{\rm H}\boldsymbol W\right)\right\}.
\end{align}
After removing terms independent of $\boldsymbol W$, we have
\begin{align}\label{eq:W-subproblem}
	\min_{\boldsymbol W}\quad&\operatorname{Tr}\!\left[\boldsymbol W^{\rm H}(\boldsymbol Q\boldsymbol D\boldsymbol Q^{\rm H}+\tau\boldsymbol I_M)\boldsymbol W\right]-2\Re\!\left\{\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{\rm H}\boldsymbol Q^{\rm H}\boldsymbol W\right)\right\}.
\end{align}
For $\tau>0$, \eqref{eq:W-subproblem} is a strictly convex quadratic problem with the unique minimizer
\begin{align}\label{eq:W-update}
	\widehat{\boldsymbol W}&=(\boldsymbol Q\boldsymbol D\boldsymbol Q^{\rm H}+\tau\boldsymbol I_M)^{-1}\boldsymbol Q\boldsymbol{\Omega}\nonumber
	&\overset{\rm (a)}{=}\boldsymbol Q(\tau\boldsymbol I_K+\boldsymbol D\boldsymbol G)^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Omega},
\end{align}
where $\rm (a)$ follows from the push-through identity~\cite{Boyd2018Introduction}. Note that \eqref{eq:W-update} requires only a $K\times K$ matrix inversion.
\subsubsection{Optimization of $\boldsymbol{\varXi}$}
Stack the continuous Fourier coefficients as
\begin{align}
	\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}\triangleq[\boldsymbol\eta_1^{\rm T},\ldots,\boldsymbol\eta_M^{\rm T}]^{\rm T}\in\mathbb C^{MT},
\end{align}
and define
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol E_i&\triangleq\operatorname{diag}\{\boldsymbol w_i^*\}\otimes\boldsymbol I_T\in\mathbb C^{MT\times MT},~\boldsymbol a_{k,i}\triangleq\boldsymbol E_i\overline{\boldsymbol h}_k\in\mathbb C^{MT},\nonumber
	\boldsymbol\Delta&\triangleq\operatorname{diag}\{\delta_1,\ldots,\delta_M\}\otimes\boldsymbol I_T,\nonumber
	\overline{\boldsymbol\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}})&\triangleq[\boldsymbol\zeta_1^{\rm T}(\boldsymbol t_1),\ldots,\boldsymbol\zeta_M^{\rm T}(\boldsymbol t_M)]^{\rm T}.
\end{align}
With these definitions, $\overline{\boldsymbol h}_k^{\rm H}\boldsymbol{\varXi}\boldsymbol w_i=\boldsymbol a_{k,i}^{\rm H}\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}$. After removing terms independent of $\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}$, the Fourier-coefficient subproblem~is
\begin{align}\label{eq:eta-subproblem}
	\min_{\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}}\quad&\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}^{\rm H}\boldsymbol A\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}-2\Re\!\left\{\boldsymbol c^{\rm H}\overline{\boldsymbol\eta}\right\}\nonumber
	\mathrm{s.t.}\quad&\boldsymbol\eta_m\in\overline{\mathcal T},\quad m=1,\ldots,M,
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}\label{eq:Aeta-beta}
	\boldsymbol A&\triangleq\sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_kv_k|u_k|^2\sum_{i=1}^{K}\boldsymbol a_{k,i}\boldsymbol a_{k,i}^{\rm H}+\mu\boldsymbol\Delta^{\rm H}\boldsymbol\Delta,\nonumber
	\boldsymbol c&\triangleq\sum_{k=1}^{K}\chi_kv_ku_k\boldsymbol a_{k,k}+\mu\boldsymbol\Delta^{\rm H}\overline{\boldsymbol\zeta}(\boldsymbol{\overline{t}}).
\end{align}
The unit-norm constraints in $\overline{\mathcal T}$ form a product of $M$ complex spheres, while the per-order bounds further restrict the feasible Fourier-coefficient amplitudes. Therefore, \eqref{eq:eta-subproblem} constitutes an inequality-constrained optimization problem over a product-sphere manifold. We solve it using a projected Riemannian gradient method. Let 
\begin{align}\label{eq:eta-egrad}
	\boldsymbol g=2(\boldsymbol A\boldsymbol{\overline{\eta}}-\boldsymbol c)
	=[\boldsymbol g_1^{\rm T},\ldots,\boldsymbol g_M^{\rm T}]^{\rm T}
\end{align}
denote the Euclidean gradient. Its projection onto the tangent space of the $m$th complex sphere is
\begin{align}\label{eq:eta-rgrad}
	\boldsymbol{\overline{g}}_m=\boldsymbol g_m-\Re\!\left\{\boldsymbol\eta_m^{\rm H}\boldsymbol g_m\right\}\boldsymbol\eta_m.
\end{align}
For a step size $\kappa>0$, the tentative point $\boldsymbol\eta_m-\kappa\boldsymbol{\overline{g}}_m$ is projected onto $\overline{\mathcal T}$. At each iteration, $\kappa$ is selected via Armijo backtracking, and the projected tentative point is accepted when it satisfies the sufficient-decrease condition. This procedure is repeated until convergence.
\subsubsection{Optimization of $\boldsymbol{t}_m$ and $\delta_m$}
For fixed $\boldsymbol\eta_m$,  $\boldsymbol t_m$ and $\delta_m$ are decoupled across the PRAs. The $m$th subproblem is
\begin{align}\label{eq:t-delta-subproblem}
	\min_{\boldsymbol t_m,\delta_m}~~&
	\|\delta_m\boldsymbol\eta_m-\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)\|_2^2\nonumber
	\mathrm{s.t.}~~~~&[\boldsymbol t_m]_q\in\{0,1\},~~q=1,\ldots,Q,~~|\delta_m|=1.
\end{align}
Since both $\boldsymbol\eta_m$ and $\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)$ have unit norm, minimizing \eqref{eq:t-delta-subproblem} over $\delta_m$ for a given $\boldsymbol t_m$ gives
\begin{align}\label{eq:delta-update}
	\widehat\delta_m =\dfrac{\boldsymbol\eta_m^{\rm H}\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)}
	{|\boldsymbol\eta_m^{\rm H}\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)|}. 
\end{align}
Then, the minimized residual is
\begin{align}\label{eq:phase-invariant-residual}
	2-2|\boldsymbol\eta_m^{\rm H}\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)|.
\end{align}
Consequently, the optimization of $\boldsymbol{t}_m$ is equivalently given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:t-correlation}
	\max_{\boldsymbol t_m\in\{0,1\}^{Q}} |\boldsymbol\eta_m^{\rm H}\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)|.
\end{align}
Exhaustive search for \eqref{eq:t-correlation} evaluates all $2^Q$ switch states and is therefore impractical for large $Q$. We instead propose an OBNS method. Given the current state $\boldsymbol t_m$, define the $q$th one-bit neighbor by
\begin{align}\label{eq:one-flip-neighbor}
	\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)}	\triangleq\boldsymbol t_m+
	(1-2[\boldsymbol t_m]_q)\boldsymbol e_q,\qquad q=1,\ldots,Q,
\end{align}
and set $\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(0)}=\boldsymbol t_m$. $\boldsymbol e_q$ denotes the $q$th basis vector, whose $q$th entry is one and all other entries are zero. It is easy to verify that the $q$th entries of $\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)}$ and $\boldsymbol t_m$ are complementary, whereas all other entries are identical.  The best neighbor is selected according to
\begin{align}\label{eq:one-flip-update}
	q_m^{\star}\in\arg\max_{q\in\{0,1,\ldots,Q\}}
	\left|\boldsymbol\eta_m^{\rm H}
	\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)})\right|.
\end{align}
If $q_m^{\star}\neq0$ and the objective is strictly improved, we set $\boldsymbol t_m\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q_m^{\star})}$ and repeat \eqref{eq:one-flip-update}; otherwise, the search terminates. Because the current state is included in the candidate set, the objective in \eqref{eq:t-delta-subproblem} does not increase after any accepted flip. When the OBNS method terminates, we denote the resulting switch state by $\widehat{\boldsymbol t}_m$.
One potential drawback of the OBNS method is the repeated evaluation of $\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)})$, which involves an inversion of a $Q\times Q$ matrix in \eqref{Ip} and incurs a high computational complexity of $\mathcal{O}(Q^3)$. To accelerate the evaluation, we exploit the diagonal structure of the switch-load perturbation to reduce the computational complexity. Define
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)\triangleq
	\boldsymbol Z_{\rm PP}+\boldsymbol Z_{\rm L}(\boldsymbol t_m).
\end{align}
Flipping the $q$th switch changes only one diagonal entry, and thus the candidate loaded-impedance matrix is
\begin{align}
	\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)})=\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)+
	\Delta_q \boldsymbol e_q\boldsymbol e_q^{\rm T},
\end{align}
where $\Delta_q$ denotes the corresponding change in the load impedance. According to the Sherman--Morrison identity, we have
\begin{align}\label{eq:woodbury-switch}
		\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)})^{-1}=\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)^{-1}-
	\frac{\Delta_{q}\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)^{-1}\boldsymbol e_q
		\boldsymbol e_q^{\rm T}\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)^{-1}}
	{1+\Delta_{q}\boldsymbol e_q^{\rm T}
		\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)^{-1}\boldsymbol e_q}.
\end{align}
Note that $\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol t_m)^{-1}$ has been obtained in the previous iteration. Therefore, no new matrix inversion is required for $\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)})^{-1}$. In addition, the calculation of $\boldsymbol J(\boldsymbol{\widetilde{t}}_m^{(q)})^{-1}$ involves sparse vectors $\boldsymbol{e}_q$ and thus can be exploited to further improve the calculation efficiency. After the OBNS terminates, $\delta_m$ is updated using \eqref{eq:delta-update}.
\subsection{Outer-Loop Updates and Stop Criteria}
For a fixed penalty coefficient $\mu$, the inner loop cyclically updates the communication block $\{\boldsymbol W,u_k,v_k\}$, the continuous Fourier-coefficient block $\boldsymbol{\varXi}$, and the practical-state block $\{\boldsymbol t_m,\delta_m\}$. The inner loop terminates when the variation of the objective in \eqref{Lrho} between adjacent iterations is less than a predefined threshold $\epsilon_{\rm in}$ or when the prescribed maximum number of inner iterations is reached.
For the outer iteration, define the hardware-consistency residual as
\begin{align}\label{eq:hardware-residual}
 \varrho\triangleq \sum_{m=1}^{M}\left\|\delta_m\boldsymbol\eta_m-\boldsymbol\zeta_m(\boldsymbol t_m)\right\|_2^2.
\end{align}
If $\varrho>\epsilon_{\rm out}$, the penalty coefficient is increased according to $\mu \leftarrow \beta\mu$, where $\beta$ is the penalty scaling factor. The outer loop terminates when $\varrho\leq\epsilon_{\rm out}$ or when the maximum number of outer iterations is reached.
After the CBP-WMMSE algorithm terminates, we normalize $\widehat{\boldsymbol{W}}$ via
\begin{align}\label{Ww}
	\widetilde{\boldsymbol{W}} = \sqrt{P_0}\widehat{\boldsymbol{W}}/\|\widehat{\boldsymbol{W}}\|_{\rm F}.
\end{align}
Then, we absorb the phase rotation $\delta_m$ into $\widetilde{\boldsymbol{W}}$ and obtain the final digital beamformer as
\begin{align}\label{Phase}
	\overline{\overline{\boldsymbol{W}}} = \mathrm{diag}\{\delta_1^*,\delta_2^*,\cdots,\delta_M^*\}\widetilde{\boldsymbol{W}}.
\end{align}
Finally, we summarize the complete procedure of the CBP-WMMSE algorithm in \textbf{Algorithm~\ref{alg:modal-bound-pdd-rwmmse}}.
\begin{algorithm}[!t]
	\caption{CBP-WMMSE Algorithm}
	\label{alg:modal-bound-pdd-rwmmse}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\STATE \textbf{Input:} $\overline{\boldsymbol h}_k$, $\chi_k$, $\boldsymbol Z_{\rm PP}$, $\boldsymbol z_{\rm PA}$, $b_q(\theta)$, $\boldsymbol C$, $P_0$, $M$, $Q$, $K$, $\epsilon_{\rm in}$, $\epsilon_{\rm out}$, $\beta$, $I_{\rm in}$, and $I_{\rm out}$.
		\STATE Initialize $\boldsymbol W$, $\boldsymbol\eta_m$, $\boldsymbol t_m$, and $\delta_m$.
		\WHILE{the stop conditions are not satisfied}
		\STATE $\mu\leftarrow\beta\mu$.
		\WHILE{the stop conditions are not satisfied}
		\STATE Update $u_k$ for all $k$ via \eqref{eq:u}.
		\STATE Update $v_k$ for all $k$ via \eqref{eq:v}.
		\STATE Update $\boldsymbol{W}$ via \eqref{eq:W-update}.
		\STATE Update $\boldsymbol{\eta}_m$ for all $m$ by solving \eqref{eq:eta-subproblem}.
		\STATE Update $\delta_m$ and $\boldsymbol{t}_m$ for all $m$ by solving \eqref{eq:t-delta-subproblem}.
		\ENDWHILE
		\ENDWHILE
		\STATE Normalize and update $\boldsymbol{W}$ via \eqref{Ww} and \eqref{Phase}.
		\STATE \textbf{Output:} $\overline{\overline{\boldsymbol{W}}}$ and $\widehat{\boldsymbol{t}}_m$.
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\subsection{Convergence Analysis}
We first consider the inner loop for a fixed $\mu$. The closed-form updates in \eqref{eq:u}, \eqref{eq:v}, and \eqref{eq:W-update} minimize their respective subproblems and hence do not increase $\mathcal L_{\mu}$. The projected Riemannian gradient update of $\boldsymbol{\varXi}$ satisfies the Armijo condition, while the one-flip update in \eqref{eq:one-flip-update} accepts a switch state only when it decreases \eqref{eq:t-delta-subproblem}. Moreover, \eqref{eq:delta-update} gives the optimal phase rotation for a fixed switch state. Therefore, the objective generated by the inner loop is non-increasing. Since $\mathcal L_{\mu}$ is bounded below, the inner loop is guaranteed to converge. We next consider the outer loop. According to \textbf{Algorithm~\ref{alg:modal-bound-pdd-rwmmse}}, $\mu$ is successively increased by a factor $\beta>1$, thereby progressively strengthening the penalty on the hardware-consistency residual in \eqref{eq:hardware-residual}. Therefore, the hardware-consistency residual approaches zero as $\mu\rightarrow\infty$ and the hardware constraint in \eqref{etaCons} can be satisfied when the  algorithm terminates.
\subsection{Computational Complexity}
Let $I_{\rm out}$ and $I_{\rm in}$ denote the numbers of outer and inner iterations, respectively. The outer loop only updates the scalar penalty coefficient according to \textbf{Algorithm~\ref{alg:modal-bound-pdd-rwmmse}}, and thus its complexity is $\mathcal O(1)$ per iteration.
Within each inner iteration, the CBP-WMMSE algorithm iteratively updates $u_k$, $v_k$, $\boldsymbol{W}$, $\boldsymbol{\eta}_m$, $\delta_m$, and $\boldsymbol{t}_m$. The updates of $u_k$ and $v_k$ mainly involve matrix multiplication and have a computational complexity of  $\mathcal O(KMT + MK^2)$. For the update of $\boldsymbol W$, forming $\boldsymbol G$ costs $\mathcal O(MK^2)$, while \eqref{eq:W-update} requires one $K\times K$ matrix inversion and the associated matrix multiplications. Therefore, the complexity of updating $\boldsymbol W$ is $\mathcal O(K^3+MK^2)$. For the update of $\boldsymbol{\varXi}$,  each gradient in \eqref{eq:eta-egrad} is evaluated in $\mathcal O(K^2MT)$ operations. Denoting the number of projected Riemannian gradient steps by $I_{\rm R}$, this block has complexity $\mathcal O(I_{\rm R}K^2MT)$. For the update of $\delta_m$, it has closed-form solution in \eqref{eq:delta-update} and mainly involve matrix multiplication with computational complexity $\mathcal{O}(T)$. For the update of $\boldsymbol{t}_m$, one round of the OBNS evaluates $Q$ neighbors for each PRA. Using \eqref{eq:woodbury-switch} and caching the current inverse, the complexity of one round is $\mathcal O(Q^2+QT)$ per PRA. Hence, $I_{\rm F}$ one-flip rounds for all PRAs cost $\mathcal O(I_{\rm F}M(Q^2+QT))$.
For the typical setting with $M>\max\{K,T\}$ and $Q>T$, the lower-order terms can be omitted. Combining the above variable updates, the overall computational complexity is
\begin{align}\label{eq:overall-complexity}
	\mathcal O\left(I_{\rm out}I_{\rm in}M
	\left(I_{\rm R}K^2T+I_{\rm F}Q^2\right)
	\right).
\end{align}

\subsection{Extensions}
The proposed algorithm can be readily extended to optimization under the ideal PRA model. Specifically, after removing the hardware-consistency constraint \eqref{etaCons} from \eqref{mbpra2} and replacing $\overline{\mathcal T}$ with the ideal feasible set $\mathcal T$ in \eqref{feasibleset},  $\boldsymbol{\eta}_m$ is independent of any specific hardware realization. Thus, $(\mathcal P_{\rm P}-2)$ shares the same problem structure as $(\mathcal P_{\rm I})$. By replacing $\boldsymbol{\eta}_m$ and $\boldsymbol{\varXi}$ with $\boldsymbol{\gamma}_m$ and $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}$, respectively, the remaining updates of $u_k$, $v_k$, $\boldsymbol W$, and $\boldsymbol{\gamma}_m$ provide an algorithm for solving the ideal-model problem $(\mathcal P_{\rm I})$.
More generally, the proposed algorithm establishes a unified framework that separates the original mixed-binary optimization into a continuous beamforming problem and a discrete hardware-approximation problem. The continuous part can incorporate existing beamforming methods developed for different objectives and system constraints~\cite{wang2026sparse}, while the Fourier coefficients $\boldsymbol{\eta}_m$ can be optimized using the projected Riemannian gradient method. The discrete part approximates the optimized Fourier coefficients using realizable switch states and can be efficiently handled by the proposed OBNS method. Since these two parts are connected only through the Fourier coefficients, they can be adapted independently. Therefore, the proposed framework can be extended to different beamforming objectives and PRA implementations without changing its overall decomposition structure.
\section{Simulation Results}\label{SR}
We compare the ideal and practical PRA models and evaluate the proposed CBP-WMMSE algorithm. Unless otherwise stated, the BS employs a half-wavelength uniform linear array with $M=32$ antennas and serves $K=4$ single-antenna users. Each user channel contains $L=6$ paths. The three RPA designs in Fig.~\ref{fig_image3} are considered under both the practical model and their corresponding ideal models. For the position-reconfigurable benchmark, the $M$ antennas are placed within a linear region of length $M\lambda$, subject to a minimum spacing of $\lambda/2$. The conventional antenna array has fixed antenna positions and radiation patterns.
\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=70mm]{Fig8/SNRSumRate.pdf}
	\caption{Sum rate versus SNR for different antenna models.}
	\label{fig_image8}
\end{figure}
Fig.~\ref{fig_image8} compares the sum-rates achieved by the different antenna models versus SNR. The ideal PRA models outperform the practical PRA models, which in turn outperform the position-reconfigurable antennas and the conventional antenna array. This ordering reflects the progressively reduced channel-reconfiguration flexibility of these models. The ideal models of RPA~2 and RPA~3 achieve nearly identical performance because the two designs have the same antenna aperture size. Among the practical models, RPA~2 achieves the highest sum rate, followed by RPA~3 and RPA~1. Although RPA~1 has more PIN diodes than RPA~3, its smaller aperture limits its pattern-reconfiguration capability. RPA~2 provides the best performance because it combines a larger aperture with a larger number of PIN diodes.
\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=70mm]{Fig9/Sum-Rate.pdf}
	\caption{Sum rate versus the number of antennas for different practical-model optimization algorithms.}
	\label{fig_image9}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=70mm]{Fig10/RunTime.pdf}
	\caption{Runtime versus the number of antennas for different practical-model optimization algorithms.}
	\label{fig_image10}
\end{figure}
Figs.~\ref{fig_image9} and~\ref{fig_image10} compare the sum-rate performance and runtime of the proposed algorithm with the fractional-programming-based alternating optimization (FP-Alt) algorithm in~\cite{Li2026AntennaCoding} and the successive exhaustive Boolean optimization (SEBO) algorithm in~\cite{Shen2026AntennaCoding}. The SEBO block size is set to one to reduce the simulation time, and WMMSE beamforming is performed during each SEBO search to maintain high-quality sum-rate solutions. The runtime is measured on an Intel Core Ultra~5 125H processor without parallel computing.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_image9}, the three algorithms achieve generally comparable sum rates, and their curves occasionally intersect as $M$ increases. This is reasonable because the joint beamforming and switch-state design is a nonconvex mixed-binary problem, and the algorithms may reach different locally optimal solutions. Nevertheless, the proposed algorithm provides competitive performance for all tested antenna numbers and RPA designs. In particular, it achieves the highest sum rate for RPA~3 throughout the considered range and provides the best result at most antenna numbers for RPA~2.
Fig.~\ref{fig_image10} shows that, for RPA~3 with only $Q=12$ switches, the proposed algorithm is slower than FP-Alt and SEBO because the additional continuous Fourier-coefficient optimization introduces noticeable overhead. When the number of switches increases to $Q=60$, as in RPA~1 and RPA~2, the advantage of the proposed algorithm becomes evident. Its runtime increases only moderately, whereas the runtimes of FP-Alt and SEBO increase substantially. For RPA~2 with $M=60$, the proposed algorithm requires approximately 1/12 of the runtime of SEBO, demonstrating its efficiency for densely switched RPAs.
\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=70mm]{Fig11/RPA2_search_benchmark.pdf}
	\caption{Objective value and runtime versus the number of switch-state evaluations for RPA~2.}
	\label{fig_image11}
\end{figure}
The discrete search is critical to the optimization of practical RPAs. Fig.~\ref{fig_image11} therefore compares the proposed OBNS, SEBO, and exhaustive search (ES) for minimizing the residual in \eqref{eq:phase-invariant-residual} for a single RPA~2. The runtime is measured on the same computing platform described above. OBNS initially decreases the objective more slowly than SEBO because it compares the complete one-bit neighborhood before updating the switch state. However, it eventually attains the lowest objective, indicating that its best-neighbor update leads to a better search trajectory for the considered problem. Moreover, although OBNS evaluates more candidates, its runtime remains substantially lower because it avoids repeated matrix inversions and reuses the inverse and intermediate quantities associated with the current switch state. At $1000$ evaluations, OBNS achieves a lower objective than SEBO and ES, while requiring only approximately $1/15$ and $1/60$ of their runtimes, respectively. These results demonstrate the effectiveness and computational efficiency of the proposed discrete search.
\section{Conclusion}\label{CON}
This paper investigated the modeling and beamforming optimization of PRA-enabled multiuser communication systems. We first interpreted pattern reconfiguration as complex reweighting of multipath components and distinguished it from the phase-only reweighting induced by position reconfiguration. We then developed a finite-aperture ideal PRA model by relating the antenna aperture to the effective Fourier order and deriving per-order power bounds on the Fourier coefficients. A multiport network model was further established for practical RPAs, and full-wave simulations together with three RPA designs verified the consistency between the ideal and practical models. For multiuser downlink transmission, we formulated WSR maximization problems under both models and developed a CBP-WMMSE algorithm for the practical mixed-binary problem. The proposed OBNS method, combined with the Sherman--Morrison update, enables efficient switch-state optimization without repeated matrix inversions. Simulation results showed that PRA-based channel reconfiguration provides clear sum-rate gains over position-reconfigurable and conventional antenna arrays, while the proposed algorithm achieves competitive performance with substantially reduced runtime for RPAs with a large number of switches.
