\section{Field Tests}
\label{sec:field}
\subsection{Field Test Settings}
We conducted the field tests in an indoor scenario as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:field_test}. Our IRS prototype machine consists of custom-designed printed circuit board (PCB) modules, each comprising a $10\times 10$ array of $100$ meta-atoms. Equipped with dual PIN diodes, each meta-atom is independently programmable to furnish four discrete phase-shift states: $\{0, \pi/2, \pi,3\pi/2\}$. The IRS operates at a center frequency of $3.5$ GHz with a $100$ MHz bandwidth. We employ the NI-USRP X410 SDR to instantiate both the transmitter and the receiver. A directional antenna is deployed at the transmitter, and a single omnidirectional antenna is deployed at the receiver. The system operates using a narrowband transmission scheme, emitting a $3.5$ GHz carrier with a $125$ KHz baseband bandwidth. The default transmit power is set to $P=5$ dBm. Baseband signals are sampled at a rate of $500$ KHz at the receiver. All the computations are conducted on a laptop with a 3.2 GHz CPU and 32 GB RAM.
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/cumulative_regret.pdf}
 \caption{Cumulative regret versus the number of blocks.}
 \label{fig:CRegret}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/regret_epsilon.pdf}
 \caption{Comparison of different $\epsilon$ values for $\epsilon$-Greedy algorithm.}
 \label{fig:diff_epsilon}
\end{figure}
Aside from CSM, $\epsilon$-greedy, and the proposed gradient sampling algorithm, we also consider a baseline method called \emph{random max sampling (RMS)}---which replaces the exploitation step in the CSM by using the best phase shift vector in the exploration stage, i.e.,
$\bm\theta^t=\arg\max_{\bm\theta\in\{\bm\theta^1,\ldots,\bm\theta^{T_0}\}} \mathrm{SNR}(\bm\theta).$
We set the learning rate $\gamma^t=\frac{0.005}{\sqrt{T}},\;t=1,2,\ldots,T$ for the proposed gradient sampling method. Regarding the $\epsilon$-greedy algorithm, we obtain $\epsilon^\star$ for the different $T$ values according to \eqref{opt:epsilon} as shown in Table \ref{tab:epsilog_T0_star}; we shall also try out other values of $\epsilon$ for comparison purposes. For the CSM and the RMS methods, we obtain $T_0^\star$ by rounding $\epsilon^\star T$ to the nearest integer; the values of $T_0^\star$ for the different $T$ values are also shown in Table \ref{tab:epsilog_T0_star}.
Moreover, we take $Q_n^{10000}(\varphi)$ as an approximation of $Q_n^\infty(\varphi)$ to compute the cumulative regret.
\subsection{SNR Enhancement Performance}
We start by considering the SNR enhancement performance of different algorithms. Fig. \ref{fig:CRegret} shows the cumulative regrets achieved by the various methods versus the number of blocks $T$. Observe that the RMS method yields the highest cumulative regret among the reinforcement learning methods, and the performance gap gradually increases with $T$. This is because the phase shifts used by RMS in the exploitation blocks are not selected to maximize CSM. In contrast, the CSM algorithm achieves a much lower cumulative regret. In addition, $\epsilon$-greedy slightly outperforms CSM when both methods use the same optimized exploration probability $\epsilon^\star$. It is also worth noting that the gradient sampling method significantly outperforms all other methods, although its objective is not specifically designed to minimize cumulative regret.
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/average_SNR.pdf}
 \caption{Average SNR versus the number of blocks.}
 \label{fig:avg_SNR}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/SNR_N.pdf}
 \caption{Average SNR versus the number of REs.}
 \label{fig:SNR_N}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/SNR_t.pdf}
 \caption{Visualize how the SNR changes with $t$ when $T=3000$.}
 \label{fig:SNR_visual}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig/SNR_CDF.pdf}
 \caption{CDF of the SNR when $T=3000$.}
 \label{fig:cdf}
\end{figure}
Next, we examine the impact of the exploration probability on the performance of the $\epsilon$-greedy method. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:diff_epsilon}, the cumulative regret is the highest when $\epsilon=0.1$ and $\epsilon=0.9$, which indicates that neither excessive exploration nor excessive exploitation is desirable. The $\epsilon$-greedy method with the optimized $\epsilon^\star$ achieves much better performance than the other choices of $\epsilon$, demonstrating the effectiveness of our strategy for optimizing $\epsilon$ based on the upper-bound approximation of cumulative regret.

Fig.~\ref{fig:avg_SNR} further demonstrates the average SNR across $T$ blocks for the different methods. As $T$ increases from $1000$ to $3000$, all methods show an increasing trend in average SNR, indicating that more blocks provide more observations for identifying effective phase shifts. Among all methods, gradient sampling consistently achieves the best performance. Its average SNR increases from about $19$ dB at $T=1000$ to nearly $23$ dB at $T=3000$. In contrast, RMS achieves the lowest SNR in all cases, suggesting that the phase shifts selected by RMS are less effective for SNR enhancement. CSM and $\epsilon$-greedy achieve comparable performance, but both remain clearly below gradient sampling. For example, when $T=3000$, their average SNR values are about $5$ dB lower than gradient sampling. These results demonstrate the advantage of gradient sampling in identifying high-quality phase shifts.
Fig. \ref{fig:SNR_N} shows the average SNR achieved by different algorithms versus the number of REs $N$. It can be observed that all methods achieve a higher average SNR with more REs. Moreover, gradient sampling consistently achieves the highest average SNR across all values of $N$. Its average SNR increases from about $14$ dB at $N=100$ to about $23$ dB at $N=400$. In comparison, the performance of CSM and $\epsilon$-greedy also improves with $N$, but the improvement is less significant than that of gradient sampling. RMS achieves the lowest performance among the four methods over the entire range of $N$. These results indicate that simply increasing the number of REs is not sufficient; an effective sampling strategy is also needed to fully utilize the enlarged IRS.
Finally, Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_visual} and Fig.~\ref{fig:cdf} illustrate the block-level SNR performance when $T=3000$. Specifically, Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_visual} shows how the SNR changes over block index $t$, while Fig.~\ref{fig:cdf} presents the corresponding CDF of the SNR samples. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:SNR_visual}, gradient sampling quickly improves the SNR in the early blocks and then maintains a high SNR of about $25$ dB, indicating that it can rapidly identify high-quality phase shifts. In contrast, CSM and RMS exhibit large fluctuations during the exploration stage and then converge to relatively stable SNR values of about $22$ dB and $19$ dB, respectively. The $\epsilon$-greedy method also improves with $t$, but it suffers from frequent low-SNR blocks due to random exploration. The CDF in Fig.~\ref{fig:cdf} confirms these observations from a distributional perspective. The curve of gradient sampling is shifted farthest to the right, and most of its SNR samples are above $23$ dB, whereas CSM and RMS have a considerable portion of samples below this level. Although $\epsilon$-greedy can reach high SNR values, it has a wider SNR distribution and a longer low-SNR tail.
