\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we propose a deep learning-based predictive beamforming algorithm for integrated sensing and communications in vehicle-to-infrastructure networks, where echo signals are leveraged to enhance communication performance.
We formulate a sum spectral efficiency maximization problem under a sensing signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) constraint and construct a penalty-based unconstrained surrogate objective for network training.
Based on this formulation, the beamforming matrix is predicted using sensing echoes and historical beamformers from previous time slots.
To this end, we develop a predictive beamforming network that processes sensing echoes and beamforming inputs through separate encoders to preserve their modality-specific characteristics. This separate design enables each modality to learn rich and complementary representations which are more effectively exploited through a cross-attention mechanism compared to naive input fusion.
The resulting features are fused via cross-attention, while gated recurrent units capture temporal dependencies across time slots.
Simulation results validate the performance of the proposed method.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Beamforming, deep learning (DL), integrated
sensing and communications (ISAC), and modality.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
Connected and autonomous vehicles, as a key application of beyond 5G (B5G) and 6G networks, demand ultra-reliable gigabit-level links and centimeter-level sensing accuracy to enable advanced services such as autonomous driving and intelligent transportation systems \cite{liu2022survey,charan2021vision}.
However, conventional architectures that deploy separate base stations (BSs) for communications and sensing suffer from inefficient spectrum usage and high hardware cost.
Integrated sensing and communications (ISAC) addresses these limitations by unifying both functions \cite{meng2023sensing,li2024framework,choi2024joint,park2025covariance}.
In the ISAC system, sensing echo signals can be exploited to facilitate predictive beamforming without necessitating pilot symbols \cite{liu2020radar,ying2021particle}, thereby reducing overhead for communications;
the Doppler frequency and time delay information extracted from sensing echoes reflect the kinematic state of the vehicle, enabling  tracking and prediction.
In \cite{liu2020radar}, an extended Kalman filter (EKF)-based predictive beamforming scheme was proposed to utilize sensing echoes for communications, and in \cite{ying2021particle}, a scheme based on the particle filter (PF) was developed to address the non-linearities and non-Gaussian noise in the trajectories of vehicles.
These filter-based methods typically depend on intensive matched filtering and recursive state tracking, which  incur significant processing overhead.
To reduce processing overhead and enhance performance, deep learning (DL)-based predictive beamforming methods were proposed \cite{liu2022learning,zhang2023deep,zhang2024transformer}.
For example, \cite{liu2022learning} proposed a long short-term memory (LSTM)-based algorithm that takes historical channel matrices as inputs, bypassing the tracking process.
However, relying on channel matrices still necessitates parameter estimation and channel reconstruction from the sensing echoes, and the sequential processing of the LSTM architecture restricts parallel computation, which increases both training time and real-time inference latency.
To overcome these limitations, \cite{zhang2024transformer} proposed a transformer-based method that directly generates the predictive beamforming matrix without explicit channel reconstruction, where the sensing echo and the beamformer from the previous time step are simply concatenated into a single input.
This naive fusion makes it difficult to preserve and effectively utilize the distinct characteristics of each echo and beamformer feature, and relying exclusively on a single previous step provides insufficient temporal information to capture dynamic channel variations, so that the prediction performance degrades significantly as the vehicle speed increases.
In this regard, we are motivated to develop an architecture that decouples sensing echoes and beamformers to explicitly extract their  features, while capturing the  temporal dependencies across multiple time slots  for high-mobility scenarios.
In this paper, we propose a DL-based predictive beamforming framework for vehicular ISAC networks, where sensing echoes are  exploited to elevate communication performance.
We first formulate a sum spectral efficiency (SE) maximization problem under a sensing  SINR constraint, which is then relaxed into a penalty-based unconstrained surrogate objective for DL training.
Driven by this formulation, we develop a predictive beamforming network that leverages sensing echoes and historical beamformers across previous time slots, explicitly decoupling these inputs to preserve their modality-specific characteristics.
This decoupled design extracts  mobility-related features from the echoes and prior beamforming decisions from historical beamformers.
Subsequently, a cross-attention mechanism effectively fuses these distinct features, while gated recurrent units (GRUs) capture the temporal dependencies across multiple time slots.
Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed framework consistently outperforms state-of-the-art benchmarks.
