\section{Cross-attention-based Predictive Beamforming  for ISAC}
To facilitate gradient-based training within a DL framework, we enforce the transmit-power constraint by normalizing the network output and incorporate the sensing SINR constraint through a penalty term. Accordingly, we construct the following unconstrained penalized surrogate objective for the original constrained problem.
To effectively solve this reformulated problem, we develop a predictive beamforming framework comprising two distinct architectures: the cross-attention GRU predictive beamforming network (C-GRU-PB-Net) and the cross-attention predictive beamforming network (C-PB-Net), where we first introduce C-GRU-PB-Net as the general architecture and present C-PB-Net as its simplified special case.
\subsection{Proposed C-GRU-PB-Net Framework}
Using the penalty method \cite{liu2022learning,zhang2024transformer}, we reformulate the optimization problem in \eqref{eq:reformulated opt1} as
\begin{align}
\nonumber
 &\mathop{{\text{maximize}}}_{{\bf F}_{n}} \;\;
 {\mathbb{E}}_{{\bf H}_n|{\bf  E}_n^{\tau},{\bf F}_n^{\tau}}\left[\sum_{k = 1}^{K}\log_2\left(\!1+\frac{|{\bf h}_{k,n}^{\sf H}{\bf f}_{k,n}|^2}{\sum_{i\neq k}^{K}|{\bf h}_{k,n}^{\sf H}{\bf f}_{i,n}|^2+\frac{\sigma^2}{P}}\right)\!\right]

 &\qquad\qquad\;\;-\lambda_1\! \sum_{k = 1}^{K}\!\left(\min\!\left(0, {\frac{\xi_{k,n}({\bf F}_n)}{{\Gamma}_{k,n}}-1}\right)\!\right)^2\!\!\!,
 \label{eq:unconstraintproblem}
\end{align}
where $\lambda_1$ represents the penalty parameter which determines the magnitude of the penalty term.
{The transmit-power penalty is removed by normalizing the network output, which guarantees \eqref{eq:TXpow} while fully utilizing the available power to improve both the communication SE and
sensing SINR.}
To effectively optimize this reformulated problem, we propose a DL architecture designed to directly predict the  beamforming matrix by  processing sensing echoes and historical beamformers as distinct input modalities.
The specific structure of the proposed architecture is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:systemmodel} and detailed as follows.
\subsubsection{Input Layer}
The complex-valued input is decomposed into its real and imaginary parts and represented as separate features $ \bar{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}={\mathcal T}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Re}\{{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}\},\mathrm{Im}\{{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}\}\bigr)$ and $\bar{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\tau}={\mathcal M}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Re}\{{\bf F}_n^{\tau}\},\mathrm{Im}\{{\bf F}_n^{\tau}\}\bigr)$.
Here, ${\mathcal{T}}(\cdot): {\mathbb{C}^{B \times  K\times L \times  \tau}}\xrightarrow{}{\mathbb{R}^{B\times  2K \times L \times \tau}}$ and ${\mathcal{M}}(\cdot): {\mathbb{C}^{B\times N_t\times K \times \tau  }}\xrightarrow{}{\mathbb{R}^{B\times 2N_t\times K\times \tau }}$ represent the mapping functions, where  $B$ is  the batch size.
\subsubsection{CNN Module}
To extract spatial features from the partitioned inputs, $ \bar{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}$ and $\bar{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\tau}$  are fed into separate convolutional neural network (CNN) modules.
Each module consists of 1-D convolutional layers and a ReLU activation function, denoted by $\varphi(x) = {\mathrm{max}(0, x)}$.
The extracted features of the echo signals and beamformers are given by ${\mathbf{\tilde E}}_{n}^{\tau} = \varphi(\text{CNN}({\mathbf{\bar E}}_{n}^{\tau}))$ and $\mathbf{{\tilde F}}_{n}^{\tau} = \varphi(\text{CNN}(\mathbf{{\bar F}}_{n}^{\tau}))$, respectively.
\subsubsection{Cross-attention Module}
To effectively integrate  the separate modalities of echo and beamformer features, we employ  multi-head cross-attention.
{The feature maps extracted from the echo are linearly projected to form the
query matrix ${\bf Q}^{e}$, while the beamformer features are used to form the
key and value matrices ${\bf K}^{b}$ and ${\bf V}^{b}$, respectively, as
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:projection}
 {\bf Q}^{e}
 ={\mathbf{\tilde E}}_{n}^{\tau}{\bf W}_Q,
 {\bf K}^{b}
 ={\mathbf{\tilde F}}_{n}^{\tau}{\bf W}_K,
 {\bf V}^{b}
 ={\mathbf{\tilde F}}_{n}^{\tau}{\bf W}_V,
\end{align}
where
${\mathbf{\tilde E}}_{n}^{\tau}\in\bbR^{N_{\rm e}\times d_{\rm e}}$ and
${\mathbf{\tilde F}}_{n}^{\tau}\in\bbR^{N_{\rm b}\times d_{\rm b}}$,
and the learnable projection matrices satisfy
${\bf W}_Q\in\bbR^{d_{\rm e}\times Hd_{\sf att}}$ and
${\bf W}_K,{\bf W}_V\in\bbR^{d_{\rm b}\times Hd_{\sf att}}$.
Thus, all projections share the same per-head dimension $d_{\sf att}$ for
$H$ attention heads, allowing the query and key features to be multiplied
despite the different token numbers $N_{\rm e}$ and $N_{\rm b}$.}
Subsequently, scaled dot-product attention is used to compute the attention output as
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:attention}
 {\mathrm{Score}({\bf Q}^{e},{\bf K}^{b},{\bf V}^{b})} = \mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{{\bf Q}^{e}({\bf K}^{b})^{\sf T}}{\sqrt{d_{\sf att}}}\right){\bf V}^{b},
\end{align}
where $\mathrm{softmax}(\cdot)$ denotes the normalization operation  and $d_{\sf att}$ denotes the dimensionality of each attention head.
\begin{algorithm} [t]
\caption{Proposed  Predictive Beamforming} \label{alg:pro}
{\bf{Initialize}}: $t_1 = 0$, random weights $\omega$, {initial penalty weight $\lambda_1^{(0)}$}, and maximum number of iterations $N_{\sf max}$
{\bf{ Offline Training:}}

{\hspace*{1em}\bf{Input:}}
Simulated training trajectories and initial beamformers

\hspace*{2em}\While {$t_1$ $<$ $N_{\sf max}$}{\hspace*{2em}Update $\omega$ to minimize $Loss(\omega)$ in \eqref{eq:Lossfunction}
\hspace*{2em}{Update $\lambda_1$ using \eqref{eq:lambda_update}}
\hspace*{2em}$t_1 \leftarrow t_1+1$}
{\hspace*{1em}\bf{Output:}}{ Well-trained $f_{\omega^{\star}}(\cdot)$}

{\bf{Online Inference:}}

{\hspace*{1em}\bf{Input:}}
{Initial beamformer ${\bf F}_0$ from beam acquisition and} real-time input sequence $(\ddot{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}, \ddot{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\tau})$

\hspace*{2em}{\bf{do}} {Beamforming prediction  using $f_{\omega^{\star}}(\cdot)$ }

{\hspace*{1em}\bf{Output: }}{Predicted beamforming matrix ${\bf F}^{\star}_n$ }
\end{algorithm}
\subsubsection{GRU Module}
In the GRU module, a single GRU unit is recurrently applied to the past $\tau$ time slots, i.e., from time slot $(n-\tau)$ to $(n-1)$.
The outputs of the cross-attention module at each time slot are used as the inputs to the GRU in order to capture the temporal correlation of the attention scores, and the hidden state at time slot $(n-1)$ is used as the output of the GRU module, as it captures the temporal dependencies of the historical echo signals and beamformers over the past $\tau$ time slots.
\subsubsection{FC Layer}
To leverage the features extracted by the GRU, its output is fed into a fully connected (FC) layer with linear activation to generate the desired output.
Finally, the optimized beamforming matrix predicted by the proposed algorithm is given by
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:output}
 {{\mathbf F}_{n}=\frac{{\tilde{\mathbf F}}_{n}}{\sqrt{{\rm Tr}({\tilde{\mathbf F}}_{n}{\tilde{\mathbf F}}_{n}^{\sf H})}},}  \quad
 {\tilde{\mathbf F}}_{n}={\mathcal C}({f}_{\omega}(\bar{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}, \bar{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\tau})).
\end{align}
Here, $f_{\omega}(\cdot)$ denotes the non-linear transformation of the proposed network parameterized by $\omega$ and  $\mathcal C(\cdot)$ represents the mapping function that constructs the complex-valued matrix.
\begin{remark}[Role of Separate Processing in Cross-Attention and GRU]\normalfont
Echo features act as the query to reflect mobility cues such as Doppler frequency and time delay, which describe vehicle motion, whereas beamformer features serve as the key and value to represent past transmission decisions.
Separate processing of these modalities preserves their modality-specific characteristics before fusion, so that the attention scores indicate how mobility-related echo features align with prior beam decisions.
Furthermore, robustness under high mobility is enhanced by the GRU, which combines these attention-based features over multiple time steps and captures long-term dynamics.
\label{Crossattention}
\end{remark}
\subsection{Proposed  C-GRU-PB-Net Algorithm}
The proposed method operates in two phases: offline training and online inference. For training, the model learns the mapping from sensing echo and beamforming sequences.
For inference, the trained model processes a sequence of sensing echoes and beamforming pairs collected over the previous $\tau$ time slots, and directly predicts the beamforming matrix.
\subsubsection{Offline Training Phase}
The proposed network is trained using mini-batches of $B$ trajectory samples generated by the offline simulator.
For the $j$th sample, we denote the historical sensing-echo and beamformer sequences available at prediction slot $n$ by $(\hat{\bf E}_{n}^{\tau,(j)},\hat{\bf F}_{n}^{\tau,(j)})$.
{Starting from the initial beamformer, the historical beamformers are sequentially generated by the network, and the corresponding sensing echoes are generated according to the sensing signal model.
The training is solver-label-free, i.e., no ground-truth beamformer
obtained from an optimization solver is required. The simulator
generates the vehicle trajectory and the corresponding channel
and sensing parameters, which are used to evaluate the communication
and sensing performance of the predicted beamformer.}
Accordingly, the training loss is expressed as
\begin{align}
 \nonumber
 &Loss(\omega) =\!-\frac1B\!\sum_{j=1}^{B}\!\sum_{k=1}^{K}\!\log_2\!\left(1+\frac{|({\bf h}_{k,n}^{(j)})^{\sf H}{\bf f}_{k,n}^{(j)}(\omega)|^2}{\sum_{i\neq k}^{K}|({\bf h}^{(j)}_{k,n})^{\sf H}{\bf f}_{i,n}^{(j)}(\omega)|^2+\sigma^2/P}\right)

 &\qquad\quad+\lambda_1\frac{1}{B}\sum_{j=1}^{B}\sum_{k=1}^{K} \left(\min\!\left(0, {\frac{\xi_{k,n}({\bf F}^{(j)}_n(\omega))}{\Gamma_{k,n}}-1}\right)\right)^2\!\!\!,
 \label{eq:Lossfunction}
\end{align}
where ${\bf f}_{k,n}^{(j)}(\omega)$ denotes the $k$th column of  ${\bf F}_n^{(j)}(\omega)$ for the $j$th training sample.
{Since $\mathbf{F}_n^{(j)}(\omega)$ is the normalized output, the training naturally accounts for the power normalization.}
The network parameters ${\omega}$ are updated iteratively via the backpropagation algorithm to minimize the loss function.
{In addition, $\lambda_1$ is adaptively updated according to the sensing
performance  as
\begin{align}
 \label{eq:lambda_update}
 \lambda_1^{(t+1)}
 =
 \lambda_1^{(t)}
 +\lambda_1^{(0)}\bar g^{(t)},
\end{align}
where
$\bar g^{(t)}
=
\frac{1}{BK}\sum_{j=1}^{B}\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\max\left(0,1-\frac{\xi_{k,n}^{(j)}}{\Gamma_{k,n}}\right)$
denotes the average sensing violation and
$t$ denotes the training iteration.}
Consequently, with the trained network parameters $\omega^{\star}$, the trained model can be represented as $f_{\omega^{\star}}\left(\hat{\bf  E}_{n}^{\tau}, \hat{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\tau}\right)$.
\subsubsection{Online Inference}
{At the initial time slot, the initial beamformer ${\bf F}_0$ is obtained through beam acquisition.
Until $\tau$ echo-beamformer pairs are accumulated, the first $\tau-1$ beamformers are generated with the GRU bypassed, after which the latest $\tau$ pairs are used through a sliding window.
Hence, except for ${\bf F}_0$, the historical beamformers are the previous outputs of the trained network rather than ideal reference beamformers.
The trained model processes these real-time input sequences $(\ddot{\mathbf E}_n^{\tau}, \ddot{\mathbf F}_n^{\tau})$ to generate the predictive beamforming matrix as
\begin{align}
 {\mathbf F}^{\star}_{n}
 =
 \frac{\tilde{\mathbf F}^{\star}_{n}}
 {\sqrt{\mathrm{Tr}\!\left(
 \tilde{\mathbf F}^{\star}_{n}
 \tilde{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\star\sf H}
 \right)}}, \quad
 \tilde{\mathbf F}^{\star}_{n}
 =
 {\mathcal C}\!\left(
 {f}_{\omega^{\star}}
 (\ddot{\bf E}_{n}^{\tau},\ddot{\mathbf F}_{n}^{\tau})
 \right).
\end{align}
where the learned parameters $\omega^{\star}$ are fixed during inference.
Since the BS acquires the sensing echo of every transmission from the physical environment, each prediction reflects the current vehicle state, which helps mitigate the accumulation of prediction errors.
Accordingly, beam acquisition is not required at every time slot and needs to be repeated only when persistent sensing degradation indicates loss of tracking.}
The overall proposed algorithm is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:pro}.
\subsection{Special Case: C-PB-Net}
C-PB-Net retains the separate feature extraction branches and the cross-attention module of C-GRU-PB-Net, while using only the sensing echo and beamformer from the previous time slot $(n-1)$ as  inputs.
It thus removes the GRU-based temporal aggregation and predicts the beamforming matrix for the subsequent time slot based on the fused feature of the previous-slot echo-beamformer pair, thereby providing a lower-complexity alternative to C-GRU-PB-Net.
