\section{Simulation results}
The path loss exponent, carrier frequency, complex radar cross-section, and number of past time slots are set to $\eta = 2$, $f_c = 10$ GHz, $\phi = 10 + 10j$, $\lambda_1^{(0)}=500$, and $\tau = 4$, respectively.
We placed the transmit BS at the origin.
The BS-to-user vehicle distances are within $50$ m, and the vehicle velocities range from $50$ km/h to $70$ km/h.
For training, the batch size is set to $B = 64$, and the dataset consists of 4096 training samples and 512 test samples, where the test samples are used to evaluate online inference in the simulation environment.
{We considered the following benchmarks for comparison: 1) Random beamforming (RBF), 2) EKF-maximum ratio transmission (EKF-MRT) \cite{liu2020radar}, 3) EKF-regularized zero-forcing (EKF-RZF), 4) LSTM-based network (HCL-Net) \cite{liu2022learning},  5) Self-attention transformer network (ECT-Net) \cite{zhang2024transformer}, and 6) Mamba-based predictive beamforming network (Mamba-PB-Net), which replaces the cross-attention and GRU modules by a selective state-space model \cite{shi2026channelmamba}}.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:sumrate}, we set $N_t=32$, $N_r=32$, $K=4$, and
$\Gamma_{k,n}=10$ dB.
Fig.~\ref{fig:sumrate}(a) and (b) show the sum SE and sensing SINR,
respectively, while Fig.~\ref{fig:sumrate}(c) shows the attention scores
between sensing echoes and beamformers from previous time slots.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:sumrate}(a) and (b), the proposed
C-GRU-PB-Net/C-PB-Net achieves higher sum SE and sensing SINR than the
considered baselines while satisfying the sensing SINR constraint.
Mamba-PB-Net improves upon HCL-Net and ECT-Net by effectively capturing
temporal dependencies through the selective state-space model, while
C-GRU-PB-Net further improves upon C-PB-Net by exploiting temporal
correlations through the GRU.
In practice, constructing the spatially filtered echo $e_{k,n}(t)$ in
\eqref{eq:rxecho} requires additional preprocessing such as angle estimation
and spatial filtering.
We therefore also consider the raw received echo ${\bf r}_n(t)$ as the network
input.
Although using ${\bf r}_n(t)$ degrades the overall communication and sensing
performance, the proposed C-GRU-PB-Net exhibits a smaller degradation than the
learning-based baselines and maintains its performance advantage.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:sumrate}(c), compared with ECT-Net, the proposed
network exhibits more structured attention patterns that better capture the
echo-beamformer correlation.
Moreover, its attention map becomes more concentrated as the transmit power
increases, reflecting the improved reliability of the sensing echo, whereas
ECT-Net shows less variation.
Overall, these results demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of the
proposed C-GRU-PB-Net in exploiting echo-beamformer relationships for predictive beamforming.
{In Fig.~\ref{fig:velocity}, we evaluate the effect of the vehicle mobility by sweeping the velocity from $10$ to $60$ km/h.
As the velocity increases, the channel varies more rapidly between consecutive slots, which makes beam prediction more challenging.
The learning-based baselines and C-PB-Net gradually degrade, whereas C-GRU-PB-Net maintains relatively stable performance owing to the GRU module that captures the temporal dependencies across multiple time slots, so that its advantage becomes more pronounced at higher velocities.}
{In Fig.~\ref{fig:array}, we plot the communication and sensing performance with
respect to the number of antennas by considering
$N_t=N_r\in\{8,16,32,64,128\}$.
Both the communication and sensing performance improve as the number of antennas
increases, since a larger transmit array provides increased array gain, while a
larger receive array improves spatial resolution and reduces the residual
inter-beam leakage in \eqref{eq:rxecho}.
Across all considered array sizes, the proposed C-GRU-PB-Net consistently
achieves the highest sum SE while satisfying the sensing threshold, and exhibits
a smaller degradation than the learning-based baselines when the raw echo is
directly used as input.
These results indicate that the proposed framework is well suited for future
6G systems employing massive MIMO \cite{isac2026fr3}.}
Finally, we evaluate the computational complexity in terms of floating-point operations (FLOPs), which quantify the number of arithmetic operations required for inference.
Using the default hyperparameters in our implementation, the estimated inference complexity per sample is approximately 2.71 MFLOPs for ECT-Net, 2.62 MFLOPs for C-PB-Net, and 3.12 MFLOPs for C-GRU-PB-Net.
Hence, C-PB-Net achieves both lower inference complexity and higher performance than ECT-Net, and C-GRU-PB-Net further improves the performance with only a modest increase in FLOPs.
\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed a DL-based predictive beamforming framework for vehicular ISAC networks that explicitly decouples sensing echoes and historical beamformers to preserve their modality-specific characteristics.
We formulated a sum SE maximization problem under a sensing SINR constraint and converted it into a DL training loss function.
The decoupled features are then fused through cross-attention, while GRUs capture their temporal evolution across multiple time slots.
Simulation results confirmed that the proposed framework outperforms the considered model-based and learning-based baselines while satisfying the sensing requirement.
The proposed framework therefore provides a practical predictive beamforming solution with reduced pilot overhead and low inference complexity for connected and autonomous vehicular networks.
