\begin{abstract}
This paper studies waveform-level security for \gls{isac}. Instead of
relying on spatial beamforming or power allocation, we randomize the
sensing waveform itself using phase keys and tangent \gls{an} applied to a
Fourier-curve constellation. Coordinated legitimate nodes know the key and
recover the sensing reference, while Eve observes only a randomized
waveform and cannot construct the correct coherent matched reference. We
consider a \gls{uav}-mounted Eve with a dominant \gls{los} link who fully
exploits favorable geometry, including a bypass scenario where Eve listens
to both the direct and echo sides to circumvent our design. We further
consider a stronger Eve that attempts phase-key reconstruction from
long-term observations; even then, residual key mismatch structurally
degrades correlation and \gls{an} cancellation, while accurate key recovery
incurs high computational complexity. Numerical results show that the
proposed design reduces Eve's probability of detecting the sensing
reference using non-coherent detection and, in the bypass case,
substantially suppresses her correct-delay lock probability, while the
legitimate receiver reliably locks onto the reference through echo
accumulation.
\end{abstract}
\glsresetall
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
\glsentryshort{an}, \glsentryshort{isac}, B5G, 6G, Security. 
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
\gls{isac} is a promising paradigm for next-generation wireless networks,
reusing radio resources for both data transmission and environmental
sensing within a unified system~\cite{liu2020isac}, thereby improving
spectral efficiency and reducing system cost toward large-scale,
sustainable sensing services. However, its dual-purpose design introduces a physical-layer security
risk: parties aware of the resource structure, whether external
eavesdroppers or authorized \glspl{cu}, may monitor sensing
activity or exploit repeated sensing references to perform unauthorized
tracking or identification. 
\gls{an} is a classical way to degrade an unintended receiver without
relying solely on higher-layer
encryption~\cite{goel2008artificial}. Most secure-\gls{isac} designs
combine \gls{an} with beamforming, steering useful energy toward legitimate
nodes while injecting \gls{an} into the remaining spatial directions to
degrade
Eve~\cite{zou2024securing,xu2022robust,su2024sensing,jia2024physical,ren2023robust},
with some made robust to bounded uncertainty in her location or
channel~\cite{xu2022robust,ren2023robust}, and others jointly optimizing
beamforming and \gls{an} covariance to make multi-target echoes less
distinguishable~\cite{huang2026isacprivacy}. However, a \gls{uav}-borne Eve is not confined to a bounded region:
she can loiter, hover, and actively reposition to explore the
surrounding geometry, converging on a vantage point with a dominant
\gls{los} link and favorable beamforming gain. Ambiguity-function
engineering offers an alternative via ghost
targets~\cite{han2026sensingsecure}, but these decoys neither hide the real
echo nor conceal the sensing activity itself, so prolonged observation still
lets Eve isolate the true target.
This motivates our central question: can the sensing reference be hidden
from Eve while remaining decodable to the legitimate receiver, e.g., a
cooperating \gls{bs}? Unlike spatial-domain defenses that control where
energy is radiated, we protect what waveform is observed. We use a Fourier-curve constellation
protected by phase keys and tangent \gls{an}: the legitimate receiver,
coordinated over secure backhaul (BS--BS), knows the per-burst phase key
and recovers the reflected sensing waveform, while Eve or a compromised
\gls{cu}, holding only the communication-domain key, sees only a
randomized waveform. Meanwhile, we consider an \gls{uav}-borne Eve who,
exploiting a strong \gls{los} link to the transmitter and the target, can
bypass the waveform-domain protection by comparing the direct
and reflected signals, without needing to recover the phase key. 
Without the phase keys needed to isolate the true sensing reference from the
transmitted structure, Eve, though able to exploit favorable geometry,
remains limited to single-echo observation: her correct-delay lock
probability stays low, whereas the legitimate receiver coherently integrates
echoes using the shared schedule and known reference. With phase-key
reconstruction, Eve can perform coherent integration across bursts, but her
performance remains limited by residual phase-key error and imperfect
\gls{an} removal, while accurate key recovery incurs high computational
complexity.
We consider an \gls{ofdm} \gls{isac} system using \gls{qpsk} for
sensing/control symbols and higher-order modulation for payload under link
adaptation, consistent with \gls{5gnr} \cite{3gpp38211}. The rest of this
paper is organized as follows. Section~II presents the secure communication
and sensing model. Section~III describes the threat model and detection
metrics. Section~IV reports the numerical results, and Section~V concludes
the paper. 
\section{Secure Communication and Sensing Model}
\subsection{Fourier-Curve Modulator}
Let $M=2^q$. Every group of $q$ coded bits selects an index
$i\in\{0,\ldots,M-1\}$, mapped to a phase
$\theta_i=\frac{2\pi i}{M}$.
We map the conventional $M$-PSK phase $\theta_i$ to a $k$-dimensional
harmonic symbol vector,
\begin{equation}
\bm{x}_i=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
\begin{bmatrix}
 e^{j\theta_i} & e^{j2\theta_i} & \cdots & e^{jk\theta_i}
\end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf T},
\qquad \|\bm{x}_i\|=1.
\label{eq:curve_symbol_vector}
\end{equation}
The $k$ components are jointly determined by the single index $i$, and may
be mapped to parallel subcarriers, orthogonal signaling dimensions, or
successive resource elements depending on the physical implementation.
Differentiating $\bm{x}_i$ with respect to $\theta_i$ traces the local
direction of the symbol-vector curve as $i$ varies, with
$\bm{x}'(\theta_i)=\frac{j}{\sqrt{k}}
\begin{bmatrix}
 e^{j\theta_i} & 2e^{j2\theta_i} & \cdots &
 ke^{jk\theta_i}
\end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf T}$. Normalizing by its norm $v_k=\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\ell^2}$, 
gives the unit tangent vector at $\bm{x}_i$,
\begin{equation}
\hat{\bm{t}}_i=\frac{\bm{x}'(\theta_i)}{v_k},
\qquad \|\hat{\bm{t}}_i\|=1.
\label{eq:unit_tangent}
\end{equation}
The tangent is orthogonal to the symbol vector,
$\Re\{\bm{x}_i^{\mathsf H}\hat{\bm{t}}_i\}=0$, once each complex vector is
identified with its real embedding in $\mathbb{R}^{2k}$. This confirms that
$\hat{\bm{t}}_i$ describes a purely rotational (phase) perturbation of
$\bm{x}_i$, with no component along $\bm{x}_i$ itself.
\subsection{Tangent \glsentryshort{an} and Channel}
Before \gls{an} injection, the transmitter applies a shared phase-key vector
\(
\bm{\varphi}=[\varphi_1,\ldots,\varphi_k]^{\mathsf T}
\)
across the $k$ harmonic components, giving the keyed Fourier-curve symbol vector
\begin{equation}
\bm{x}_i(\bm{\varphi})
 =\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
\begin{bmatrix}
 e^{j(\theta_i+\varphi_1)} &
 e^{j(2\theta_i+\varphi_2)} &
\cdots &
 e^{j(k\theta_i+\varphi_k)}
\end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf T}.
\label{eq:keyed_symbol_vector}
\end{equation}
The keyed tangent $\hat{\bm{t}}_i(\bm{\varphi})$ follows by differentiating
$\bm{x}_i(\bm{\varphi})$ with respect to $\theta_i$. The legitimate receiver
knows $\bm{\varphi}$ and builds the keyed codebook directly; an
unintended receiver without the key faces a geometry mismatch even before
\gls{an} is considered.
For transmission $r$, the transmitter draws one real scalar
$\xi_r\sim\mathcal N(0,1)$, shared across all $k$ harmonic components
\begin{equation}
\bm{x}^{\tx}_{i,r}
 =\sqrt{1-\beta}\,\bm{x}_i(\bm{\varphi})
 +\sqrt{\beta}\,\xi_r\hat{\bm{t}}_i(\bm{\varphi}),
\qquad 0\leq\beta<1.
\label{eq:protected_symbol_vector}
\end{equation}
The tangent direction is deterministic for a fixed $i$, while the signed
amplitude $\xi_r$ is random and drawn independently for each
symbol vector, not each component. Since $\bm{x}_i(\bm{\varphi})$ and
$\hat{\bm{t}}_i(\bm{\varphi})$ are unit-norm and orthogonal with
$\E\!\left[\|\bm{x}^{\tx}_{i,r}\|^2\right]=1$. 
After synchronization and channel equalization, the real-valued received
symbol vector is
\begin{equation}
\bm{Y}_r=\sqrt{1-\beta}\,\bm{x}_i(\bm{\varphi})
 +\sqrt{\beta}\,\xi_r\hat{\bm{t}}_i(\bm{\varphi})+\bm{N}_r,
\label{eq:received_model}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{Y}_r,\bm{x}_i,\hat{\bm{t}}_i,\bm{N}_r\in\mathbb{R}^{2k}$ and
$\bm{N}_r\sim\mathcal N(\bm{0},\sigma_c^2\bm{I}_{2k})$. The total symbol-vector
SNR is
$\rho=\frac{\E[\|\bm{x}^{\tx}_{i,r}\|^2]}
 {\E[\|\bm{N}_r\|^2]}=\frac{1}{2k\sigma_c^2}
\label{eq:snr_definition}$. 
\subsection{QPSK Sensing-Waveform Design}
We consider QPSK phase sequences
\begin{equation}
s[n] = e^{j\theta_n}, \theta_n \in \left\{0, \frac{\pi}{2}, \pi, \frac{3\pi}{2}\right\}, n = 0, \ldots, N-1.
\label{eq:qpsk}
\end{equation}
Each $s[n]$ is mapped to the weighted subcarrier symbol vector
$\bm{u}_k[n]$, as demonstrated in Eq.~\ref{eq:curve_symbol_vector}. Its entries are loaded onto the active subcarriers $\mathcal{K}$ of an $N_{\rm FFT}$-point OFDM symbol (inactive subcarriers set to zero) and converted to the time-domain sensing burst $\bm{x}_n$ by the unitary inverse OFDM transform.\footnote{That is, $\bm{x}_n = \bm{F}^{\mathsf H}\bm{U}_n$, where $\bm{U}_n\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\rm FFT}}$ satisfies $\bm{U}_n[\mathcal{K}]=\bm{u}_k[n]$, $\bm{U}_n[\mathcal{K}^c]=\bm{0}$, and $\bm{F}$ is the unitary DFT matrix.} Because $\bm{F}$ is unitary and $\bm{U}_n$ is supported only on $\mathcal{K}$, Parseval's theorem gives $\bm{u}_k[n]^{\mathsf H}\bm{u}_k[n-\tau] = \bm{x}_n^{\mathsf H}\bm{x}_{n-\tau}$, so correlating the weighted subcarrier symbol vectors is equivalent to correlating the transmitted time-domain bursts. The resulting subcarrier-combined harmonic autocorrelation
\begin{equation}
J_k(\bm{\theta})
=
\sum_{\tau\neq 0}|R_k[\tau]|^2
+
\lambda
\max_{\tau\neq 0}|R_k[\tau]|^2 ,
\label{eq:qpsk_objective}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\theta}=[\theta_0,\ldots,\theta_{N-1}]^{\mathsf T}$ collects the \gls{qpsk} phases of the sensing sequence. The first term penalizes total sidelobe energy and the second the peak sidelobe, with $\lambda$ controlling their tradeoff; we set $\lambda=10$ throughout to emphasize \gls{psr}. Since the phase alphabet is discrete, the resulting optimization is combinatorial; we solve it via discrete coordinate descent with random restarts, repeated per harmonic order $k$ and summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:qpsk_waveform_design}.
The nominal sensing sequence is phase-keyed and protected by tangent
\gls{an}. Cooperating BSs are synchronized, and the burst schedule and
key-\gls{an} pair $(\bm{\varphi}_{\mathrm{s}},\xi)$ are shared over secure
backhaul. The receiver correlates the known reference $\bm{x}$ over the
expected delay window. After compensating known front-end and
channel distortions, the sensing waveform is reconstructed, with remaining
uncertainty from residual timing error, further reduced through
echo accumulation. For communication, the \gls{bs} and \gls{cu} instead share a
communication-domain key $\bm{\varphi}_{\mathrm{c}}$ \cite{han2026matched}, instantiating the
same keyed lookup table of Fourier-curve symbol vectors and tangent directions,
enabling covariance-aware demodulation without knowledge of the
\gls{an} realization. This is to protect the sensing
reference from both external eavesdroppers and \glspl{cu}.
\begin{algorithm}[]
\scriptsize
\caption{\glsentryshort{qpsk} harmonic-waveform design for fixed $k$}
\label{alg:qpsk_waveform_design}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE Choose $N$, $k$, restarts $R$, sweeps $S$; define $\mathcal{Q}=\{0,\pi/2,\pi,3\pi/2\}$ and objective $J_k(\bm{\phi})$ in \eqref{eq:qpsk_objective}.
\FOR{$r=1,\ldots,R$}
\STATE Initialize $\bm{\phi}^{(r)}$ (structured or random seed).
\FOR{$s=1,\ldots,S$}
\STATE \texttt{improved} $\leftarrow$ false.
\FOR{each chip $n$ (random order)}
\STATE Set $\phi_n\leftarrow\arg\min_{\phi\in\mathcal{Q}}J_k$, others fixed; if $J_k$ decreased, \texttt{improved} $\leftarrow$ true.
\ENDFOR
\IF{\texttt{improved} = false}
\STATE \textbf{break} (stop restart $r$).
\ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\STATE Store best sequence from restart $r$.
\ENDFOR
\STATE Return the best sequence overall (or all within tolerance of it).
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\subsection{Covariance-Aware Demodulation}
\subsubsection{Unknown-\glsentryshort{an} Maximum Likelihood Receiver}
Unlike the sensing case, where the waveform $\bm{x}$ is known, communication has no such prior: the candidate
$i$ is itself the unknown, so its tangent direction $\hat{\bm{t}}_i$ is
unknown until $i$ is decided. The receiver therefore marginalizes $\xi_r$
rather than estimating it, giving
\begin{equation}
\bm{Y}_r\mid i\sim\mathcal N\!\left(
\sqrt{1-\beta}\bm{x}_i,\bm{\Sigma}_i
\right),
\end{equation}
with $\bm{\Sigma}_i=\sigma_c^2\bm{I}_{2k}
 +\beta\hat{\bm{t}}_i\hat{\bm{t}}_i^{\mathsf T}
\label{eq:covariance}$. 
The \gls{an} increases variance only along candidate $i$'s tangent. Since all
tangents have unit norm, with
$\det(\bm{\Sigma}_i)
 =(\sigma_c^2)^{2k-1}(\sigma_c^2+\beta)$
is independent of $i$. Define $\bm{r}_i=\bm{Y}_r-\sqrt{1-\beta}\bm{x}_i$.
Applying the Sherman--Morrison identity yields the candidate metric
\begin{equation}
\Lambda_i(\bm{Y}_r)
 =\frac{\|\bm{r}_i\|^2}{\sigma_c^2}
 -\frac{\beta(\bm{r}_i^{\mathsf T}\hat{\bm{t}}_i)^2}
 {\sigma_c^2(\beta+\sigma_c^2)}.
\label{eq:ml_metric}
\end{equation}
The hard decision is $\hat{i}=\arg\min_i\Lambda_i$. The first term is the
ordinary Euclidean metric; the second reduces the penalty when the residual
lies along the \gls{an} direction expected under candidate $i$. The receiver thus
accounts for the candidate-dependent covariance rather than erasing or
estimating the \gls{an} sample, a correction the Euclidean baseline omits.
Let $b_a(i)$ denote bit position $a\in\{0,\ldots,q-1\}$ in the binary label
of candidate $i$. For coded control and payload channels, the
covariance-aware soft demapper forms
\begin{equation}
L_{r,a}=\log
\frac{\sum_{i:b_a(i)=0}\exp[-\Lambda_i(\bm{Y}_r)/2]}
{\sum_{i:b_a(i)=1}\exp[-\Lambda_i(\bm{Y}_r)/2]} .
\label{eq:llr}
\end{equation}
Both sums are evaluated with log-sum-exp for numerical stability; positive
$L_{r,a}$ favors bit zero. A standard rate-$1/2$ \gls{ldpc} code with
belief-propagation decoding is used only for control and payload, which
have explicit \gls{ber} targets. The sensing waveform remains uncoded to
preserve the optimized phase sequence; the receiver instead correlates with
the known sensing reference.
\section{Threat Model}\label{sec:thmodel}
We assume Eve (i) performs non-coherent detection on the direct and
reflected signals to bypass the system, (ii) exploits long-term
observations to attempt cross-burst coherent reference reconstruction,
and (iii) searches the phase-key space to reconstruct phase-keys. If the
key estimate is sufficiently accurate, Eve can recover the nominal
sensing sequence and form a coherent reference. This is a stronger
assumption than Eve merely knowing the waveform-generation structure,
since a newly generated nominal-like sequence remains misaligned with the
observed protected waveform. Cyclostationary detection based on repeated burst
timing can be mitigated by randomizing the sensing-burst schedule, which is
shared with the legitimate receiver over secure backhaul. Since \gls{ofdm} subcarrier spacing is public
numerology observable from the direct link, Eve could apply standard
Doppler compensation without knowledge of the sensing sequence; moreover,
for typical target/\gls{uav} velocities the Doppler shift is small
relative to the subcarrier spacing regardless. We therefore adopt a
delay-only detection model.
\gls{uav}-to-\gls{uav} and
\gls{uav}-to-ground links at typical operating altitudes and
frequencies are predominantly \gls{los}, with multipath components
attenuated well below the direct path due to the elevated,
obstruction-free geometry. Under this assumption, both the direct and
reflected links are well approximated as single-tap
(\glsentryshort{los}-dominant) channels. This LOS-only, single-tap model
represents a best-case channel for Eve and thus yields a favored detection performance for Eve. With
\begin{equation}
h_d[n]\approx g_d\,\delta[n-\tau_d],
\quad
h_r[n]\approx g_r\,\delta[n-\tau_r],
\end{equation}
where $g_d,g_r$ are the complex path gains and $\tau_d,\tau_r$ are fixed
propagation delays. Absorbing $\tau_d$ into the time reference and defining
$\ell_0\triangleq\tau_r-\tau_d$ as the delay of interest, the convolutions
$(h_d*x)[n]$ and $(h_r*x)[n-\ell_0]$ collapse to simple scaled shifts,
$g_d\,x[n]$ and $g_r\,x[n-\ell_0]$, respectively. Under a delay-only bypass
model, the direct-path signal and reflected-path signal are $d[n]$ and $r[n]$, and the two hypotheses are then
\begin{equation}\begin{split}
\mathcal{H}_0:&
\begin{cases}
d[n]=g_d\,x[n]+u[n],
r[n]=w[n],
\end{cases}
\mathcal{H}_1:
&\begin{cases}
d[n]=g_d\,x[n]+u[n],
r[n]=g_r\,x[n-\ell_0]+w[n],
\end{cases}\end{split}
\label{eq:eve_bypass_hyp}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{H}_0$ is the null hypothesis that no target-generated
echo is present at Eve, $\mathcal{H}_1$ is the target-present
hypothesis, $x[n]$ is the transmitted sensing burst, and
$u[n],w[n]\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_w^2)$ are independent \gls{awgn} terms.
Under $\mathcal{H}_0$, the detection threshold is set for the required
false-alarm rate $P_{\mathrm{FA}}$. We then assess under $\mathcal{H}_1$ whether
Eve detects the target echo and estimates its delay.
Eve uses the direct path as a
surrogate and computes the cross-correlation statistic
\begin{equation}
 z[\ell]=\sum_n r[n]\,d^*[n-\ell],
\qquad \ell=0,\dots,L-1,
\label{eq:matched_filter_delay}
\end{equation}
followed by the peak-search rule
\begin{equation}
\max_{\ell} |z[\ell]| \underset{\mathcal{H}_0}{\overset{\mathcal{H}_1}{\gtrless}} \eta,
\qquad
\hat\ell=\arg\max_\ell |z[\ell]|.
\label{eq:peak_search}
\end{equation}
$\eta$ is the non-coherent detection
threshold applied to the correlation magnitude.
Since $\ell_0$ is neither known nor grid-aligned, Eve must search candidate
bins; sidelobes then affect only the rate of spurious crossings at incorrect
bins, not the correct-bin statistic itself, so the two effects are treated
separately below. Define the normalized autocorrelation of the sensing
burst,
\begin{equation}
\chi[\ell] \triangleq \frac{1}{E_s}\sum_n x[n]\,x^*[n-\ell],
\qquad E_s=\sum_n|x[n]|^2.
\label{eq:ambiguity_def}
\end{equation}
\subsubsection{Noise Model} $z[\ell]$ correlates two noisy signals rather
than one noisy signal against a clean reference.
The noise cross-term $w[n]u^*[n]$ contributes equally under both hypotheses,
so any asymmetry in $\sigma_z^2$ would have to come from the signal-noise
cross term $g_r\sum_n x[n-\ell_0]u^*[n]$ under $H_1$; We further restrict attention
to the regime of a strong direct link, so that
$d[n]\approx g_d x[n]$ acts as a quasi-clean surrogate reference and this
cross-noise term is negligible. Under this approximation the noise variance
is dominated by the echo channel noise variance $\sigma_w$ alone, with
$\sigma_z^2 \approx |g_d|^2\sigma_w^2 E_s$, 
which recovers the standard complex-Gaussian form
\begin{equation}\begin{split}
 &z[\ell_0]\mid \mathcal{H}_0 \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_z^2),
 &z[\ell_0]\mid \mathcal{H}_1 \sim \mathcal{CN}(g_rg_d^* E_s\chi[0],\,\sigma_z^2).
\end{split}\label{eq:pd_statistic}
\end{equation}
\subsubsection{Correct-Bin and False-Lock Probabilities}
As before,
\begin{equation}\begin{split}
P_{\mathrm{corr}}&=\Pr\!\big(\hat{\ell}=\ell_0,\ |z[\hat{\ell}]|>\eta
\mid\mathcal{H}_1\big),
P_{\mathrm{false}}&=\Pr\!\big(\hat{\ell}\neq \ell_0,\ |z[\hat{\ell}]|>\eta
\mid\mathcal{H}_1\big),
\label{eq:pcorr_pfalse}\end{split}
\end{equation}
with $P_D=P_{\mathrm{corr}}+P_{\mathrm{false}}$ and
$P_{\mathrm{miss}}=1-P_D$. 
\subsubsection{Role of the Mainlobe}
Ignoring competition from other bins, the
mainlobe alignment $|\chi[0]|^2$ bounds correct-bin detection,
\begin{equation}
 P_{\mathrm{corr}} \;\lesssim\;
 Q_1\!\left(
\sqrt{\frac{2|g_rg_d|^2 E_s^2\,|\chi[0]|^2}{\sigma_z^2}},
\sqrt{-2\ln P_{\mathrm{FA}}}
\right),
\label{eq:pd_explicit}
\end{equation}
which reduces to the classical matched-filter result under a perfectly clean
direct link and ideal waveform, and is an upper bound rather than the true $P_{\mathrm{corr}}$.
\subsubsection{Role of the Sidelobes}
$P_{\mathrm{false}}$ is governed by the
off-peak values $\chi[\ell]$, $\ell\neq0$: a large sidelobe at offset
$\ell'\neq0$ gives mean response $g_rg_d^*E_s\chi[\ell']$ at bin
$\ell_0+\ell'$, which can cross $\eta$ and inflate $P_{\mathrm{false}}$, and
hence $P_D$, without improving ranging accuracy. A lower-sidelobe
waveform can exhibit \emph{lower} $P_D$ than a higher-sidelobe one while
achieving equal or higher $P_{\mathrm{corr}}$, and why the peak sidelobe
ratio,
$\mathrm{PSR}=\frac{1}{\max_{\ell\neq0}|\chi[\ell]|^2},
\label{eq:psr_def}$
together with $|\chi[0]|^2$, characterizes ranging reliability more
completely than $P_D$ alone. 
