\section{Numerical Results}
\subsection{Validation of Secure Communication}
Here we validate the communication security design. The sensing and control
channels use \gls{qpsk} for reliability and sensing performance, while the
payload channel uses higher-order modulation for throughput. We consider a
naive Eve with a Euclidean demapper and an architecturally informed Eve
(\gls{cu}) using a covariance-aware demapper but lacking the correct key. Independent Monte Carlo realizations are used for tangent
\gls{an} and phase keys, with default $\beta=0.5$, following the representative setting used
in~\cite{han2026matched}.
Figure.~\ref{fig:uncoded_qpsk_combined} shows an uncoded \gls{qpsk}
harmonic-dimension sweep to demonstrate the security of the sensing reference
signal. In general, increasing $k$ decreases \gls{ber} for both demodulators,
since the added harmonics enrich the signal's phase features and redundancy. The
covariance-aware demodulator outperforms the Euclidean baseline, and the gap
widens with increasing harmonic dimension, as the candidate-dependent noise
covariance becomes more pronounced and thus more exploitable at higher $k$.
Once the waveform is keyed, the \gls{ber} of a receiver lacking the
phase key remains high even with the covariance-aware demodulator, while the
legitimate receiver, which possesses the key, sees no degradation in
performance. Figure~\ref{fig:ldpc_combined} compares the $1/2$ \gls{ldpc}-coded 64-PSK
decoding performance at $4$ and $8$ dB. The overall trends match the uncoded
case. Euclidean decoder performs worse compared to
the previous \gls{qpsk} scenario, which follows from the higher-order
constellation itself: with more candidate points packed into the same
unit-energy space, the minimum Euclidean distance between neighboring
symbols shrinks, making the Euclidean metric more sensitive to
noise and to the \gls{an} perturbation, independent of any keying effect.
The results indicate that communication can be protected by the
proposed design, with Eve's \gls{ber} limited to $0.5$ without
phase key. Higher $k$ reduces \gls{ber} but at the cost of spectral
efficiency. For sensing and control signals, which are typically short,
spectral efficiency is less relevant; control signals in particular require
security and reliability, motivating a high $k$ combined
with \gls{ldpc} coding for ultra-low \gls{ber}.
\subsection{Validation of waveform design and legitimate sensing}
Next, we validate the sensing waveform design in a cooperating BS--BS case.
The waveform-only results in Table~\ref{tab:opt_vs_random_corr} show that the
optimized sequence achieves higher \gls{psr} than the random baseline, with
\gls{psr} around $20$~dB for most $k$. The dips at $k=4$ and $k=8$ are a
structural effect of the QPSK alphabet: for $\phi_n = i\pi/2$, the harmonic
sum $\sum_{m=1}^{k} e^{jm\phi_n}$ has period $4/\gcd(i,4)$ in $m$ and
vanishes exactly when $k$ is a multiple of that period, leaving only
$\phi_n=0$ non-degenerate and sharply reducing waveform diversity. For
$k>M$, these harmonic phases wrap modulo $2\pi$ and repeat cyclically.
