\subsection{validation of secure sensing}
Finally, we evaluate secure sensing performance, considering the threat
from Section~\ref{sec:thmodel} where Eve observes the transmitted signal
over a long period and attempts cross-burst coherent reference
reconstruction. We transmit the same nominal sequence multiple times with
independent \gls{an} realizations and phase keys. Eve cross-correlates the
received signals to detect potential sensing signals, the detection rate is $P_C$. Two
random-sequence baselines isolate the contributions of keying, \gls{an},
and waveform optimization: unprotected ($\textbf{Rand. No}$ in
Table~\ref{tab:keyed_burst_beta}), and keyed with \gls{an} $\beta=0.5$
($\textbf{Rand. A+K}$). As Table~\ref{tab:keyed_burst_beta} shows, the
unprotected optimized sequence ($\textbf{Opt. No}$) gives $P_C=1.0$ versus
$0.05$ for the unprotected random baseline. Keying alone ($\beta=0$) drops
$P_C$ to $0.386$; adding \gls{an} drops it further, falling below the
unprotected random baseline by $\beta\approx0.3$ and reaching $P_C=0$ from
$\beta\approx0.6$, matching the keyed-and-\gls{an} random baseline. To
isolate the role of key randomization itself, the $\textbf{Opt. A+SK}$
entry considers optimized bursts with the same phase key but
independent \gls{an} realizations ($\beta=0.5$); its high $P_C$ confirms that phase-key
updates, not \gls{an} alone, is essential for suppressing
$P_C$ for Eve. Notably, the keyed optimized \gls{psr} remains
much higher: \gls{psr} reflects correlation \emph{shape}, while $P_C$
depends on peak \emph{magnitude} relative to the noise floor. Independent
keys disrupt the constructive alignment behind the optimized peak,
collapsing its magnitude even as the shape (and \gls{psr}) stays favorable,
so high \gls{psr} cannot rescue detection once the mainlobe is suppressed.
The comparison of \textbf{Opt. $0.1\pi$} and \textbf{Opt. $0.25\pi$}
evaluates waveform-based detection when Eve has estimated the phase keys.
When the average phase-key error increases to $\sigma_\phi=0.25\pi$, Eve can
barely identify the sequence through waveform-based detection. With such low $P_C$, the sensing reference can be made
difficult for Eve to detect and reconstruct, or the system can
further randomize transmit timing and deliberately send keyed random
sequences with \gls{an} to confuse Eve.
In Tab.~\ref{tab:key_mismatch_ser_k5}, Eve jointly evaluates phase-key
candidates and their corresponding sensing-symbol decisions from multiple
observations, then identifies the signal by comparing the recovered sequences.
Matching success generally decreases nonlinearly with \gls{ser}. We omit an
explicit matching-error model and focus on low mismatch, neglecting this
additional loss in Eve's favor. For a uniform-grid search with resolution
$\Delta_\phi=\sigma_\phi\pi$, each coefficient has
$Q\approx\lceil2/\sigma_\phi\rceil$ candidates, yielding $Q^k$ candidate keys.
We assume that Eve scores each key by demodulating all $N$ QPSK symbols. Since
four constellation metrics are evaluated per symbol, the attack requires
$C_{\rm Eve}=4NQ^k=4N\lceil2/\sigma_\phi\rceil^k$ metric evaluations per burst,
as reported in Table~\ref{tab:key_mismatch_ser_k5}. This symbol-wise detector
avoids enumerating the $Q^k4^N$ hypotheses of a naive joint search over every
key and symbol sequence.
