Next, Fig.~\ref{fig:pd_k2_k6_compare} considers the bypass case, where Eve
listens on the direct path while attempting to detect the reflected path.
For tractability, we assume Eve always has \gls{los} to both the
transmitter and target, with the direct path $4$--$8$~dB stronger than
the reflected path. We evaluate the sensing reference against a
random-sequence baseline, both keyed with \gls{an}, where direct- and
reflected-path observations from the same burst share the same key and
\gls{an} realization. In Fig.~\ref{fig:pd_k2_k6_compare},
both $P_{\mathrm{corr}}$ and $P_D$ are similar for the random and the
optimized sequences. This follows because Algorithm~1 optimizes the
mainlobe/sidelobe trade-off for the clean, unkeyed, \gls{an}-free case
through a sensitive phase alignment across chips; keying and \gls{an}
disrupt this alignment severely, bringing performance down to the level
of a random sequence of the same length.
However, Eve's performance can be limited by
controlling transmit power: employing $k = 5$ relaxes the \gls{snr}
requirement for coherent sensing because the legitimate receiver correlates
against the already-known sequence. 
For \textbf{Eve without phase-key reconstruction}, as established in
Table~II, independent key refreshing prevents Eve from constructing a stable
cross-burst coherent reference, limiting her to single-echo observation. The
red arrow marks $P_{\mathrm{corr}}$ at $1$~dB average reflected signal:
Eve's $P_{\mathrm{corr}}$ remains below $0.1$ (marked with the black arrow),
while the legitimate receiver reaches $0.92$ with power accumulation of $10$ echoes. This result
assumes Eve knows the legitimate receiver's location and exploits its beamforming gain, representing a best-case detection scenario for
Eve. For \textbf{Eve with phase-key reconstruction}, with
$\sigma_\phi=0.1\pi$ and assuming sequence-mismatch error is neglected,
Eve's $P_{\mathrm{corr}}$ is generally lower than that of the legitimate
receiver; the gap is most prominent at high \gls{snr}. This stems from two
compounding effects of the phase-key mismatch: the residual phase error
directly degrades correlation, and it simultaneously worsens \gls{an}
cancellation, further corrupting the observation. Both effects are structural
and persist after accumulating multiple observations. At $\sigma_\phi=0.1\pi$, exhaustive search requires Eve to test about
$3.20\times10^6$ key hypotheses for a single burst within a short observation
time. Since the phase keys are randomly and independently refreshed, this
cost cannot be amortized over multiple bursts, making accurate key recovery
computationally demanding.
