\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
This paper studied waveform randomization for protecting sensing references
in \gls{isac} systems using Fourier-curve modulation, phase keys, and
tangent \gls{an}. The results show that Eve's sensing capability can be
strongly suppressed even when she exploits both direct- and reflected-path
observations under favorable geometry. We further considered phase-key
reconstruction by Eve and showed that residual key mismatch and imperfect
\gls{an} removal still limit coherent sensing, while accurate key recovery
incurs high computational complexity.
Future work will investigate joint optimization of the phase key,
tangent-\gls{an} power, and harmonic dimension $k$ under explicit sensing
accuracy and communication reliability constraints, and will validate the
design in multi-target and multi-Eve scenarios closer to practical B5G/6G
deployments.
