\begin{abstract}
Downlink channel state information (CSI) feedback is essential for beamforming optimization in frequency-division duplex (FDD) massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems. However, the feedback overhead increases with the number of antennas and subcarriers, posing a major challenge to practical deployment. Although recent deep learning (DL)-based methods reduce this overhead by compressing CSI at the user equipment (UE) and reconstructing it at the base station (BS), most of them treat CSI as a generic image and rely on convolutional or Transformer architectures. As a result, the low-rank multipath prior is insufficiently exploited, while non-negligible computation is introduced at both the UE and the BS. To address this issue, we propose a low-rank prior (LRP)-guided  CSI feedback framework that performs structured sensing and reconstruction directly over rank-one channel components. Specifically, the LRP encoder conducts learnable rank-one sensing to obtain path-aware measurements at the UE, while the LRP decoder reconstructs CSI through rank-one synthesis at the BS, avoiding the iterative recovery required by classical solvers. To compensate for finite-rank approximation residuals, we extend our previous DCRNet into DCRNetV2 by incorporating the proposed LRP backbone and gated dilated-convolutional residual branches. Experiments on multiple datasets and scenarios show that the proposed methods achieve a better accuracy-complexity tradeoff than existing model-based and DL-based baselines. In particular, standalone LRP achieves more than $10$~dB NMSE improvement over model-based baselines with much lower complexity, while DCRNetV2 achieves comparable accuracy to state-of-the-art Transformer-based methods with only about one-fifth of the complexity. The source code is available at \url{https://github.com/tangshunpu/DCRNetV2}.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Massive MIMO, CSI feedback, low-rank prior, deep learning, FDD systems.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Background}
Massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) is a key technology for next-generation wireless networks, as it can substantially improve spectral and energy efficiency~\cite{larsson2014massive}. By equipping the base station (BS) with many antennas, massive MIMO enables spatial multiplexing and directional beamforming to serve multiple users simultaneously. To fully exploit these gains, the BS requires accurate instantaneous downlink channel state information (CSI) for beamforming design~\cite{love2008overview}. In time-division duplex (TDD) systems, the BS can obtain downlink CSI from uplink pilots via channel reciprocity. In frequency-division duplex (FDD) systems, however, such reciprocity generally does not hold, and thus the user equipment (UE) must estimate the downlink CSI and feed it back through a limited uplink control channel~\cite{sim2016compressed}. In wideband orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) systems with large antenna arrays and many subcarriers, the CSI matrix becomes high-dimensional, making direct feedback impractical. 
Early studies applied compressed sensing to reduce the feedback overhead by exploiting channel sparsity~\cite{gao2018compressive}. However, these methods rely on sparsity assumptions that are sensitive to model mismatch, and their iterative inverse-problem solvers incur high BS-side latency and energy consumption~\cite{qin2018sparse}. Recently, deep learning (DL)-based methods have learned CSI compression and reconstruction in an end-to-end manner without explicit sparsity assumptions~\cite{CsiNet,guo2022overview}. Most of them treat CSI as a generic image and use convolutional or Transformer-based autoencoders, achieving higher reconstruction accuracy than conventional model-based methods, especially at high compression ratios~\cite{CsiNetPlus,TransNet}. However, their considerable computation at both the UE and the BS limits their use in resource-constrained devices and massive machine-type communications (mMTC) scenarios. Although lightweight networks~\cite{DCRNet,CLNet} reduce this cost, they often sacrifice reconstruction accuracy.
To address this issue, recent works have explored model-driven DL, which incorporates  channel-structure priors into network design~\cite{cao2023deepunfolding,he2019modeldriven}. These priors reduce the need for over-parameterized networks and improve interpretability. However, many model-driven methods still rely on NN-based iterative solvers at the BS, keeping the overall complexity high. More importantly, the low-rank multipath prior of CSI has not been fully exploited~\cite{Joint_Sparse_and_Low_Rank,Low_rank_and_Sparse_Channel_Estimation}. Under standard far-field geometric massive MIMO-OFDM models, the angular-delay CSI is dominated by a small number of separable delay-angular path responses, each of which contributes a rank-one component. Thus, the channel is naturally low-rank or approximately low-rank~\cite{Vlachos2019Wideband,zhou2017lowranktensor,shao2022exploiting}, providing a principled way to reduce both UE- and BS-side complexity. This motivates a CSI feedback architecture in which the low-rank prior (LRP) serves as the main mechanism for user-side compression and BS-side reconstruction, while lightweight neural refinement is used only to compensate for components not captured by the low-rank model.
\subsection{Related Works}
\subsubsection{Model-based CSI feedback}
Early CSI feedback designs in FDD massive MIMO followed the compressed-sensing paradigm. The user side applies a random projection to the vectorized angular-delay channel, and the BS recovers the channel by solving a sparse-recovery problem \cite{rao2014distributed}. Specifically, orthogonal matching pursuit (OMP)~\cite{omp_ref} can be used to reconstruct the CSI by iteratively selecting the most correlated sensing vector,  estimating its strength, and removing its effect until the main sparse channel components are recovered. Iterative shrinkage-thresholding algorithm (ISTA) and its accelerated variant fast-ISTA (FISTA)~\cite{fista} solve a relaxed $\ell_1$-regularized least-squares problem through iterative shrinkage. Although these methods are interpretable, their performance degrades under model mismatch, where the sparsity assumption does not always hold, and their complexity grows quickly with the antenna and subcarrier dimensions, which limits their applicability to large-scale systems.
\subsubsection{DL-based CSI feedback}
To overcome the limitations of model-based methods, CsiNet~\cite{CsiNet} pioneered DL-based CSI feedback by compressing the angular-delay-domain CSI with a convolutional autoencoder and reconstructing it at the BS, achieving clear gains over classical compressed sensing. Following this paradigm, subsequent works improved the accuracy–complexity tradeoff from different perspectives. Specifically, the authors in \cite{Cao2021Lightweight} reduced user-side complexity using channel-shuffle-based lightweight convolutions. CRNet~\cite{CRNet} extracted multi-resolution features with different receptive fields, ACRNet~\cite{ACRNet} combined aggregated residual convolutions with a width-scaling rule and DCRNet~\cite{DCRNet} used dilated convolutions to enlarge the receptive field with limited extra parameters. In addition, the authors in \cite{Sun2021Lightweight,CLNet} exploited the correlation between real and imaginary CSI components to reduce redundant computation. However, these lightweight designs usually have limited feature extraction capability, which restricts their reconstruction performance.
To pursue higher reconstruction accuracy, CsiNet+\cite{CsiNetPlus} enlarged the convolutional kernels and deepened the autoencoder to strengthen nonlinear feature extraction. Following this idea of increasing model capacity, Transformer-based methods\cite{Cheng2024SwinCFNet,TransNet} were introduced to capture long-range dependencies in CSI, and TCLNet~\cite{TCLNet} further combined convolutional blocks with Transformer modules to exploit both local structures and global interactions. More recently, large-model-based CSI feedback has also been explored. CSI-LLM~\cite{LLMCsiNet,Cui2025LLM,Guo2026PromptEnabled} reformulates CSI feedback as a sequence-to-sequence learning problem and uses an LLM to map the CSI representation into a compact feedback codeword. The authors in \cite{WiFoCF, Guo2026PromptEnabled} further extend this direction by introducing wireless foundation models, where a large model is pre-trained on diverse channel datasets and then adapted to different out-of-distribution scenarios, demonstrating strong generalization capability. Although these methods achieve improved reconstruction accuracy and robustness, they usually rely on deeper networks, attention modules, or large pre-trained models, leading to significantly higher computational cost.
\subsubsection{Model-driven DL for CSI feedback}
To combine the interpretability of model-based methods and further improve the accuracy of DL-based methods, model-driven DL~\cite{he2019modeldriven} has been developed for CSI feedback. Specifically, L2O methods~\cite{ms4l2o} unfold classical iterative solvers into trainable networks, thereby preserving an interpretable recovery process. Based on this idea, CSI-L2O~\cite{Ma2025LowComplexity} integrates compressive sensing with L2O, using a learnable linear projection at the UE for low-complexity compression and an NN-based iterative solver at the BS for CSI reconstruction. Similarly, FISTA-Net unfolds FISTA with adaptive thresholding for sparse CSI recovery~\cite{Guo2022ModelDriven}, while MarkovNet and DIFNet exploit temporal correlation through differential feedback and unfolding designs~\cite{9513579,Zhang2023ModelDriven}. Moreover, IdasNet removes redundant CSI patches through a self-information model before compression and recovery~\cite{9768327}. Although these methods improve CSI feedback efficiency by incorporating channel priors and optimization structures, their BS-side iterative reconstruction still introduces non-negligible computational cost~\cite{Chen2025CSICompression}.
\subsubsection{Low-rank perspective}
The low-rank property of CSI has been widely recognized in channel acquisition and estimation because wideband massive MIMO channels are usually generated by a small number of scattering clusters, where each path contributes a separable delay-angular response~\cite{Vlachos2019Wideband,Joint_Sparse_and_Low_Rank,zhou2017lowranktensor,shao2022exploiting}. Existing works exploit this property from different perspectives. The authors in \cite{Shen2015Joint} formulated joint multi-user CSI acquisition as a low-rank matrix completion problem by using the correlation among scheduled users. The authors in \cite{Joint_Sparse_and_Low_Rank, zhou2017lowranktensor} used low rank together with sparsity or tensor decomposition for channel estimation, while the work in ~\cite{wei2021randomized} compressed CSI by feeding back sampled submatrices and reconstructing the full channel through randomized low-rank approximation. More recently, Guo et al.~\cite{Guo2022ModelDriven} proposed a two-stage low-rank feedback scheme that decomposes CSI into a basis part and a linearly represented residual part, followed by unfolded FISTA-based reconstruction. These studies demonstrate the value of the low-rank prior, but they mainly use it as a completion constraint, a submatrix approximation, or a reconstruction-side decomposition. Moreover, the sensing and reconstruction processes are not fully co-designed according to the path-wise low-rank structure, and still rely on iterative solvers at the BS.
\subsection{Contributions}
In this paper, we revisit CSI feedback from a low-rank perspective and propose a model-driven DL framework that explicitly embeds the low-rank multipath prior into both the encoder and the decoder. Instead of treating CSI feedback as a generic image compression task, we formulate it as a structured sensing and reconstruction problem over rank-one channel components. At the user side, the proposed LRP module performs learnable rank-one matched filtering over the angular-delay CSI, producing path-aware measurements with much lower complexity than convolutional encoding. At the BS side, LRP reconstructs the CSI through rank-one synthesis from the feedback codeword, which keeps the main reconstruction path interpretable and avoids the explicit reconstruction of the full CSI matrix  equired by classical solvers. To address finite-rank approximation errors and local residual structures beyond the rank-one prior, we further extend our previous DCRNet into DCRNetV2, which attaches gated dilated-convolutional residual branches to the LRP backbone while preserving the rank-one path as the dominant representation. The main contributions are summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}
 \item We formulate CSI feedback as structured sensing and reconstruction over rank-one channel components and propose LRP. At the UE side, LRP performs learnable rank-one sensing with paired delay- and angular-domain sensing vectors, while at the BS side it reconstructs CSI through low-rank synthesis from rank-one factors. This design makes the dominant multipath prior explicit, jointly optimizes sensing and reconstruction, and avoids the iterative full-matrix recovery used by classical solvers.
 \item We design DCRNetV2 by attaching dilated-convolutional residual branches to the LRP backbone without replacing the rank-one main path. The DCR encoder, factored residual decoder, and final refinement module compensate for finite-rank approximation errors, off-grid leakage, and local cluster residuals, enabling the model to scale from lightweight to high-accuracy variants while preserving the physical interpretability of LRP.
 \item  We introduce Sionna-RT-Mix5, a new ray-tracing benchmark for CSI feedback that covers five outdoor urban and campus scenes with diverse LoS and NLoS propagation conditions. We further include a held-out Shenzhen CBD scenario to evaluate out-of-distribution generalization and adaptation with limited fine-tuning data.
\item We conduct extensive experiments on COST 2100, Sionna-RT-Mix5, and real FDD massive MIMO data to compare the proposed methods with representative model-based and DL-based baselines. We also measure on-device inference latency and energy consumption to evaluate their practical efficiency. The results show that standalone LRP achieves more than $10$~dB NMSE improvement over model-based baselines with much lower complexity, while DCRNetV2 achieves comparable accuracy to state-of-the-art Transformer-based methods with only about one-fifth of the FLOPs.
\end{itemize}
