\section{System Model}\label{sec:system_model}
We consider an FDD massive MIMO-OFDM system, where the BS is equipped with \textcolor{black}{$N_t \gg 1$ transmit antennas} and the UE has a single antenna over $N_c$ subcarriers. The downlink received signal at the $n$-th subcarrier can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{receive signal}
 y_n=\bm{h}_{n}^H\bm{b}_n x_n+z_n,
\end{align}
where $\bm{h}_n \in \mathbb{C}^{N_t \times 1}$ denotes the complex-valued CSI vector for the $n$-th subcarrier, $\bm{h}_n^H$ denotes its conjugate transpose, $\bm{b}_n \in \mathbb{C}^{N_t \times 1}$ is the beamforming vector, $x_n$ is the transmitted symbol, and $z_n \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^2)$ is the complex additive noise. The CSI across all subcarriers can be collected as $\bm{H}=[\bm{h}_1,\bm{h}_2,\cdots,\bm{h}_{N_c}]^T \in \mathbb{C}^{N_c\times N_t}$, which contains $2N_c N_t$ real-valued coefficients.
To obtain a better quality of service, the UE needs to feed back the downlink CSI $\bm{H}$ to the BS, which then uses it to optimize the beamforming vectors $\{\bm{b}_n\}$. However, directly feeding back $\bm{H}$ would require $2N_c N_t$ real-valued feedback symbols, which is impractical for large $N_c$ and $N_t$ given the limited bandwidth of the uplink control channel. Therefore, the CSI must be compressed at the UE before feedback. Specifically, the CSI matrix $\bm{H}$ can be transformed into the angular-delay domain by a 2D-discrete Fourier transform (2D-DFT), in which the channel exhibits a sparse structure, given by
\begin{align}\label{DFT}
 \bm{\tilde{H}}=\bm{F}_c \bm{H} \bm{F}_t^{H} ,
\end{align}
where $\bm{F}_c\in\mathbb{C}^{N_c\times N_c}$ and $\bm{F}_t\in\mathbb{C}^{N_t\times N_t}$ transform the subcarrier and antenna dimensions into the delay and angular domains, respectively. \textcolor{black}{After the 2D-DFT, most of the energy of $\tilde{\bm{H}}$ is concentrated in the first $N_a\leq N_c$ delay-domain rows.} The remaining $N_c-N_a$ rows contain near-zero values, and we therefore truncate $\bm{\tilde{H}}$ to its first $N_a$ rows and denote the resulting angular-delay CSI as $\bm{H}_a \in \mathbb{C}^{N_a\times N_t}$. To further reduce the feedback overhead, the UE applies an encoder $\mathcal{E}_{\theta_1}(\cdot)$ to compress $\bm{H}_a$ into a low-dimensional codeword, given by
\begin{equation}
 \bm v
 =
 \mathcal{E}_{\theta_1}(\bm H_a),
 \quad
 \bm v\in\mathbb{R}^{M},
 \label{eq:system_encoder}
\end{equation}
\textcolor{black}{where $M\in\mathbb{Z}^{+}$ denotes the number of real-valued feedback symbols. The corresponding compression ratio is defined as
$\eta={M}/{2N_aN_t}$.}
Upon receiving the codeword $\bm v$, the BS reconstructs the angular-delay CSI through a decoder $\mathcal{D}_{\theta_2}(\cdot)$, given by
\begin{equation}
 \hat{\bm H}_a
 =
 \mathcal{D}_{\theta_2}(\bm v).
 \label{eq:system_decoder}
\end{equation}
The objective of the encoder-decoder pair is to minimize the reconstruction error between the original and the reconstructed CSI, which is measured by the mean squared error (MSE), given by
\begin{equation}
 \label{object}
 \min_{\theta_1,\theta_2}
 \mathbb{E}\!\left[
 \left\|\bm H_a-\hat{\bm H}_a\right\|_F^2
 \right].
\end{equation}
\section{Low-Rank Prior-based CSI Compression}\label{sec:proposed_method}
In this section, we recast CSI feedback as a structured sensing and reconstruction problem and present \textcolor{black}{the proposed LRP framework as shown in \autoref{fig:lrp_arch}}, where the \textcolor{black}{LRP encoder} performs rank-one sensing to obtain a low-dimensional measurement and the \textcolor{black}{LRP decoder} reconstructs the CSI from the measurement.  We first introduce the low-rank multipath prior of the angular-delay channel, and then present the detailed design of the encoder and decoder. Finally, we describe the optimization procedure and analyze the computational complexity.
