\subsection{Low-Rank Multipath Prior}
After the 2D-DFT in \eqref{DFT}, the truncated angular-delay CSI matrix
\(\bm H_a\in\mathbb{C}^{N_a\times N_t}\) is not a generic dense matrix, but is governed by the underlying multipath propagation structure. We now derive this prior from the frequency-spatial channel model. Under a standard far-field geometric channel with a ULA at the BS, we assume that the path gains are frequency independent over the considered OFDM bandwidth, the array response is frequency invariant, and the delay and spatial responses are separable. Then, the \(n\)-th subcarrier channel row can be written as \cite{Vlachos2019Wideband, Joint_Sparse_and_Low_Rank, zhou2017lowranktensor}
\begin{align}
 \bm H_{n,:}
 =
 \sum_{\ell=1}^{L}
 \alpha_\ell e^{-j2\pi f_n\tau_\ell}
 \bm a^{H}(\theta_\ell),
 \quad n=1,\ldots,N_c,
 \label{eq:freq_spatial_geo_channel}
\end{align}
where \(\alpha_\ell\), \(\tau_\ell\), and \(\theta_\ell\) denote the complex path gain, delay, and angle of the \(\ell\)-th path, respectively, \(f_n\) is the baseband frequency of the \(n\)-th subcarrier, and \(\bm a(\theta_\ell)\in\mathbb{C}^{N_t}\) is the array steering vector. By stacking all subcarriers, \eqref{eq:freq_spatial_geo_channel} can be rewritten as
\begin{align}
 \bm H
 =
 \sum_{\ell=1}^{L}
 \alpha_\ell
 \bm q(\tau_\ell)\bm a^{H}(\theta_\ell),
 \label{eq:freq_spatial_stacked_channel}
\end{align}
where
\(\bm q(\tau_\ell)=[e^{-j2\pi f_1\tau_\ell},\ldots,e^{-j2\pi f_{N_c}\tau_\ell}]^{T}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_c}\)
is the frequency-domain delay response. Let \(\bm S_a=[\bm I_{N_a},\bm 0]\in\mathbb{R}^{N_a\times N_c}\) denote the row-selection matrix that keeps the first \(N_a\) delay bins. Combining \eqref{DFT} with \eqref{eq:freq_spatial_stacked_channel}, the truncated angular-delay CSI is obtained as
\begin{align}
 \bm H_a
 &=
 \bm S_a\bm F_c\bm H\bm F_t^{H} \notag
 &=
 \sum_{\ell=1}^{L}
 \alpha_\ell
 \big(\bm S_a\bm F_c\bm q(\tau_\ell)\big)
 \big(\bm F_t\bm a(\theta_\ell)\big)^{H}.
 \label{eq:geo_low_rank}
\end{align}
This expression shows that each physical path remains an outer product of a transformed delay response and a transformed angular response after the 2D-DFT and delay-row truncation. Therefore, the multipath channel is represented as a sum of rank-one delay-angular components. Since premultiplication and postmultiplication by DFT matrices, together with delay-row selection, cannot increase the matrix rank, \(\bm H_a\) satisfies
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{rank}(\bm H_a)
 &\leq
 \sum_{\ell=1}^{L}
 \operatorname{rank}\!\left(
 \alpha_\ell
 \big(\bm S_a\bm F_c\bm q(\tau_\ell)\big)
 \big(\bm F_t\bm a(\theta_\ell)\big)^{H}
 \right) \notag
 &\leq L.
 \label{eq:rank_bound}
\end{align}
When the number of dominant paths is much smaller than the matrix dimensions, i.e.,
\(L\ll\min(N_a,N_t)\), \(\bm H_a\) is therefore low-rank or can be accurately approximated by a low-rank matrix \cite{Low_rank_and_Sparse_Channel_Estimation, shao2022exploiting}.  Motivated by this observation, we adopt the low-rank structure as the working prior and approximate \(\bm H_a\) by \(R\) rank-one components, given by
\begin{align}
 \bm H_a
 \approx
 \sum_{r=1}^{R}\bm d_r\bm a_r^{H},
 \label{eq:low_rank_prior}
\end{align}
where \(R\) is a designable model rank that controls the number of rank-one
components used by the architecture and need not equal the physical path number
\(L\).
\subsection{Rank-One Sensing-Based CSI Compression}
The low-rank prior in \eqref{eq:low_rank_prior} suggests that \(\bm H_a\) is not an arbitrary random matrix and cannot simply be regarded as a generic image. Instead, it has an explicit physical structure that is generated by a small number of multipath components, and each component is rank-one and separable across the delay and angular domains. This observation motivates the use of rank-one sensing, under which a low-rank matrix can be efficiently recovered from a small number of rank-one measurements formed by pairs of sensing vectors along the row and column dimensions \cite{cai2015rop, zhong2015efficient,pmlr-v89-li19e,rank_one_prior}. By designing the sensing vectors to align with the delay and angular steering vectors, we can obtain measurements that are directly informative about the underlying multipath structure, rather than relying on generic convolutional filters to learn this structure implicitly.
Specifically,  for the \(r\)-th sensing branch, we construct the measurement operator as a rank-one matrix
\(\bm A_r=\bm u_r\bm v_r^{H}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_a\times N_t}\) from two complex-valued
sensing vectors \(\bm u_r\in\mathbb{C}^{N_a}\) and
\(\bm v_r\in\mathbb{C}^{N_t}\), which yields the bilinear measurement, given by
\begin{align}
 c_r
 =
 \left\langle \bm A_r, \bm H_a \right\rangle_F
 =
 \bm u_r^{H}\bm H_a \bm v_r,
 \qquad r=1,\ldots,R_{\mathrm{enc}},
 \label{eq:lpa_rank_one_sensing}
\end{align}
where \(\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_F\) denotes the complex Frobenius inner
product. Notably, substituting the low-rank prior \eqref{eq:low_rank_prior}
into \eqref{eq:lpa_rank_one_sensing} yields
\(c_r\!\approx\!\sum_{k=1}^{R}(\bm u_r^{H}\bm d_{k})(\bm a_{k}^{H}\bm v_r)\),
which is also similar to the matched filtering that measures the alignment between $\bm H_a$ 
and the delay--angular pattern parameterized by 
$(\bm u_r,\bm v_r)$. In other words, each sensing branch detects whether the CSI contains 
a path component whose delay--angular signature aligns with the 
pattern parameterized by $(\bm u_r,\bm v_r)$.
To achieve the desired feedback dimension \(M\), we further project the measurements \(\{c_r\}\) to a low-dimensional codeword through a lightweight MLP layer. Specifically, we stack the measurements into a vector
\(\bm c=[c_1,\ldots,c_{R_{\mathrm{enc}}}]^T\in\mathbb{C}^{R_{\mathrm{enc}}}\) and then convert it into a real-valued vector by concatenating the real and imaginary parts as \(\bar{\bm c}=[\Re\{\bm c\}^{T},\Im\{\bm c\}^{T}]^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{2R_{\mathrm{enc}}}\). The feedback codeword is then produced by \textcolor{black}{the LRP encoder}, given by
\begin{align}
 \bm z
 =
 \bm W_{\mathrm{enc}} \sigma(\bar{\bm c})+\bm b_{\mathrm{enc}},
 \label{eq:lpa_codeword_projection}
\end{align}
where \(\bm W_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times 2R_{\mathrm{enc}}}\) and
\(\bm b_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}\) are learnable parameters, and \(\sigma(\cdot)\) is
an activation function. In this way, the \textcolor{black}{LRP encoder} embeds the low-rank
delay--angular prior directly into the measurement structure, rather than
relying on generic neural layers to learn this structure implicitly without introducing larger computational overhead.
\subsection{Low-Rank Multipath Reconstruction}
At the BS side, the \textcolor{black}{LRP decoder} reconstructs the CSI from the feedback codeword with the help of the same low-rank prior. Rather than directly generating all \(N_aN_t\) complex CSI coefficients using NN layers, it instead generates the rank-one factors of each path component and then synthesizes the CSI through their outer products. Specifically, when receiving the codeword \(\bm z\), the \textcolor{black}{LRP decoder} first maps the received codeword
\(\bm z\in\mathbb{R}^{M}\) to the rank-one factors of all \(R\) reconstruction
branches, given by
\begin{align}
 \big[\hat{\bm d}_1^{T},\hat{\bm a}_1^{T},\ldots,
 \hat{\bm d}_{R}^{T},\hat{\bm a}_{R}^{T}\big]^{T}
 =
 \textcolor{black}{\bm W_{\mathrm{dec}}} \bm z+\textcolor{black}{\bm b_{\mathrm{dec}}},
 \label{eq:decoder_linear_projection}
\end{align}
where \(\textcolor{black}{\bm W_{\mathrm{dec}}}\in\mathbb{C}^{R(N_a+N_t)\times M}\) and
\(\textcolor{black}{\bm b_{\mathrm{dec}}}\in\mathbb{C}^{R(N_a+N_t)}\) are learnable parameters. The vectors
\(\hat{\bm d}_r\in\mathbb{C}^{N_a}\) and \(\hat{\bm a}_r\in\mathbb{C}^{N_t}\) are
the delay-domain and angular-domain factors of the \(r\)-th branch\footnote{The complex coefficient of each rank-one component is absorbed into the magnitudes and phases of $\hat{\bm d}_r$ and $\hat{\bm a}_r$, so no separate
scalar gain is introduced.}. The branch output
is the rank-one outer product
\begin{align}
 \hat{\bm H}_{a,r}
 =
 \hat{\bm d}_r
 \hat{\bm a}_r^{H}.
 \label{eq:decoder_rank_one_component}
\end{align}
Next, \textcolor{black}{the LRP decoder} sums the outputs of all branches to obtain the low-rank reconstruction of the CSI, given by
\begin{align}
 \hat{\bm H}_{a}^{\mathrm{LR}}
 =
 \sum_{r=1}^{R}
 \hat{\bm d}_r
 \hat{\bm a}_r^{H}.
 \label{eq:decoder_low_rank_reconstruction}
\end{align}
We note that the number of decoder branches $R$ is a reconstruction rank hyperparameter rather than the exact
number of physical propagation paths. Therefore, each branch is not required to correspond to one physical path, while  the $R$ branches jointly provide a compact low-rank approximation of the dominant CSI structure.
\begin{algorithm}[t!]
  \SetAlgoLined
  \DontPrintSemicolon
  \caption{Inference Process of the Proposed Method}\label{alg:dcrnet}
  \KwIn{Angular-delay CSI matrix 
  \(\bm H_a\in\mathbb{C}^{N_a\times N_t}\); trained parameters 
  \(\bm{\Theta}_e\) and \(\bm{\Theta}_d\).}
  \KwOut{Reconstructed CSI \(\hat{\bm H}_a\).}

  \textbf{UE side: rank-one sensing}\;
  \For{$r=1,\ldots,R_{\mathrm{enc}}$}{
 Compute matched-filtering coefficient 
 \(c_r\leftarrow\bm u_r^{H}\bm H_a\bm v_r\)\;
  }
  Stack coefficients 
  \(\bm c\leftarrow[c_1,\ldots,c_{R_{\mathrm{enc}}}]^{T}\in\mathbb{C}^{R_{\mathrm{enc}}}\)\;
  Form real-valued representation 
  \(\bar{\bm c}\leftarrow[\Re\{\bm c\}^{T},\Im\{\bm c\}^{T}]^{T}\)\;
  Generate feedback codeword 
  \(\bm z\leftarrow\bm W_{\mathrm{enc}}\sigma(\bar{\bm c})+\bm b_{\mathrm{enc}}\)\;
  Send \(\bm z\) to the BS over the feedback link\;

  \textbf{BS side: low-rank synthesis}\;
  Apply linear projection to obtain rank-one factors
  \(\{\hat{\bm d}_r,\hat{\bm a}_r\}_{r=1}^{R}
  \leftarrow \textcolor{black}{\bm W_{\mathrm{dec}}}\bm z+\textcolor{black}{\bm b_{\mathrm{dec}}}\)\;
  Reconstruct
  \(\hat{\bm H}_a\leftarrow
  \sum_{r=1}^{R}
  \hat{\bm d}_r\hat{\bm a}_r^{H}\)\;

  \Return{\(\hat{\bm H}_a\)}\;
  \end{algorithm}




\subsection{Optimization}
The above design has specified the structural form of the rank-one sensing \textcolor{black}{LRP encoder} in \eqref{eq:lpa_rank_one_sensing}--\eqref{eq:lpa_codeword_projection} and the low-rank synthesis \textcolor{black}{LRP decoder} in \eqref{eq:decoder_linear_projection}--\eqref{eq:decoder_low_rank_reconstruction}, which can be jointly characterized by the structured parameter set
\begin{align}
 \bm{\Theta}
 =
 \{\mathcal{U},\mathcal{V},\bm W_{\mathrm{enc}},\bm b_{\mathrm{enc}},\textcolor{black}{\bm W_{\mathrm{dec}}},\textcolor{black}{\bm b_{\mathrm{dec}}}\},
 \label{eq:optimized_parameters}
\end{align}
where \(\mathcal{U}=\{\bm u_r\}_{r=1}^{R_{\mathrm{enc}}}\) and \(\mathcal{V}=\{\bm v_r\}_{r=1}^{R_{\mathrm{enc}}}\) are the rank-one sensing vectors. The remaining question is how to determine \(\bm{\Theta}\). A straightforward solution is to follow classical low-rank matrix sensing. \textcolor{black}{In this approach, the sensing matrices \(\{\bm A_m\}_{m=1}^{\tilde M}\) are usually generated at random, for example from a Gaussian distribution, and then kept fixed to form a linear sensing operator \(\mathcal A(\bm X)=[\langle \bm A_m,\bm X\rangle_F]_{m=1}^{\tilde M}\).} Given the measurements $\bm y=\mathcal A(\bm H_a)$, the
low-rank matrix is recovered by solving
\begin{align}
\hat{\bm H}_a
=
\arg\min_{\bm X}
\tfrac{1}{2}
\left\|
\mathcal A(\bm X)-\bm y
\right\|_2^2
+
\lambda \|\bm X\|_* ,
\label{eq:classical_nuclear_norm_recovery}
\end{align}
where $\|\bm X\|_*$ is the regularization term that promotes low-rank structure, and $\lambda$ is a regularization parameter. 
\begin{figure*}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figs/DCRNetV2_archi.pdf}
 \caption{Architecture of DCRNetV2. The LRP backbone is shown in blue, and the convolutional residual branch is shown in orange. The two branches are fused at the encoder output, and they are decoded separately at the BS side. The final CSI estimate is obtained by summing the direct low-rank reconstruction and the factored residual reconstruction.}
 \label{fig:dcrnetv2_arch}
\end{figure*}
However, this classical formulation is not well suited to CSI feedback. First, random sensing matrices are designed to be universal for general low-rank matrices, while the angular--delay CSI matrix $\bm H_a$ follows a specific multipath structure as shown in \eqref{eq:geo_low_rank}. Therefore, random sensing patterns may not match the dominant delay--angular components
of the channel. Second, \eqref{eq:classical_nuclear_norm_recovery} requires solving an optimization problem for every received codeword, which introduces iterative online optimization at the BS. Third, the sensing operator and the recovery algorithm are designed
separately, so the obtained measurements are not necessarily optimal for the final CSI reconstruction error.
Motivated by these three points, we jointly optimize the sensing and reconstruction stages by directly minimizing the empirical reconstruction error over a training dataset. Specifically, given \(N_s\) training samples, the parameter set is obtained by minimizing the empirical reconstruction error:
\begin{align}
 \min_{\bm{\Theta}}\;
 \frac{1}{N_s}\sum_{i=1}^{N_s}
 \big\|\bm H_a^{(i)}-\hat{\bm H}_a^{(i)}\big\|_F^2,
 \label{eq:structured_parameter_optimization}
\end{align}
which is solved by mini-batch stochastic gradient-based training over the CSI samples. In this way, the rank-one sensing vectors \(\{\bm u_r,\bm v_r\}\) are adapted to the angular-delay statistics of practical CSI, and they are coupled with the synthesis decoder through the same training objective, so that each measurement \(c_r\) in \eqref{eq:lpa_rank_one_sensing} is encouraged to preserve the CSI features most useful for the final reconstruction rather than being fixed independently of the recovery stage.
\subsection{Complexity Analysis}
\label{subsec:lrp_complexity}
We now analyze the computational complexity of the proposed LRP framework. At the user side, each rank-one sensing in \eqref{eq:lpa_rank_one_sensing} is computed as \(\bm u_r^{H}(\bm H_a\bm v_r)\) at a cost of \(\mathcal{O}(N_aN_t)\), so the sensing stage with \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}\) operators scales as \(\mathcal{O}(R_{\mathrm{enc}}N_aN_t)\), and the subsequent linear projection in \eqref{eq:lpa_codeword_projection} adds \(\mathcal{O}(MR_{\mathrm{enc}})\). Therefore, the user-side complexity is \(\mathcal{O}(R_{\mathrm{enc}}(N_aN_t+M))\). At the BS side, the direct linear projection in \eqref{eq:decoder_linear_projection} costs \(\mathcal{O}(MR(N_a+N_t))\), and the rank-one synthesis in \eqref{eq:decoder_low_rank_reconstruction} sums \(R\) rank-one outer products at \(\mathcal{O}(RN_aN_t)\), giving a BS-side complexity of \(\mathcal{O}(R[M(N_a+N_t)+N_aN_t])\). The overall complexity of the LRP framework is therefore
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{O}\!\left(R_{\mathrm{enc}}(N_aN_t+M)+R[M(N_a+N_t)+N_aN_t]\right).
 \label{eq:overall_complexity}
\end{equation}
Since the encoder uses only lightweight rank-one sensing and a linear projection, and the decoder avoids any explicit \(\mathcal{O}(N_a^{2}N_t^{2})\) matrix reconstruction, the proposed scheme incurs much lower complexity than conventional convolutional autoencoder-based feedback, and is therefore well suited for resource-constrained users.
\section{Design of DCRNetV2}\label{sec:dcrnetv2}
In this section, we present DCRNetV2, an architecture family that enhances LRP while preserving its core backbone. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dcrnetv2_arch}, \textcolor{black}{the framework is designed to preserve critical channel information. At the encoder, global low-rank and local features are extracted in parallel before compression, since local details discarded by the LRP projection cannot be recovered later from the codeword. At the decoder, the codeword is first restored to the angular-delay domain via low-rank synthesis and a lightweight rank extension, followed by convolutional refinement to capture local correlations.}
\subsection{Encoder Design}
\label{subsec:dcrnetv2_combine}
At the user side, the angular-delay channel $\bm H_a$ is processed by two parallel branches. The first branch is the LRP encoder in \eqref{eq:lpa_codeword_projection}, which performs learnable rank-one sensing followed by a linear projection and produces the codeword \bm $z_{\mathrm{LRP}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}$. \textcolor{black}{Each rank-one sensing matrix can be viewed as a learnable separable template that globally matches the angular-delay channel, making LRP effective at extracting its dominant structured components. However, pure rank-one sensing patterns struggle in rich-scattering, non-line-of-sight (NLoS) environments. In such scenarios, severe multipath delay spread and angular dispersion naturally smear channel energy across adjacent bins, forming diffuse local clusters that cannot be fully captured by a few global rank-one components alone.}
To address this issue, the second branch introduces the DCR encoder shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dcrnetv2_arch}. It uses asymmetric $1\times k$ and $k\times 1$ convolutions to separately learn angular and delay correlations, while parallel dilated paths expand the receptive field to cover broader multipath clusters. \textcolor{black}{Moreover, the DCR branch operates directly on $\bm H_a$ in parallel with LRP because local details lost during global projection cannot be restored by subsequent post-processing. Together, the two branches extract complementary global and local features prior to compression.} Denoting the DCR operation as $f_{\mathrm{DCR}}(\cdot)$, the auxiliary codeword is given by
\begin{align}
 \bm z_{\mathrm{DCR}}
 =
 \gamma_e\,f_{\mathrm{DCR}}(\bm H_a)
 \in\mathbb{R}^{M},
 \label{eq:dcr_aux_codeword}
\end{align}
where \(\gamma_e\) is a learnable scalar gate that controls how much of the convolutional residual enters the feedback vector. Next, since the two codewords are generated by different transforms, direct element-wise addition would implicitly assume aligned feature semantics. We instead concatenate them and employ a small MLP to learn their interaction, following the widely used concatenation-based feature fusion in multi-branch networks~\cite{ronneberger2015unet}, given by
\begin{align}
 \bm z
 =
 \bm z_{\mathrm{LRP}}
 +
 \bm z_{\mathrm{DCR}}
 +
 g_{\mathrm{fuse}}\!\left(\left[\bm z_{\mathrm{LRP}};\bm z_{\mathrm{DCR}}\right]\right),
 \label{eq:dcr_lrp_encoder}
\end{align}
where \(g_{\mathrm{fuse}}(\cdot)\) is a two-layer MLP. The additive terms preserve a direct path from both branches, while the MLP learns only the residual interaction between the low-rank and convolutional codewords. To avoid training instability at the early stage, \(\gamma_e\) and the output layer of \(g_{\mathrm{fuse}}(\cdot)\) are initialized to a small value so that the initial codeword is close to \(\bm z_{\mathrm{LRP}}\) and the convolutional contribution is gradually increased when necessary.
\subsection{Decoder Design}
\label{subsec:dcrnetv2_decoder}
At the BS side, the codeword \(\bm z\) is first decoded by a low-rank synthesis module that mirrors \eqref{eq:decoder_linear_projection}--\eqref{eq:decoder_low_rank_reconstruction} and produces a direct rank-\(R\) estimate
\begin{align}
 \hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{dir})}
 =
 \sum_{r=1}^{R}\hat{\bm d}_r\hat{\bm a}_r^{H},
 \label{eq:dcrv2_direct_decoder}
\end{align}
where \(\hat{\bm d}_r\) and \(\hat{\bm a}_r\) are obtained from \(\bm z\) through the LRP linear projection. This direct path preserves the interpretable low-rank reconstruction of LRP, but its rank is hard-capped at \(R\). \textcolor{black}{When the channel exhibits rich scattering or continuous multipath dispersion, the channel energy can spread over a larger number of effective rank components, resulting in a slowly decaying rank tail that is difficult to represent with a fixed rank-$R$ model. This leads a representation bottleneck for the direct decoder. A straightforward solution is to increase $R$, but doing so linearly increases both the computational complexity of the decoder.}
To supplement the rank more efficiently, we introduce a residual decoder in Fig.~\ref{fig:dcrnetv2_arch} that generates extra rank-one components from the same codeword \(\bm z\), but with a lower cost than enlarging the direct branch. Specifically, the codeword is first mapped by the residual feature projection \(\bm W_{\mathrm{res}}\in\mathbb{R}^{R_{\mathrm{e}}d\times M}\) and reshaped into a tensor \(\bm F\in\mathbb{R}^{R_{\mathrm{e}}\times d}\), where \(d\ll M\) is a small bottleneck dimension. Each extra rank-one component is then generated from a shared factored pair, which can be expressed as
\begin{align}
 \hat{\bm d}^{(e)}_r=\bm W_d\bm F_r,\quad
 \hat{\bm a}^{(e)}_r=\bm W_a\bm F_r,
 \label{eq:dcrv2_extra_components}
\end{align}
where \(\bm W_d\in\mathbb{R}^{N_a\times d}\) and \(\bm W_a\in\mathbb{R}^{N_t\times d}\) are shared across the \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\) residual components. \textcolor{black}{The shared factorization provides a lightweight means of increasing the reconstruction order without assigning an independent high-dimensional projection to every additional component.} Each component is then weighted by a per-rank sigmoid gate \(g_r(\bm z)\in[0,1]\), inspired by the channel-wise attention mechanism in SENet~\cite{hu2018squeeze}, which adaptively re-weights feature channels via a sigmoid excitation. Here, the same idea is applied along the rank dimension, so that each residual component is treated as a rank channel whose contribution is emphasized or suppressed through end-to-end learning. Moreover, a global learnable scalar \(\alpha\) is introduced to control the overall strength of the supplementary components relative to the direct branch. The hybrid-rank CSI estimate is obtained by summing the direct reconstruction and the supplementary residual reconstruction, given by
\begin{align}
 \hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{hyb})}
 =
 \hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{dir})}
 +
 \alpha\sum_{r=1}^{R_{\mathrm{e}}} g_r(\bm z)\,\hat{\bm d}^{(e)}_r\left(\hat{\bm a}^{(e)}_r\right)^{H},
 \label{eq:dcrv2_hybrid_decoder}
\end{align}
where \(\alpha\) and the bias of \(g_r(\cdot)\) are initialized to a small value so that the residual branch starts near zero and the hybrid estimate falls back to \(\hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{dir})}\) at initialization. In this way, the direct components in \(\hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{dir})}\) carry the dominant multipath energy, while the gated bank in \eqref{eq:dcrv2_hybrid_decoder} acts as a switchable rank tail: the per-rank gates close on well-conditioned channels and open only on samples whose residual rank actually reduces the reconstruction error, while the global \(\alpha\) lets the network learn the overall trade-off between the two paths. In addition, the cost savings of this design are explicit. Since \(\bm W_d\) and \(\bm W_a\) are shared and \(\bm F_r\) is generated through the \(d\)-dimensional bottleneck, the factored form in \eqref{eq:dcrv2_extra_components} only costs \(\mathcal{O}(R_{\mathrm{e}}d+d(N_a+N_t))\) parameters, instead of the \(\mathcal{O}(R_{\mathrm{e}}M(N_a+N_t))\) required by enlarging the direct branch from \(R\) to \(R+R_{\mathrm{e}}\). Therefore, the effective reconstruction rank increases at a cost essentially independent of \(M\).
