\subsection{DCR Refinement Module}
\label{subsec:dcrnetv2_refine}
\textcolor{black}{After the hybrid-rank decoder restores the codeword to the full angular-delay domain, we further apply a DCR refinement module sequentially to the reconstructed CSI. Unlike the encoder, where local features must be extracted before dimensionality reduction, the decoder has already recovered a full-dimensional channel estimate at this stage. Therefore, the refinement module can directly exploit the spatial neighborhood of each angular-delay coefficient without passing through another information bottleneck. This naturally leads to a reconstruction-then-refinement structure, which is also commonly adopted in CSI feedback networks~\cite{CsiNet,CsiNetPlus,DCRNet}.}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dcrnetv2_arch}, this module contains two parallel convolutional paths followed by concatenation and a \(1\times1\) fusion layer. One path uses dilated and asymmetric kernels to collect local off-grid leakage over a larger angular-delay neighborhood, while the other path preserves short-range details with standard and asymmetric convolutions. Denoting this single refinement operator by \(f_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot)\), the refined estimate is
\begin{align}
 \hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{ref})}
 =
 \hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{hyb})}
 +
 \gamma_{\mathrm{ref}}\,
 f_{\mathrm{ref}}\!\left(\hat{\bm H}_a^{(\mathrm{hyb})}\right),
 \label{eq:dcrv2_refine_block}
\end{align}
where \(\gamma_{\mathrm{ref}}\) is a learnable scalar gate initialized close to zero. 
\subsection{Complexity Control}
\label{subsec:dcrnetv2_variants}
To balance reconstruction accuracy and computational cost, we introduce several DCRNetV2 variants. These models share the same architecture but vary across four parameters: the sensing rank $R_{\mathrm{enc}}$ at the UE, and the direct rank $R$, residual rank $R_{\mathrm{e}}$, and bottleneck size $d$ at the BS.At the UE, $R_{\mathrm{enc}}$ denotes the number of rank-one sensing operators with complexity $\mathcal{O}(R_{\mathrm{enc}}(N_aN_t + M))$. Reducing $R_{\mathrm{enc}}$ directly lowers the encoder FLOPs for user devices. At the BS, the direct rank $R$ sets the number of main components in \eqref{eq:dcrv2_direct_decoder}, where a small $R$ captures dominant path energy and a larger $R$ provides a finer low-rank base. The residual branch then introduces $R_{\mathrm{e}}$ extra components at a modest cost of $\mathcal{O}(R_{\mathrm{e}}d(N_a + N_t))$, expanding the effective rank without running full high-dimensional projections.Adjusting these four parameters yields a spectrum of models, from compact user-friendly setups to high-accuracy variants, all built upon the same low-rank backbone. Section~\ref{sec:simulation} details the specific configurations used in our experiments.
