\section{Simulation Results}\label{sec:simulation}
In this section, we present the simulation results of the proposed LRP and DCRNetV2 methods, and compare them with representative model-based and DL-based CSI feedback methods in terms of reconstruction accuracy and computational cost. We also analyze the trade-off between accuracy and complexity, and evaluate the inference latency and energy consumption on a real mobile device. Moreover, we test the generalization performance of the proposed methods under the proposed benchmark and real measured CSI datasets.
\subsection{General Setup}
\label{subsec:sim_setup}
Following the standard practice in \cite{CsiNet,CsiNetPlus}, we first evaluate the proposed methods on the COST 2100 channel dataset~\cite{cost2100} under the indoor and outdoor scenarios, with 5.3 GHz and 300 MHz carrier frequencies, respectively. The BS is equipped with \(N_t=32\) antennas and \(N_c=1024\) OFDM subcarriers, and the truncated angular-delay CSI has \(N_a=32\) rows. The training, validation, and test sets contain \(100{,}000\), \(30{,}000\), and \(20{,}000\) samples, respectively.  We report the normalized mean squared error (NMSE) in dB, given by
\begin{align}
 \mathrm{NMSE} = 10\log_{10}\left(\mathbb E\left[\frac{\|\hat{\bm H}_a-\bm H_a\|_F^2}{\|\bm H_a\|_F^2}\right]\right).
\end{align}
We also evaluate the computational cost in terms of FLOPs to compare the efficiency of different methods. For the proposed LRP and DCRNetV2 variants, we train each model for 1500 epochs using mean squared error loss and the Adam optimizer with a batch size of 200 and an initial learning rate of \(2\times10^{-3}\).
