\subsection{Baseline Methods}
\label{subsec:dcrnetv2_variants_sim}
To evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed LRP architecture, we compare it with representative model-based feedback methods, including MS4L2O~\cite{ms4l2o}, the classical iterative shrinkage thresholding algorithm (ISTA), and its accelerated variant FISTA~\cite{fista}. To evaluate the proposed DCRNetV2 architecture, we first compare it with lightweight DL-based feedback networks, including CsiNet~\cite{CsiNet}, CRNet~\cite{CRNet}, CLNet~\cite{CLNet}, ACRNet-$1\times$ and DCRNet-$1\times$~\cite{DCRNet}. We also compare with high-performance DL-based feedback methods, including DS-NLCsiNet~\cite{DSNLCsiNet}, CsiNet$+$~\cite{CsiNetPlus}, ACRNet-$10\times$ and DCRNet-$10\times$~\cite{DCRNet}, TransNet~\cite{TransNet}, TCLNet~\cite{TCLNet}, LLMCsiNet-S~\cite{LLMCsiNet}, and SSE-Net~\cite{SSE-Net}.
We implement standalone LRP by varying the encoder sensing rank \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}\in\{128,512\}\) and the decoder synthesis rank \(R\in\{4,8,16\}\) to examine the low-rank sensing and reconstruction trade-off. For DCRNetV2, we use five variants with gradually increased capacity. The mini version adopts \((R_{\mathrm{enc}},R,R_{\mathrm{e}},d)=(128,10,8,8)\), where \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}\) denotes the encoder rank, \(R\) is the number of direct decoder components, \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\) is the number of factored residual components, and \(d\) is only the residual-decoder bottleneck dimension. The small and base versions remove the residual branch and use a plain low-rank decoder with \(R=16\), while increasing \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}\) from \(256\) to \(512\). The unified version uses \((R_{\mathrm{enc}},R,R_{\mathrm{e}},d)=(512,32,16,16)\), and the large version further increases the residual rank to \(R_{\mathrm{e}}=32\) while keeping \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}=512\), \(R=32\), and \(d=16\).
\subsection{Performance Comparison}
\label{subsec:sim_baselines}
In Table~\ref{tab:1a}, we first compare the standalone LRP architecture with model-based feedback methods under the COST 2100 indoor and outdoor scenarios. From this table, we can see that LRP consistently outperforms the model-based methods in terms of NMSE for the same or lower FLOPs. For example, at \(\eta=1/4\), LRP with \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}=128\) and \(R=4\) achieves \(-13.18\)~dB indoor NMSE with only \(0.68\)M FLOPs, while the best competitor FISTA gives \(-10.46\)~dB with \(42.99\)M FLOPs. The trend is similar for another compression ratio and the outdoor scenario. It is worth noting that when the encoder sensing rank \(R_{\mathrm{enc}}\) and decoder synthesis rank \(R\) are increased, LRP can further improve the NMSE while still maintaining a much lower FLOPs than the model-based methods, even outperforming or approaching the performance of the lightweight DL-based feedback methods in Table~\ref{tab:1b}. This is because the proposed LRP architecture keeps the physical low-rank prior in the compression and reconstruction process, which is more effective than the generic random projection and iterative recovery used in the model-based methods. These results validate the effectiveness of the proposed LRP architecture.
