Table~\ref{tab:1b} compares DCRNetV2 with lightweight DL baselines under $\sim$5M FLOPs at $\eta=1/4$. Across this low-complexity regime, DCRNetV2 offers a superior accuracy-complexity trade-off. Specifically, at $\eta=1/4$, DCRNetV2-mini lowers the indoor NMSE from $-28.04$~dB (DCRNet-$1\times$) to $-31.00$~dB while cutting FLOPs from $4.01$M to $2.83$M. At $\eta=1/8$, it reaches nearly $-20$~dB NMSE with only $1.87$M FLOPs, roughly half the computation of ACRNet-$1\times$ with a $>4$~dB gain. Scaled variants further enhance accuracy; for instance, DCRNetV2-unified achieves $-32.84$~dB indoor NMSE at $\eta=1/4$ and SOTA outdoor performance with modest FLOP increases.
Table~\ref{tab:high_perf} further evaluates DCRNetV2 against high-performance, large Transformer-based baselines. Scenario-specific variants DCRNetV2-large-I and DCRNetV2-large-O achieve competitive accuracy with substantially lower complexity. At $\eta=1/4$, DCRNetV2-large-I reaches $-33.20$~dB indoor NMSE with $7.00$M FLOPs, outperforming TransNet's $-32.38$~dB at only one-fifth of the computation. For outdoor channels, DCRNetV2-large-O matches TransNet with $-14.85$~dB at $8.25$M FLOPs. Even at $\eta=1/16$, where TransNet holds a marginal NMSE lead, both large variants yield comparable accuracy while slashing FLOPs by nearly $10\times$. These results demonstrate that DCRNetV2 effectively scales into the high-accuracy regime with superior computational efficiency.
Fig.~\ref{fig:indoor_quant_bits_nmse} evaluates the actual feedback-bit cost after quantizing the DCRNetV2 indoor codeword. The FP32 markers provide the unquantized reference, while entropy coding, uniform quantization, and quantization-aware training (QAT) are compared under the same compression ratios. Entropy coding preserves most of the FP32 accuracy at moderate bit widths with far fewer transmitted bits. For example, at \(\eta=1/8\), \(\eta=1/16\), and \(\eta=1/32\), the 6-bit entropy-coded codewords require only \(1513\), \(760\), and \(372\) bits, respectively, while staying close to the FP32 references. At \(\eta=1/4\), entropy coding reduces the feedback load from \(16384\) bits to \(3228\) bits, with NMSE changing from \(-32.84\)~dB to \(-30.65\)~dB. Under very low bit widths, entropy coding degrades more gracefully than uniform quantization and QAT, indicating that the learned codeword has a non-uniform symbol distribution that should be exploited by the feedback quantizer.
Fig.~\ref{fig:iphone_latency} further shows the on-device inference latency and energy reduction on iPhone 17 Pro Max using the Core ML framework\footnote{\url{https://developer.apple.com/machine-learning/core-ml/}}. Since Transformer models have recently become dominant in AI community, Core ML provides mature graph and kernel optimizations for them, from which TransNet can directly benefit. In contrast, the proposed LRP and DCRNetV2 rely on less standard asymmetric separable convolutions that are not equally optimized by the current backend. Even under this setting, standalone LRP requires only \(0.0348\)--\(0.0423\)~ms on average, which is about \(3.4\)--\(4.1\) times faster than TransNet. After adding the DCRNetV2 residual modules, all listed variants still have lower average latency than TransNet. For example, DCRNetV2-mini achieves \(0.1088\)~ms average latency with \(25.6\
