In Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, we take DCRNetV2-unified as the full model and conduct ablation studies to analyze the contribution of different components. The first row corresponds to the pure LRP backbone without any residual paths, which already achieves a reasonable NMSE of \(-21.62\)~dB with only \(2.72\)M FLOPs. Adding the decoder-side DCR branch improves the NMSE to \(-29.34\)~dB with \(3.84\)M FLOPs, showing the benefit of refinement after low-rank synthesis. Adding the DCR encoder without the decoder residual reaches \(-32.00\)~dB, which indicates that convolutional user-side features also help preserve off-grid details in the feedback codeword. These results validate the design of the DCRNetV2 architecture, where the LRP backbone captures the dominant low-rank component while the residual branches compensate for the remaining details that lie outside the low-rank subspace.
To further verify the generality of the low-rank prior, we conduct an ablation study by adding the LRP path to two different convolutional backbones: the CsiNet architecture and a simple convolutional encoder with size \(5\times5\) kernels followed by an MLP decoder. From this table, we can see that adding the LRP path consistently improves the NMSE for both backbones at different compression ratios. For CsiNet, LRP brings a significant improvement of \(5.66\)~dB at \(\eta=1/4\) and \(1.78\)~dB at \(\eta=1/16\). For the Conv5-MLP backbone, the gains are \(1.03\)~dB and \(4.22\)~dB, respectively. These results support the effectiveness of the low-rank prior in improving CSI feedback performance, and show that the proposed LRP architecture has potential to enhance various backbone designs.
\subsection{Extension to More Scenarios}
\label{subsec:sim_generalization}

To further validate the generalization performance of the proposed methods, we introduce a new ray-tracing CSI feedback benchmark. Specifically, we use NVIDIA Sionna\footnote{\url{https://developer.nvidia.com/sionna}} to generate CSI samples from five outdoor scenes (Sionna-RT-Mix5) in Fig.~\ref{fig:sionna_scenarios}. Among them, Paris, Florence, and Munich are public scenes released by NVIDIA, while Zhejiang University (ZJU) and Singapore University of Technology and Design (SUTD) are self-constructed campus-scale scenes to cover different LoS and NLoS propagation characteristics. We also conduct the Shenzhen CBD skyline scenario for test-only evaluation. More details can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:rt_scenarios}\footnote{The dataset can be accessed at \url{https://github.com/tangshunpu/RT-CSI}.}.

Table~\ref{tab:rt_csi_scene_cr} reports the corresponding NMSE results at \(\eta\in\{1/4,1/8,1/16\}\) on the five Sionna-RT-Mix5 scenes. All models are trained on the training set of this dataset including all five scenes, and evaluated on the test set of each scene separately. From this table, we can see that DCRNetV2-unified achieves the best NMSE when $\eta=1/4$ in all five scenes, and is competitive with TransNet in the remaining cases. These results show that the proposed DCRNetV2 architecture can generalize well to more realistic ray-tracing channels with different propagation characteristics, and can achieve competitive performance compared with the SOTA DL-based feedback methods with much lower computational cost. 
We also evaluate out-of-distribution generalization on the Shenzhen CBD scenario. Specifically, all models are pretrained on Sionna-RT-Mix5 and tested on \(40{,}000\) Shenzhen samples without fine-tuning. We then fine-tune these models with a small number of Shenzhen training samples. From the results in Fig.~\ref{fig:finetune_samples_nmse}, we can see that DCRNetV2-unified achieves the second-best NMSE without fine-tuning, which is less than \(1\)~dB worse than TransNet. After fine-tuning, DCRNetV2-unified quickly approaches TransNet. These results indicate that, although the limited parameter count of DCRNetV2 may lead to slightly worse out-of-distribution NMSE than large Transformers, it still achieves competitive accuracy at much lower FLOPs and adapts to a new scenario with only a few fine-tuning samples.
