\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we have investigated CSI feedback by exploiting the low-rank multipath structure of the channel. Specifically, we proposed the LRP architecture, which learns structured sensing and reconstruction over rank-one channel components. We further developed DCRNetV2 by combining the LRP backbone with convolutional residual branches to capture both the dominant low-rank components and the remaining channel details. Moreover, we introduced a ray-tracing CSI feedback benchmark with realistic outdoor scenarios to evaluate the generalization performance of different methods. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed methods achieve a better accuracy--complexity trade-off than existing model-based and DL-based feedback methods and generalize well to ray-tracing and real-world scenarios. However, the path-wise rank-one formulation of LRP relies on a far-field channel model with approximately frequency-invariant array responses. In near-field wideband scenarios, spherical wavefronts and frequency-dependent spatial responses introduce angle--distance--frequency coupling, which may weaken the spatial--frequency separability of individual paths and limit the accuracy of the corresponding rank-one approximations~\cite{Cui2023NearFieldWideband}. Future work includes extending LRP to near-field wideband channels through frequency-dependent spherical-wave modeling and local low-rank representations over subbands and subarrays~\cite{Cui2024NearFieldBeamforming}. We will also explore additional physical priors and incorporate Transformer architectures to further improve CSI feedback performance.
