  After the BS locations are determined, UE candidates are sampled on a $1~\mathrm{m}\times1~\mathrm{m}$ grid over the union of disks centered at the BS locations. Each UE is placed at $z=z_{\mathrm{terrain}}+1.5~\mathrm{m}$, where $z_{\mathrm{terrain}}$ denotes the local terrain elevation. For the five non-Shenzhen scenes, the aggregate gain from BS $b$ is computed as $g_b=\sum_{n_t,n_c}|\mathbf{H}_{b,n_t,n_c}|^2$ over all transmit antennas and subcarriers. The serving BS is then selected by $b^\star=\arg\max_b g_b$, and only the wideband channel from $b^\star$ is stored. For Shenzhen, we instead store all valid UE-BS links and tag each sample with its BS index, which supports cross-BS and few-shot adaptation analyses. Before storage, each angular-delay CSI tensor is normalized per sample by the maximum absolute real or imaginary coefficient and shifted to $[0,1]$.
The held-out Shenzhen Futian CBD scene is configured as a dense high-rise stress case. In addition to the BS-height constraint, diffuse reflection is enabled for all ITU materials with $\sigma_{\mathrm{diff}}=0.1$, while the default Sionna RT setting uses $\sigma_{\mathrm{diff}}=0$. We set $R_{\mathrm{UE}}=400~\mathrm{m}$ around each BS and increase the PathSolver depth to 7 to capture richer multipath propagation. The fixed Shenzhen test set contains $40~\mathrm{k}$ valid UE-BS channel samples and is used exclusively for testing. For few-shot adaptation, separate Shenzhen training sets are generated and split into training and validation subsets with an $80/20$ ratio.
\section{Visualizations}\label{app:lrp_visualization}
