\begin{abstract}
This letter investigates efficient near-field beamforming designs for wireless communication systems aided by multiple extremely large-scale intelligent reflecting surfaces (Multi-XL-IRSs). Previous studies have mostly assumed far-field channel conditions, based on which angle-based beamforming is adopted for the base station (BS) and IRSs. However, this approach may lead to degraded performance in practical near-field scenarios. To address this issue, we consider near-field channel modeling and formulate an optimization problem aimed at maximizing the received power at the user equipment (UE) by jointly optimizing the beamforming at the BS and IRSs. Given the intractability under the beam training (BT)-based communication framework, the problem is revisited and reformulated. Subsequently, an efficient algorithm based on alternating optimization (AO) is proposed to solve the reformulated problem. Numerical results demonstrate that, in typical setups, our AO-based beamforming designs provide over 170\
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Intelligent reflecting surface, wireless beam routing, near-field channel, beamforming, beam training.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
Intelligent reflecting surfaces (IRSs) have emerged as a transformative technology for wireless communication systems \cite{c8,you2024generationadvancedtransceivertechnologies,c4}. Comprising massive reconfigurable elements, IRSs dynamically adjust the signal phase/amplitude in a cost-effective, full-duplex manner. By strategically deploying IRSs on environmental surfaces, propagation environments can be programmatically controlled.
Extensive research has demonstrated IRSs' potential to enhance coverage in obstructed scenarios through multi-hop reflections over multiple IRSs, establishing virtual line-of-sight (LoS) paths between the base station (BS) and the user equipment (UE) \cite{single2multi}. This capability has spurred investigations into multi-IRS beam routing, which achieves superior cooperative passive beamforming (CPB) gains compared to single-IRS systems \cite{cooperative2021,multi-beam-multi-hop2022,MAMP2023}.
To mitigate multi-hop pathloss, it is necessary to deploy XL-IRSs with massive passive reflecting elements to achieve sufficient power capture and beamforming gains \cite{Power-Scaling-Laws-and-Near-Field-Behaviors}. 
Notably, inter-array channels in such deployments often exhibit near-field characteristics because the near-far field boundary is proportional to the array size \cite{MIMO-RD-ARD-distance}\footnote{This work focuses on the near-field condition caused by passive XL-IRSs. Active IRSs can amplify reflected signals, thus reducing the required number of active elements \cite{Active_STAR_RIS}. Therefore, active IRSs are typically manifested as small-scale arrays and adhere to far-field channel models.}. However, existing studies are predominantly based on simplified far-field channel models \cite{single2multi,cooperative2021,multi-beam-multi-hop2022, MAMP2023}, leading to angle-based beamforming designs, which can result in degraded performance in practical near-field conditions due to channel mismatch.
Although our previous work \cite{BSbeamforming} addressed the near-field beamforming design in single-IRS systems, it cannot be applied to multi-IRS systems.
Motivated by the above, this letter proposes a joint beamforming design scheme for BS and IRSs under near-field channel modeling to optimize multi-IRS beam routing. 
We formulate a received power maximization problem under beam training (BT) protocols, which proves intractable. To address this issue, we reformulate the original problem and propose an alternating optimization (AO)-based algorithm to solve the reformulated problem. 
Numerical results depict the power scaling of the multi-IRS beam routing systems and demonstrate the superiority of the proposed AO-based beamforming design over the benchmark schemes.
\textit{Notations}: 
Variables, column vectors, and matrices are denoted by normal-face letters (e.g., $x$), bold-face lower-case letters (e.g., $\mathbf{x}$), and bold-face upper-case letters (e.g., $\mathbf{X}$), respectively. 
$[\mathbf{x}]_n$ denotes the $n$-th element of a vector. $[\mathbf{X}]_{n,m}$ denotes the $(n,m)$-th element of a matrix.
$(\cdot)^T$ and $(\cdot)^H$ denote the transpose and conjugate transpose, respectively. $\textrm{dist}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}) = \|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y}\|_2$ calculates the Euclidean distance between two coordinates. $\angle \mathbf{X}$ normalizes the amplitude of each element in a matrix (phase-only preservation). $\textrm{diag}(\mathbf{x})$ creates a diagonal matrix from a vector. $\textrm{diagvec}(\mathbf{X})$ extracts diagonal elements of a matrix into a column vector. $\Vert\cdot\Vert_1$ and $\Vert\cdot\Vert_2$ denote the $\ell_1$-norm and $\ell_2$-norm, respectively. Lastly, $\mathcal{CN}(0, \sigma^2)$ represents the circularly symmetric complex Gaussian distribution.
\section{System Model}
As depicted in Fig. \ref{fig:system}, we consider a multi-IRS-assisted beam routing system, where the direct BS-UE link is obstructed, while a virtual LoS link is established via multi-hop reflections over $K \in \mathbb{N}^+$ IRSs. The BS is equipped with $M$ antennas, indexed by $\mathcal{M} = \{1, \cdots, M\}$. The $k$-th IRS is composed of $N_k$ elements, indexed by $\mathcal{K} = \{1, 2, \cdots, k, \cdots, K\}$ and $\mathcal{N}_k = \{1, 2, \cdots, N_k\}$. For simplicity, the index $k$ also equals the hop count from the BS to the $k$-th IRS. 
A single-antenna UE is within the coverage area of the $K$-th IRS\footnote{If there are multiple UEs within the coverage area of the $K$-th IRS, the results of the proposed algorithm can be directly applied to IRS beam training for these multiple UEs.}. 
The control links from the BS to the IRSs and the feedback link from the UE to the BS are assumed to be separate and reliable.
\begin{figure}[t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[scale = 0.13]{LIS/figures/system.png}

 \caption{A Multi-IRS-assisted wireless beam routing system.}
 \label{fig:system}

\end{figure}
To model the near-field LoS channels, the coordinates of the $m$-th BS antenna, $n_k$-th IRS element, and UE are denoted as $\mathbf{c}_m^{\rm{B}}, \mathbf{c}_{n_k}^{\rm{I}}$, and $\mathbf{c}^{\rm{U}}$, respectively, where $m \in \mathcal{M}, n_k \in \mathcal{N}_k, k \in \mathcal{K}$. Consequently, the distance from the $m$-th BS antenna to the $n_1$-th IRS element is given by $d_{n_1,m} = \textrm{dist}(\mathbf{c}_{n_1}^{\rm{I}},\mathbf{c}_m^{\rm{B}})$. The distance from the $n_{k-1}$-th IRS element to the $n_{k}$-th IRS element is $d_{n_{k},n_{k-1}} = \textrm{dist}(\mathbf{c}_{n_{k}}^{\rm{I}},\mathbf{c}_{n_{k-1}}^{\rm{I}})$, where $2\leq k \leq K$. The distance from the $n_K$-th IRS element to the UE is $d_{n_K} = \textrm{dist}(\mathbf{c}_{n_K}^{\rm{I}}, \mathbf{c}^{\rm{U}})$.
\subsection{Signal Model}
Let $\mathbf{H}_1 \in \mathbb{C}^{N_1 \times M}$ represent the channel from the BS to the first IRS, $\mathbf{H}_{k} \in \mathbb{C}^{N_{k}\times N_{k-1}},~2\le k\le K$ represent the channel from the ($k-1$)-th IRS to the $k$-th IRS, and $\mathbf{h}^H \in \mathbb{C}^{1\times N_K}$ represent the channel from the $K$-th IRS to the UE. 
For the wireless beam routing system, we focus on the cascaded LoS link by treating the non-LoS (NLoS) components as part of the environment scattering, which has been shown to have a negligible impact on system performance \cite{cooperative2021, multi-beam-multi-hop2022}.
Therefore, considering only the LoS component, the near-field channel between the BS and the first IRS is modeled as \cite{liuYW2023NFC_Tutorial}
\begin{equation} 
 \label{BS-IRS_channel}
 \begin{split}
 [\mathbf{H}_1]_{n_1,m} = \sqrt{\frac{G^{\rm B}_{n_1,m}
 A_{n_1,m}^{\rm I}}
 {4\pi d^2_{n_1,m}}}e^{j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}d_{n_1,m}},~n_1 \in \mathcal{N}_1, m\in \mathcal{M}, 
 \end{split}
 \end{equation}
where $G^{\rm B}_{n_1,m}$ is the antenna gain from the $m$-th BS antenna to the $n_1$-th IRS element, and $A_{n_1,m}^{\rm I}$ is the effective aperture of the $n_1$-th IRS element relative to the $m$-th BS antenna. Similarly, we have
\begin{equation} 
 \label{inter-IRS_channel}
 \begin{split}
 [\mathbf{H}_k]_{n_{k},n_{k-1}} &= \sqrt{\frac{G^{\rm I}_{n_{k},n_{k-1}}
 A_{n_{k},n_{k-1}}^{\rm I}}
 {4\pi d^2_{n_{k},n_{k-1}}}}e^{j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}d_{n_{k},n_{k-1}}}, 
 \end{split}
 \end{equation}
where $n_{k}\in\mathcal{N}_{k}$, $n_{k-1}\in\mathcal{N}_{k-1}$, $2\le k\le K$. $G^{\rm I}_{n_{k},n_{k-1}}$ is the element gain from the $n_{k-1}$-th IRS element to the $n_{k}$-th IRS element. $A_{n_{k},n_{k-1}}^{\rm I}$ is the effective aperture of the $n_{k}$-th IRS element relative to the $n_{k-1}$-th IRS element. Moreover,
\begin{equation} 
 \label{IRS-UE_channel}
 \begin{split}
 [\mathbf{h}^H]_{n_{K}} &= \sqrt{\frac{G^{\rm I}_{n_K}
 A^{\rm U}_{n_K}}
 {4\pi d^2_{n_K}}}e^{j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}d_{n_K}}, 
 \end{split}
 \end{equation}
where $G^{\rm I}_{n_{K}}$ is the element gain from the $n_{K}$-th IRS element to the UE antenna. $A^{\rm U}_{n_{K}}$ is the effective aperture of the UE antenna relative to the $n_{K}$-th IRS element. 
Based on the above, the received signal at the UE is
\begin{equation} 
 \label{received_signal}
 \begin{split}
 y &= \mathbf{h}^H
 \mathbf{\Phi}_K
 \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1
 \mathbf{H}_1
 \mathbf{w}s+z,
 \end{split}
 \end{equation}
where $s$ is the transmitted symbol, $z \sim \mathcal{C}\mathcal{N}(0, {\sigma^2})$ is the additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN), $\mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{C}^{M\times 1}$ is the BS beamforming vector subject to the power constraint $\Vert\mathbf{w}\Vert_2^2 \leq P_{\rm t}$. $\mathbf{\Phi}_k={\rm{diag}}( e^{j\varphi_{k,1}}, \cdots, e^{j\varphi_{k, N_k}})\in \mathbb{C}^{N_k\times N_k}$ represents the phase shifts introduced by the $k$-th IRS, where $\varphi_{k, n_k} \in [0,2\pi), k\in\mathcal{K}, n_k\in\mathcal{N}_k$. Consequently, the received signal power at the UE is
\begin{equation}
 \label{received-power}
 \begin{aligned}
  P_{\rm r} &= \left|\mathbf{h}^H
 \mathbf{\Phi}_K
 \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1
 \mathbf{H}_1
 \mathbf{w}\right|^2.
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The achievable data rate in bit/second/Hz is calculated by 
\begin{equation}
 \label{achievable-data-rate}
 \begin{aligned}
  R = \log_2(1+\frac{P_{\mathrm{r}}^2}{\sigma^2}).
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Since the BS and IRSs are usually strategically deployed at fixed locations, we assume that the inter-array LoS channels, i.e., $\mathbf{H}_k, k\in \mathcal{K}$, are known \emph{a priori}, which can be obtained from their deployment geometry \cite{liuYW2023NFC_Tutorial}. In contrast, due to mobility of the UE, the channel $\mathbf{h}^H$ is random and unknown. 
\subsection{BT-Based Transmission Protocol}
\label{BT-Based Transmission Protocol}
For practical IRS implementations, codebook-based BT is usually preferred to establish an initial link, especially when the channel dimension is large or the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is low \cite{codebook-IRS-Dai}. Specifically, under the control of the BS, the $K$-th IRS performs beam sweeping based on a predefined codebook, and the UE measures the signal powers of these beams and feeds the index of the selected codeword to the BS. After BT, the BS applies the selected codeword at the $K$-th IRS to establish an initial link for subsequent channel estimation and data transmissions.
During the BT process, only the phase shifts of the $K$-th IRS are adjusted for beam sweeping, while the beamforming vectors of the BS and intermediate IRSs, i.e., $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K-1$, are pre-designed and held fixed \cite{Fast-Near-Field-Beam-Training-IRS, WCNC2024}. 
Therefore, the procedure of the BT-based transmission protocol can be summarized as follows. 
\begin{enumerate}
 \item First, $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K-1$, are optimized and pre-configured based on prior inter-array LoS channel information. 

 \item Second, BT is performed at the $K$-th IRS to determine the optimal codeword for the $K$-th IRS, i.e., $\mathbf{\Phi}_{K}$. 
 \item Third, with $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K$ all fixed, a low-dimensional channel estimation is performed for the equivalent baseband BS-UE channel, followed by data transmission. \end{enumerate}
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
Based on the BT-based communication protocol, this letter aims to develop an efficient joint beamforming design approach for the BS and intermediate IRSs, given the inter-array near-field LoS channels. To this end, a received power maximization problem is formulated as
\begin{equation} 
 \label{BS-beamforming-problem-1}
 \begin{split}
\max_{\mathbf{w},~\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots,K-1}
~&\Vert\mathbf{h}^H
 \mathbf{\Phi}_K
 \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1
 \mathbf{H}_1
 \mathbf{w}\Vert_2^2,
 {\rm s.t.}~ &\Vert\mathbf{w}\Vert_2^2 \leq P_{\rm t},
 ~&\varphi_{k, n_k} \in [0,2\pi), k\in\mathcal{K}, n_k\in\mathcal{N}_k,
 ~&\mathbf{\Phi}_K \in \mathcal{C},
 \end{split}
 \tag{P1}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{C}$ represents the predefined codebook used for BT\footnote{The codebook-based BT has been widely studied in the literature \cite{multi-beam-training-IRS,Fast-Near-Field-Beam-Training-IRS, WCNC2024, 2D_DFT_DA}, so it is not studied in this letter.}. It is evident that the objective function in (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-1}) reaches its maximum when $\Vert\mathbf{w}\Vert_2^2 = P_{\rm t}$. 
According to the BT-based transmission protocol, the optimization of $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K-1$ occurs before the BT at the $K$-th IRS, when both $\mathbf{h}^H$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_K$ are unknown. This renders the optimization of $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K-1$ infeasible.
Consequently, (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-1}) cannot be solved directly.
\subsection{Benchmark Schemes}
\subsubsection{Conventional Angle-based Beamforming Design}
To address (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-1}), prior research \cite{single2multi, cooperative2021, multi-beam-multi-hop2022, MAMP2023} has mostly utilized angle-based beamforming for $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K-1$ assuming far-field channel modeling. In this approach, given the assumed inter-array far-field LoS channels, each array aligns its transmit/reflect beamforming phases with the angle of departure (AoD) directed toward the subsequent array.
However, for multi-IRS wireless beam routing systems, it is necessary to employ near-field channel modeling for accurate channel characterization \cite{liuYW2023NFC_Tutorial}, rendering the existing angle-based beamforming method inapplicable due to channel mismatch. 
\subsubsection{Near-field Beam Focusing}
We come up with an intuitive beamforming design of $\mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots, K-1$, which is named near-field beam focusing. In this approach, given the inter-array near-field LoS channels, each array aligns its transmit/reflect beamforming phases at the center point of the subsequent array. According to the diffraction limit, the signal energy is distributed mainly in the region centered around the focal point \cite{c9}. Thus, intuitively, the total received power tends to be maximized in each array.
\section{Proposed AO-Based Beamforming Design}
An efficient AO-based algorithm is proposed to resolve (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-1}). Specifically, we adopt the uniform amplitude approximation for $\mathbf{h}^H$, which is based on the Fresnel approximation and can adequately address the near-field multiple-input single-output (MISO) channels, as concluded in \cite{liuYW2023NFC_Tutorial, Power-Scaling-Laws-and-Near-Field-Behaviors}. Consequently, we have $[\mathbf{h}^H\mathbf{\Phi}_K]_{n_{K}} = \sqrt{\frac{G^{\rm I} A^{\rm U}} {4\pi d^2_{n_K}}}e^{j(\frac{2\pi d_{n_K}}{\lambda}+\varphi_{K, n_K})}\approx \alpha_K e^{j(\frac{2\pi d_{n_K}}{\lambda}+\varphi_{K, n_K})}$, where $\alpha_K$ represents the uniform amplitude of $\mathbf{h}^H$. Given that $\varphi_{K, n_K}, n_K \in \mathcal{N}_K$ are adjustable within $[0, 2\pi)$, we introduce an auxiliary variable, $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T} \in \mathbb{C}^{1\times N_K}$, and set $\alpha_K \boldsymbol{\psi}^{T} \equiv \mathbf{h}^H \mathbf{\Phi}_K$, where $\boldsymbol{\psi}^T$ is restricted by $\left|[ \boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}]_{n_K}\right| = 1, \forall~n_K\in \mathcal{N}_K$. With this transformation, (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-1}) is transformed into 
\begin{equation} 
 \label{BS-beamforming-problem-2}
 \begin{split}
\max_{\mathbf{w},\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T},\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k=1,2,\cdots,K-1}&
~\Vert \boldsymbol{\psi}^T
 \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1
 \mathbf{H}_1
 \mathbf{w}\Vert_2^2,
 {\rm s.t.}~ &\Vert\mathbf{w}\Vert_2^2 = P_{\rm t},
  ~&\varphi_{k, n_k} \in [0,2\pi), k\in\mathcal{K}, n_k\in\mathcal{N}_k,
~&\left|[\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}]_{n_K}\right| = 1,~n_K\in\mathcal{N}_K,
 \end{split}
 \tag{P2}
\end{equation}
where the intractability caused by the BT-based transmission protocol in (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-1}) is eliminated. However, (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-2}) is still non-convex due to its non-convex objective and unit-modulus constraints. Moreover, high-dimensional variables render exhaustive or heuristic algorithms infeasible because of their prohibitively high complexity. To address this problem, we propose an efficient AO-based algorithm that determines one variable at a time while keeping the others fixed in each iteration, as shown in Algorithm 1.
For the update of $\mathbf{w}$, since ${\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}} \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \mathbf{H}_1 \in \mathbb{C}^{1 \times M}$ and $\mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times 1}$, based on the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, the update of $\mathbf{w}$ should be $\mathbf{\widetilde{w}} \leftarrow \left ({\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}} \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \mathbf{H}_1\right )^H$, plus a normalization represented by $\mathbf{w} \leftarrow \sqrt{P_{\rm t}} \mathbf{\widetilde{w}}/ \Vert \mathbf{\widetilde{w}}\Vert_2$.
For the update of $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k =1,2,\cdots, K-1$, let us take $\mathbf{\Phi}_1$ as an example. Since $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K \cdots \mathbf{\Phi}_{2}\mathbf{H}_2 \in \mathbb{C}^{1 \times N_1}$ and $\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{C}^{N_1 \times 1}$, we have $\boldsymbol{\psi}^T
 \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1
 \mathbf{H}_1
 \mathbf{w} = (\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K \cdots \mathbf{\Phi}_{2}\mathbf{H}_2) \odot (\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T \cdot \textrm{diagvec}(\mathbf{\Phi}_1)$. With this transformation, the update of $\mathbf{\Phi}_1$ should follow $\textrm{diagvec}(\mathbf{\Phi}_1) = \angle\left((\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K \cdots \mathbf{\Phi}_{2}\mathbf{H}_2) \odot (\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T\right)^H$ to align the phases of $(\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K \cdots \mathbf{\Phi}_{2} \mathbf{H}_2) \odot (\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T$, which is
$\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \leftarrow \mathrm{diag}\left(\angle\left((\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K  \cdots \mathbf{\Phi}_{2} \mathbf{H}_2) \odot (\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T\right)^H\right)$. Similar derivations can be applied to obtain the updates for $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k = 2,\cdots, K-1$, which are shown in Steps 10 and 13 of Algorithm 1. 
For the update of $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}$, $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T} \leftarrow \angle \left(\mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \mathbf{H}_1 \mathbf{w}\right )^{H}$ is performed to achieve phase alignment of $\mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \mathbf{H}_1 \mathbf{w}$. 
The above analysis implies that every update in Algorithm 1 is non-decreasing. Therefore, Algorithm 1 is guaranteed to converge monotonically to a local stationary point. However, convergence to the global optimal point cannot be guaranteed due to the non-convexity of the original problem.
\floatname{algorithm}{Algorithm}
\begin{algorithm}[t]
\label{algorithm-1}
	\caption{AO-based beamforming design}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Input}: $\mathbf{H}_k, k\in\mathcal{K}$, number of iterations $I_\mathrm{max}$.
\STATE \textbf{Initialization}: Initialize $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}$, and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k =1,2,\cdots, K-1$, which satisfies unit-modulus constraint for each of their elements. 

\FOR{ $i = 1,2, \cdots, I_\mathrm{max}$}
\STATE \textbf{ // Design the BS beamforming.}
\STATE $\mathbf{\widetilde{w}} \leftarrow \left ({\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}} \mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \mathbf{H}_1\right )^H$
\STATE $\mathbf{w} \leftarrow \sqrt{P_{\rm t}} \mathbf{\widetilde{w}}/ \Vert \mathbf{\widetilde{w}}\Vert_2$
\STATE \textbf{ // Design the IRS beamforming.}
\STATE $\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \leftarrow \mathrm{diag}\left(\angle\left((\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K  \cdots \mathbf{\Phi}_{2}\mathbf{H}_2) \odot (\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T\right)^H\right)$
\FOR{$k = 2, \cdots, K-2$}
\STATE $\mathbf{\Phi}_k  \leftarrow \mathrm{diag}\Big{(}\angle\big{(}(\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_{k+1}\mathbf{H}_{k+1})~\odot$
\STATE $~~~~~~~~(\mathbf{H}_{k}\mathbf{\Phi}_{k-1}\cdots\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T\big{)}^H\Big{)}$
\ENDFOR
\STATE $\mathbf{\Phi}_{K-1} \leftarrow \mathrm{diag}\Big{(}\angle\big{(}(\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}\mathbf{H}_K)~\odot$ 
\STATE $~~~~~~~~~~~(\mathbf{H}_{K-1}\mathbf{\Phi}_{K-2}\cdots\mathbf{H}_1\mathbf{w})^T\big{)}^H\Big{)}$
\STATE \textbf{ // Determine the auxiliary variable.}
\STATE $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T} \leftarrow \angle \left(\mathbf{H}_K\cdots\mathbf{\Phi}_1 \mathbf{H}_1 \mathbf{w}\right )^{H}$
\ENDFOR
\STATE \textbf{Output}: $\mathbf{w}$, and $\mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k =1,2,\cdots, K-1$.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm} 
\subsection{Complexity Analysis}
Without loss of generality, the number of elements in each IRS is uniformly denoted as $N$.
For the proposed Algorithm 1, the complexity is determined by the update of $\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{\Phi}_{k},~k =1,2,\cdots, K-1$, $\boldsymbol{\psi}^{T}$ in each iteration, and the number of iterations $I_{\mathrm{max}}$, which is approximately $\mathcal{O}\left(I_{\mathrm{max}}K^2N^2\right)$. Since the inter-array LoS channels are assumed to be quasi-static, ideally the Algorithm 1 can be performed only once in an offline manner.
\section{Numerical Results}
\label{Numerical Results}
In this section, Monte Carlo simulations are conducted to evaluate the proposed \emph{AO-based beamforming}. Two benchmarks are adopted: 1) \emph{angle-based beamforming}; 2) \emph{beam focusing}. For BT at the $K$-th IRS, the scheme proposed in \cite{2D_DFT_DA}, which involves a two-dimensional (2D) angle-domain BT followed by a one-dimensional (1D) distance-domain BT, is adopted.
 Unless otherwise specified, uniform rectangular arrays (URA) with half-wavelength spacing are considered for BS and IRSs, which are placed parallel and equidistant \cite{liuYW2023NFC_Tutorial}\footnote{The proposed scheme is independent of the channel dimensions, allowing the analytical results to be readily extended to other array geometries.}. The inter-array distance is 5 m, and the UE distance (from the center of the $K$-th IRS to the UE) is 20 m. The other parameters are listed in Table \ref{parameters}. To simulate the unknown NLoS components, the Rician factor is set to 20 dB. 
Based on the transmission protocols in Section \ref{BT-Based Transmission Protocol}, the inter-array LoS channels are used to obtain the beamforming of the BS and intermediate IRSs, while channels including both LoS and NLoS components are used during BT. 
The resulting points are averaged over 100 channel realizations.
