Fig. \ref{Signal-Amplitudes} shows the incident signal amplitudes on the $K$-th IRS.
The total power of the incident signal under angle-based beamforming is very low due to channel mismatch. The incident signal under beam focusing is largely confined within the central region of the array, which aligns with the diffraction limit.
Compared with angle-based beamforming and beam focusing, the proposed AO-based beamforming achieves a larger total incident signal power and a more uniform distribution (meaning greater array gain) of the incident signal power. 
These observations demonstrate that the AO-based scheme can effectively improve both the total incident power and the array gain of the $K$-th IRS.
Fig. \ref{fig:convergence} shows the convergence of Algorithm 1. It is shown that Algorithm 1 converges within 6 iterations for various parameters. Furthermore, the convergence values of the proposed AO-based scheme are significantly higher than those of the benchmark schemes, showing its effectiveness in solving (\ref{BS-beamforming-problem-2}).
Fig. \ref{fig:N-IRS} shows the impact of the number of IRS elements on the received signal power at the UE. 
The number of elements in each IRS is uniformly denoted as $N$, which varies from 169 to 6400, which means that the side length of each IRS varies from 0.13 to 0.8 m. 
Since 3 IRSs are deployed, the CPB gain derived under far-field assumptions is $\mathcal{O}(N^6)$ according to \cite{cooperative2021}, which means that $P_{\rm{r}}$ and $N$ exhibit a log-linear relationship. This is verified in Fig. \ref{fig:N-IRS} when $N$ is approximately less than 400, where far-field assumptions hold. In contrast, as $N$ continues to increase, the logarithmic linear power scaling no longer holds due to the failure of the far-field assumptions. Furthermore, as $N$ approaches infinity, the received signal power will saturate according to the principle of energy conservation \cite{Power-Scaling-Laws-and-Near-Field-Behaviors}.
Furthermore, in the large $N$ regime of Fig. \ref{fig:N-IRS}, the AO-based scheme achieves significant gains, e.g., more than 170\
Besides, we notice that the received signal power is too low to support viable data transmission in the small $N$ regime, e.g., less than -70 dBm when $N<625$. Therefore, multi-IRS beam routing systems should practically operate in the large $N$ regime, where the proposed AO-based scheme can be applied to improve system performance significantly.
Fig. \ref{fig:UE distance} shows the impact of the UE distance on the achievable data rate. The AO-based scheme outperforms the benchmark schemes across different UE distances, since higher received signal power is achieved, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:N-IRS}. For the same reason, the performance of the proposed AO-based scheme distorts more slowly with increasing UE distance, compared to that of the benchmark schemes.
Fig. \ref{fig:Rician factor} shows the impact of the Rician factor on the achievable data rate. To compare beamforming design schemes without the influence of the BT process, we uniformly adjust the Rician factor of all inter-array channels while keeping the Rician factor of $\mathbf{h}^H$ fixed at 20 dB. The proposed AO-based scheme outperforms the benchmark schemes across different Rician factors, showing its effectiveness.
Moreover, as the Rician factor increases, the achievable rates for all schemes increase and then plateau. The increase is because only the inter-array LoS channels are used for the beamforming design, while the random NLoS components are not exploited. The plateau is because when the Rician factor is sufficiently large, the channel gradually converges with the LoS component.
