\section{Simulation Results}
\label{sec:results}
The simulation parameters are listed in Table \ref{tab:sim_parameters}. Unless
otherwise stated, the channel parameters follow~\cite{zuo_joint_2023,gao_joint_2023}.
All results are averaged over independent channel realisations,
with the BD reflection coefficient held at its default value
except where it is swept.
\subsection{Convergence}
\label{subsec:convergence}
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_convergence}
\caption{Convergence of Algorithm~\ref{alg:ao} for three BS antenna and \gls{ris} element configurations, compared with a fixed passive beamforming (PBF) benchmark in which only the active beamforming (ABF) is optimised.}
  \label{fig:convergence}
\end{figure}
Fig.~\ref{fig:convergence} shows the average weighted sum rate versus the
outer AO iteration index $\tau_0$ for three antenna--RIS configurations,
together with a benchmark in which the RIS phases are kept fixed and only the
active beamformers are optimised. The average objective increases and the relative change in the weighted sum
rate falls below the stopping tolerance $\zeta_0$ within approximately $12$
AO iterations in all cases, confirming the convergence behaviour of
Algorithm~\ref{alg:ao}. The results also show that increasing either the
number of BS antennas or the number of RIS elements improves the achievable
weighted sum rate. In particular, increasing $N$ from $20$ to $32$ provides
a clear gain, while increasing $M$ from $8$ to $10$ for $N=32$ gives a
further improvement, since additional BS antennas provide greater active
beamforming gain and additional RIS elements enhance the passive
beamforming gain. The fixed-PBF benchmark settles after a single iteration
at about $23.6$~bps/Hz, below all jointly optimised configurations, which
indicates the gain from optimising the RIS phases. As the benchmark uses
$N=12$, this comparison is indicative rather than like-for-like.
